var e={name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},t=[{slug:`how-much-does-a-dtc-agency-cost`,title:`Ecommerce Marketing Agency Cost: Pricing Models and Real Value`,titleTag:`Ecommerce Marketing Agency Cost Explained | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`Ecommerce marketing agency cost explained: retainer, % of ad spend, performance and hybrid fees, hidden costs, and how to judge value with margin and MER.`,primaryKeyword:`ecommerce marketing agency cost`,secondaryKeywords:[`dtc agency pricing`,`how much does a marketing agency cost`,`agency percentage of ad spend`,`performance marketing agency fees`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Strategy & Scaling`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/performance-marketing-agency`,title:`What a Performance Marketing Agency Does`,text:`The services, metrics and vetting process behind the fee.`},{href:`/compare/agency-vs-in-house-media-buyer`,title:`Agency vs In-House Media Buyer`,text:`Compare the full cost of hiring against outsourcing.`},{href:`/meta-ads-audit`,title:`Meta Ads Audit`,text:`Find out what your account needs before you pay for management.`},{href:`/tools/mer-calculator`,title:`MER Calculator`,text:`Measure blended efficiency before and after an agency starts.`}],author:e,directAnswer:`Ecommerce marketing agency cost depends on the pricing model and the scope of work. Most agencies charge a monthly retainer, a percentage of ad spend, a performance fee or a hybrid. The useful comparison is total cost, including creative and tools, against the extra contribution margin the agency produces for your brand.`,definitionBox:{term:`Total agency cost`,definition:`Everything you pay to have an agency run growth: the management fee, creative production, software and any performance bonus. Ad spend goes to the platforms and is normally billed separately.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`why-cost-varies`,text:`Why agency cost varies so much`},{id:`pricing-models`,text:`The five agency pricing models`},{id:`fee-arithmetic`,text:`Example arithmetic: one brand, four fee structures`},{id:`cost-drivers`,text:`What drives the cost up or down`},{id:`hidden-costs`,text:`Hidden costs founders miss`},{id:`judge-value`,text:`How to judge value with contribution margin and MER`},{id:`questions-to-ask`,text:`Questions to ask before you sign`},{id:`brand-scaling-pricing`,text:`How Brand Scaling prices an engagement`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`why-cost-varies`,h2:`Why ecommerce marketing agency cost varies so much`,content:`Ask five agencies for a quote and you will get five structures that are hard to compare. Ecommerce marketing agency cost is not one number, because agencies sell different things under the same label. One runs your Meta account and nothing else. Another writes creative, edits UGC, rebuilds your product page and runs Klaviyo flows. A third mostly reports on what the platforms already do automatically.

So before you compare fees, compare scope. Write down which channels the agency will manage, how many new ads it will ship each month, who produces the footage, whether store and conversion work is included, and who owns tracking. Two quotes that look far apart can be close once you price in the work one of them leaves for you.

The other reason costs vary is risk. An agency paid only on results carries more risk, so it prices that in, chooses clients carefully, or both. A flat retainer moves the risk to you. Neither is better by default. The right structure is the one that rewards the agency for the outcome you actually care about, which for most DTC brands is profitable new customer growth.`},{id:`pricing-models`,h2:`The five agency pricing models`,content:`Almost every DTC agency uses one of these models or a mix of them. Each one creates a different incentive, and that incentive matters more than the headline fee.`,table:{headers:[`Model`,`How it works`,`Works best when`,`Watch out for`],rows:[[`Monthly retainer`,`Fixed fee for a defined scope`,`Scope is clear and spend is stable`,`Paying the same in slow months; scope creep`],[`Percentage of ad spend`,`Fee rises and falls with spend`,`Spend is growing and the agency drives creative`,`Incentive to raise spend rather than profit`],[`Performance fee`,`Share of revenue or profit above a baseline`,`Tracking is clean and the baseline is agreed`,`Disputes over attribution and baseline`],[`Hybrid`,`Lower base fee plus a percentage or bonus`,`You want shared risk with predictable cost`,`Complex terms that are hard to audit`],[`Project fee`,`One-off price for a build, audit or launch`,`You need a store build, setup or Q4 push`,`No ongoing ownership once the project ends`]]}},{id:`fee-arithmetic`,h2:`Example arithmetic: one brand, four fee structures`,content:`The figures below are an illustration we made up to show how the models behave. They are not market rates and not our prices. Assume a brand spends $40,000 a month on ads and does $150,000 in revenue, of which $30,000 is above an agreed baseline.

Notice what happens when spend doubles. The retainer stays flat, the percentage fee doubles, and the performance fee only grows if revenue above the baseline grows. That is why the percentage model needs a profit guardrail, such as a minimum MER the agency must hold before it raises budgets.`,table:{headers:[`Example structure`,`Fee at $40K spend`,`Fee if spend doubles to $80K`],rows:[[`Retainer of $5,000 a month`,`$5,000`,`$5,000`],[`10% of ad spend`,`$4,000`,`$8,000`],[`10% of revenue above baseline ($30,000)`,`$3,000`,`Depends on new revenue above baseline`],[`$2,500 base plus 5% of ad spend`,`$4,500`,`$6,500`]]}},{id:`cost-drivers`,h2:`What drives the cost up or down`,content:`Agency pricing tracks the hours and skill a brand needs each month. These five factors move it most.`,list:[`Spend level: more spend means more campaigns, more testing and more risk to manage, so fees usually rise with budget even under a retainer.`,`Creative volume: an agency shipping 30 to 50 new ads a month needs strategists, editors and creators. Volume is the most expensive part of the work and usually the most valuable.`,`Number of channels: Meta alone is cheaper to run than Meta plus TikTok, Google Shopping and Performance Max, because each channel needs its own testing and reporting.`,`CRO and store work: product page rebuilds, bundles, post-purchase upsells and speed work add design and development hours.`,`Tracking and data: setting up Conversions API, fixing attribution and building reporting is front-loaded work that often appears as an onboarding fee.`]},{id:`hidden-costs`,h2:`Hidden costs founders miss`,content:`The management fee is rarely the full bill. Budget for these before you sign, and ask the agency which ones are included.`,list:[`Creative production: UGC creator fees, product samples, shipping to creators, editing and usage rights. If the agency only manages ads, you will pay for footage separately.`,`Software: attribution tools, analytics, creative testing tools, Shopify apps for bundles or reviews, and email platform costs that rise with list size.`,`Testing budget: new concepts need spend before they prove themselves. That spend is real money even when a test loses.`,`Onboarding and audits: some agencies charge a setup fee for tracking, account restructuring and strategy.`,`Contract terms: minimum terms and long notice periods are a cost if the fit is wrong.`,`Your team time: approvals, product briefings and weekly calls still need someone on your side.`]},{id:`judge-value`,h2:`How to judge value with contribution margin and MER`,content:`The right question is not whether a fee is high but whether the agency adds more contribution margin than it costs. Contribution margin here means revenue minus product cost, shipping, payment fees, ad spend and marketing costs.

Example only: before the agency, a brand does $120,000 a month on $40,000 of ad spend, an MER of 3.0. Its gross margin after product, shipping and fees is 60%, so contribution is $120,000 x 0.6 = $72,000, minus $40,000 spend, which leaves $32,000.

Three months later it does $160,000 on $45,000 spend, an MER of about 3.56. Contribution is $160,000 x 0.6 = $96,000, minus $45,000 spend, a $5,000 fee and $3,000 of creative, which leaves $43,000. The agency costs $8,000 and adds $11,000 a month, so it pays for itself with room to spare.

Run the same test on any quote. Use MER, total revenue divided by total marketing spend, rather than platform ROAS, because platform numbers can rise while the business stands still.`},{id:`questions-to-ask`,h2:`Questions to ask before you sign`,content:`These questions expose most mismatches before money changes hands.`,list:[`Exactly what is included each month, and what is billed as extra?`,`How many new creatives will you ship monthly, and who films them?`,`Which metric do you report on: platform ROAS, MER or contribution margin?`,`What happens to the fee if spend goes up or down?`,`Who owns the ad account, pixel, creative files and data if we part ways?`,`What is the minimum term and notice period?`,`Who works on my account day to day, and how many accounts do they run?`,`Can I see real case studies from brands at my revenue level?`]},{id:`brand-scaling-pricing`,h2:`How Brand Scaling prices an engagement`,content:`We do not publish a price list, because the right structure depends on your channels, creative needs and unit economics. Every engagement starts with a Brand Growth Audit, a one-on-one review of your ad account, creative, store and margins with Gaurav. After the audit we quote a monthly retainer, a percentage of ad spend, a performance component or a mix, whichever matches the work.

Our partners are usually DTC brands with product-market fit doing roughly $20K to $2M+ a month, spending at least $10K to $20K a month on paid ads. Below that level, a full agency fee is often a large share of the budget, and a focused audit or project can be the better first step.

Whoever you talk to, ask for the quote in writing with the scope attached: channels, creative volume, reporting cadence and who owns which task. That single page makes it far easier to compare agencies fairly and to hold the one you choose to what was promised.`}],faqs:[{question:`Is a percentage of ad spend a fair way to pay an agency?`,answer:`It can be, if there is a guardrail. A percentage fee rewards the agency for growing spend, which is good when growth is profitable and bad when it is not. Tie budget increases to a minimum MER or contribution margin target so the agency only earns more when the business earns more.`},{question:`Should I choose an agency that only charges on performance?`,answer:`Pure performance deals sound safe but depend on clean tracking and an agreed baseline. If attribution is disputed, the relationship suffers. Many brands prefer a hybrid, a modest base fee plus a performance component, which keeps the agency invested without arguments over every attributed sale.`},{question:`Does agency cost include creative production?`,answer:`Often it does not. Some agencies only manage campaigns and expect you to supply footage, while others include strategy, editing and UGC sourcing. Ask how many new ads ship each month, who films them and whether creator fees and usage rights are inside the quote or billed separately.`},{question:`How long before an agency pays for itself?`,answer:`Expect the first weeks to go on tracking, offer and creative foundations, with testing after that. Judge the agency on contribution margin and MER over roughly a quarter rather than on the first month, while watching early signals such as creative output, test velocity and the quality of reporting.`},{question:`Is an agency cheaper than hiring an in-house media buyer?`,answer:`It depends on what you need. One in-house buyer costs a salary plus tools but rarely covers creative strategy, editing, CRO and email. An agency spreads those skills across a team. Compare the full cost of each option against the scope you need, not the salary against the fee.`},{question:`What spend level makes an agency worth it?`,answer:`Agencies make most sense once you have product-market fit and enough budget to test properly. Our partners usually spend at least $10K to $20K a month on paid ads. Below that, the fee can take too large a share of the budget, so an audit or project often comes first.`}]},{slug:`how-to-scale-facebook-ads`,title:`How to Scale Facebook Ads Without Breaking Your CPA`,titleTag:`How to Scale Facebook Ads Profitably | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`How to scale Facebook ads: 20 to 30% budget steps, horizontal scaling, Advantage campaign budget, cost caps and the creative volume that keeps CPA stable.`,primaryKeyword:`how to scale facebook ads`,secondaryKeywords:[`scaling facebook ads`,`vertical vs horizontal scaling`,`facebook ads cost cap`,`scale meta ads budget`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Meta Ads`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/viral-creatives`,title:`Video ad creative scaling`,text:`How we test hooks and angles weekly and rebuild winners before they fatigue.`},{href:`/blog/meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda Explained`,text:`Why creative diversity now decides who wins the auction.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`Have the scaling campaigns and creative pipeline run for you.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-creative-testing`,title:`Meta Ads Creative Testing`,text:`The testing system that feeds new winners into scale.`},{href:`/cases/q4-shaping-up-be-incredible-while-many-slow-down-weekend-ve`,title:`$133,420 in 7 Days at 4.06x`,text:`A Q4 scaling week on $32,857 of spend.`}],author:e,directAnswer:`How to scale Facebook ads profitably: raise budgets on proven campaigns in 20 to 30% steps every few days, expand winners into new audiences, placements and markets, and use cost caps or bid caps to protect CPA. The real lever is creative volume: a steady supply of new winning ads lets budget grow without efficiency collapsing.`,definitionBox:{term:`Vertical vs horizontal scaling`,definition:`Vertical scaling raises the budget on a campaign that already works. Horizontal scaling spreads a winning formula into new campaigns, ads, audiences, placements or countries.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`scale-meta-ads-8-figure-dtc-brand`,text:`How to scale Meta ads for an 8-figure DTC brand`},{id:`when-to-scale`,text:`When you are ready to scale`},{id:`vertical-scaling`,text:`Vertical scaling in 20 to 30% steps`},{id:`horizontal-scaling`,text:`Horizontal scaling`},{id:`advantage-campaign-budget`,text:`CBO and Advantage campaign budget`},{id:`cost-caps-bid-caps`,text:`Cost caps and bid caps`},{id:`creative-volume`,text:`Creative volume is the real lever`},{id:`stop-signals`,text:`Signals to slow down or stop`},{id:`scaling-results`,text:`What scaling looks like on real accounts`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`scale-meta-ads-8-figure-dtc-brand`,h2:`How to scale Meta ads for an 8-figure DTC brand`,content:`To scale Meta ads for an 8-figure DTC brand, keep the account to a few broad or Advantage+ campaigns, raise budgets in steps of about 20% while blended MER and new-customer CAC hold, and put most of the work into creative volume. At that spend the limit is new winning ads and margin, not targeting, so every step is judged on contribution margin in Shopify rather than platform ROAS alone.

At this size Meta already has enough purchase data to find buyers. What stops scale is creative fatigue, offers that cannot absorb higher CPMs, and too many small campaigns splitting the budget. This is the structure behind one DTC client's $376,256 in Meta purchase value from 2,508 purchases between 1 and 25 October 2026.`,list:[`Consolidate: one broad or Advantage+ sales campaign for scale, one cost-capped campaign that protects margin, and catalog retargeting kept to a small share of spend.`,`Feed creative every week: new angles, hooks and formats tested in a separate campaign, with winners moved into the scale campaign.`,`Step budgets instead of jumping them: about 20% at a time, and only while blended MER and new-customer CAC stay inside your limits.`,`Protect the offer: bundles and quantity breaks keep order value high enough to pay for higher CPMs as spend rises.`,`Add channels when Meta saturates: TikTok, Google Shopping and email capture the demand Meta ads create.`]},{id:`when-to-scale`,h2:`When you are ready to scale Facebook ads`,content:`Most guides on how to scale facebook ads start with budget tactics. The better starting point is whether the account deserves more money. Scaling multiplies whatever is already true: a profitable campaign becomes a bigger profitable campaign, and a leaky one loses money faster.

Check these conditions before you add budget. If two or more are missing, fix them first, because no bid strategy will cover a weak offer or broken tracking.`,list:[`CPA has held at or below target for at least a week, not just for a lucky day or two.`,`The campaign has exited the learning phase and delivery is stable.`,`You know your break-even CPA and ROAS from real contribution margin.`,`Conversions API is sending purchase events and match quality is healthy.`,`You have inventory and fulfilment capacity for a higher order volume.`,`At least three or four ads are carrying spend, not one hero ad.`]},{id:`vertical-scaling`,h2:`Vertical scaling: 20 to 30% budget steps`,content:`Vertical scaling means raising the budget on a campaign that is already working. Large jumps can push Meta to find many more buyers overnight, and it reaches less likely ones to do it, so CPA often spikes. Steps of 20 to 30% every two to three days give delivery time to settle while still compounding quickly.

Example arithmetic: a campaign at $500 a day, raised 25% every other day, grows like this.`,table:{headers:[`Day`,`Daily budget`,`Change`],rows:[[`Day 1`,`$500`,`Starting budget`],[`Day 3`,`$625`,`+25%`],[`Day 5`,`$781`,`+25%`],[`Day 7`,`$977`,`+25%`],[`Day 9`,`$1,221`,`+25%`],[`Day 11`,`$1,526`,`+25%`]]},subsections:[{h3:`Rules that keep vertical scaling safe`,content:`Judge each step on at least two or three days of data, not a single afternoon. If CPA rises more than your tolerance after a step, hold the budget instead of cutting it straight away. Cutting and raising repeatedly confuses delivery more than holding steady does.`}]},{id:`horizontal-scaling`,h2:`Horizontal scaling: more ads, audiences and markets`,content:`Vertical scaling eventually hits a ceiling where each extra dollar buys fewer customers. Horizontal scaling raises that ceiling by giving Meta more ways to find buyers.`,list:[`New creative concepts built on the winning angle, aimed at different buyer types.`,`A duplicate of the winning campaign with a different bid strategy, such as a cost cap version next to a highest volume version.`,`New placements, like Reels and Stories, using native vertical edits rather than cropped feed ads.`,`New countries with similar buyers, such as the UK, Canada or Australia, once shipping and pricing are ready.`,`Fresh landing pages or offers, such as a bundle, that open a new reason to buy.`]},{id:`advantage-campaign-budget`,h2:`CBO and Advantage campaign budget`,content:`Meta now calls campaign budget optimization Advantage campaign budget. You set one budget at campaign level and Meta moves money between ad sets toward the cheapest results. For scaling, that is usually what you want: fewer, broader ad sets with enough budget each, rather than many small ones competing for the same people.

Keep the structure simple. A typical scaling account has one Advantage+ sales campaign with the proven ads, one cost-controlled campaign for extra volume, and a separate testing campaign that feeds them. Ad set spend limits can stop Meta from starving a new ad set, but use them lightly, because heavy limits remove the reason to use a campaign budget at all.

When you raise an Advantage campaign budget, the same 20 to 30% rule applies. And because consolidation gives Meta's ranking system more room to choose, the quality and variety of ads inside the campaign matters more than the targeting around them.`},{id:`cost-caps-bid-caps`,h2:`Cost caps and bid caps: scaling with a ceiling`,content:`Highest volume bidding spends the whole budget and accepts whatever CPA the auction gives. That is fine at low spend, but at high spend it lets cost per purchase drift. Cost controls put a ceiling on what you will pay, which is why we add them once a brand moves into its scale phase.`,table:{headers:[`Bid strategy`,`What it controls`,`Use it when`,`Risk`],rows:[[`Highest volume`,`Nothing, spends the full budget`,`Testing and early scaling`,`CPA drifts as spend rises`],[`Cost per result goal (cost cap)`,`Average CPA over time`,`You know your target CPA`,`Under-delivery if the cap is too tight`],[`Bid cap`,`Maximum bid in each auction`,`Peak periods and volatile CPMs`,`Very low spend if set too low`],[`ROAS goal`,`Minimum return on spend`,`Catalogues with mixed prices`,`Volume drops when the goal is ambitious`]]},subsections:[{h3:`Manual bidding in practice`,content:`Set the first cap near your break-even CPA, with a budget several times higher than you expect to spend, then loosen the cap in small steps if delivery is too slow. A jewelry brand we work with drove $54K in 7 days using manual bidding campaigns at 4.43x ROAS.`}]},{id:`creative-volume`,h2:`Creative volume is the real lever`,content:`Budgets and bids decide how fast you spend. Creative decides how far you can go. Every ad has a pool of people it persuades, and as spend rises that pool runs dry: frequency climbs, CTR falls and CPA rises. The only lasting fix is more ads that reach different people for different reasons.

Meta's Andromeda retrieval system makes this stronger. It matches ads to people based on the creative itself, so ten similar ads behave like one, while ten distinct concepts open ten audiences. That is why our partner brands ship 30 to 50+ new creatives and angles a month and move toward 40+ assets a month once they are scaling.

Plan creative ahead of budget. Before each big step up, make sure the testing campaign has produced at least a few new winners ready to join the scaling campaigns.

Variety means more than new hooks on the same video. Mix formats such as UGC, founder clips, demonstrations, static images and carousels, and mix angles such as problem, result, comparison and gift. Each new combination gives Meta another route to a buyer who ignored your previous ads. The creative testing guide linked below covers how to find those winners without wasting budget.`},{id:`stop-signals`,h2:`Signals to slow down or stop`,content:`Scaling is not a one-way street. Pull back or hold when you see these patterns for more than a couple of days.

When a signal appears, diagnose before you cut. Check whether the cause is creative fatigue, a tracking problem, a seasonal rise in CPMs or an issue on the store such as a stock-out or a slow page. Each one has a different fix, and cutting budget only treats the symptom.`,list:[`CPA sits above target for three days in a row after a budget step.`,`Frequency rises while CTR falls on your top ads, a classic sign of creative fatigue.`,`CPMs climb and conversion rate drops at the same time.`,`Platform ROAS holds but blended MER in Shopify falls, meaning Meta is claiming sales it did not cause.`,`The share of new customers in your orders drops, so you are mostly paying to reach existing buyers.`,`Stock or fulfilment cannot keep up with the order volume.`]},{id:`scaling-results`,h2:`What scaling looks like on real accounts`,content:`During Q4, one dropshipping brand generated $133,420 in 7 days at 4.06x ROAS on $32,857 of spend. On a body contouring account, a disciplined scaling rule of thumb produced $19,977 in a day at 3.77x ROAS on $5,294 spend. Neither result came from one clever setting. They came from proven creative, budgets raised in steps and cost controls that kept CPA in range while spend grew.

If you want a second opinion before your next push, a Meta ads audit or a full management engagement can map where your account's ceiling sits and what it will take to raise it.`}],faqs:[{question:`How do you scale Meta ads for an 8-figure DTC brand?`,answer:`Run a few broad or Advantage+ campaigns instead of many small ones, raise budgets about 20% at a time while blended MER and new-customer CAC hold, and test new creative every week so winners replace ads before they fatigue. At that size creative volume and margin limit growth more than targeting does.`},{question:`How many new creatives does an 8-figure brand need on Meta?`,answer:`Enough to replace ads before they fatigue and to keep testing new angles every week. Brand Scaling partners receive 30 to 50 or more new creatives a month, with volume rising as spend grows.`},{question:`Should an 8-figure DTC brand use Advantage+ sales campaigns?`,answer:`Usually yes, as the main scale campaign, because the account has enough purchase data for Meta to find buyers. Pair it with a cost-capped campaign so spend slows down on expensive days instead of buying unprofitable sales.`},{question:`What should you measure when scaling Meta ads past 7 figures?`,answer:`Blended MER, new-customer CAC and contribution margin from Shopify first, platform ROAS second. Platform ROAS over-credits retargeting and existing customers, so it can rise while the business makes less money.`},{question:`How much should I increase my Facebook ads budget at once?`,answer:`Increase it by 20 to 30% every two to three days on campaigns that are already hitting target. Bigger jumps often push Meta to reach less likely buyers too quickly, which raises CPA. If a step pushes CPA past your tolerance, hold the budget for a few days instead of cutting it back immediately.`},{question:`Is horizontal or vertical scaling better?`,answer:`You need both. Vertical scaling is faster and simpler on a proven campaign, but it hits a ceiling as the best audience gets saturated. Horizontal scaling, through new creative concepts, bid strategies, placements and countries, raises that ceiling. Most accounts scale vertically until efficiency slips, then expand horizontally.`},{question:`Do cost caps limit how much I can scale?`,answer:`They limit spend when the auction gets expensive, which is the point. A cost cap set too tight will under-deliver, so start near break-even CPA with a generous budget and loosen it gradually. Many brands run a cost-capped campaign beside a highest volume campaign to get both volume and control.`},{question:`Why does ROAS drop when I scale Facebook ads?`,answer:`At higher spend Meta has to reach people who are less ready to buy, and your best ads get shown to the same people more often. CPA rises as a result. Adding new creative concepts, raising budgets in steps and using cost controls slows that decline, and better offers or AOV can offset it.`},{question:`Should I duplicate winning ad sets to scale?`,answer:`Duplicating can help when you want to test a different bid strategy or budget, but duplicates of the same ads in the same audience compete with each other. Prefer one consolidated campaign with a campaign budget, and use duplicates deliberately, for example a cost-capped copy of a proven campaign.`}]},{slug:`tiktok-shop-strategy`,title:`TikTok Shop Strategy for DTC Brands: Listings to LIVE`,titleTag:`TikTok Shop Strategy for DTC Brands | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`A practical TikTok Shop strategy for DTC brands: listings, pricing, affiliate creators and samples, LIVE selling, Spark Ads, GMV Max and shop operations.`,primaryKeyword:`tiktok shop strategy`,secondaryKeywords:[`tiktok shop affiliate creators`,`gmv max`,`tiktok shop live selling`,`how to sell on tiktok shop`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Growth Channels`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/tiktok-shop-agency`,title:`TikTok Shop Agency`,text:`Have your listings, creators, LIVE and GMV Max managed.`},{href:`/blog/dtc-affiliate-program`,title:`How to Scale a DTC Affiliate Program`,text:`Commission structures and recruiting that carry over to TikTok.`},{href:`/tiktok-ads-agency`,title:`TikTok Ads Agency`,text:`Paid TikTok campaigns and Spark Ads for DTC brands.`},{href:`/compare/meta-ads-vs-tiktok-ads`,title:`Meta Ads vs TikTok Ads`,text:`Where each platform fits in your channel mix.`}],author:e,directAnswer:`A TikTok Shop strategy that works combines search-friendly product listings, pricing built for impulse buying, an affiliate creator program fuelled by free samples, regular LIVE sessions and paid amplification through Spark Ads and GMV Max. Behind it you need reliable fulfilment, because shop performance affects how much reach TikTok gives your products.`,definitionBox:{term:`TikTok Shop`,definition:`TikTok's in-app store, where people buy products straight from videos, LIVE streams and the Shop tab without leaving the app. Sales can come from your own account, affiliate creators or ads.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-strategy-covers`,text:`What a TikTok Shop strategy has to cover`},{id:`listings`,text:`Product listings built for search and the feed`},{id:`pricing`,text:`Pricing and offers`},{id:`affiliate-creators`,text:`Affiliate creators and samples`},{id:`tiktok-live`,text:`Selling on TikTok LIVE`},{id:`spark-ads-gmv-max`,text:`Spark Ads and GMV Max`},{id:`content-cadence`,text:`Content cadence`},{id:`shop-operations`,text:`Operations that protect your shop`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-strategy-covers`,h2:`What a TikTok Shop strategy has to cover`,content:`TikTok Shop is not a storefront you open and wait on. It is a loop: videos and LIVE streams create sales, sales and good reviews improve how TikTok ranks your products, and better ranking brings more creators and more reach. A TikTok Shop strategy has to keep every part of that loop turning at once.

That means six workstreams running in parallel: listings, pricing, affiliate creators, LIVE, paid amplification and operations. Brands that treat it as only a creator channel, or only an ad channel, usually stall, because the weak part of the loop slows everything else down.

Before you start, decide what TikTok Shop is for. For some brands it is a new customer acquisition channel that feeds the Shopify store. For others it is a sales channel in its own right. The answer changes how you price, which products you list and how much margin you can give creators.`},{id:`listings`,h2:`Product listings built for search and the feed`,content:`Shoppers find products in the feed, in the Shop tab and through TikTok search. Your listing has to convert people who arrive from a 15-second video with almost no context.`,list:[`Titles that use the words people search, such as the product type, main benefit and key attribute, rather than your internal product name.`,`A first image that shows the product clearly on a clean background, followed by images of it in use, size references and what is in the box.`,`A short product video on the listing itself, because many shoppers check it before buying.`,`Complete attributes and categories so TikTok can match the product to searches and recommendations.`,`Variants grouped on one listing where it makes sense, so reviews and sales build on a single page.`,`A plan to earn early reviews, such as follow-up messages and sample programs, because a listing with no reviews converts poorly.`],subsections:[{h3:`Choose which products to list first`,content:`Start with one to three hero products that are easy to show on camera, priced for an impulse decision and strong enough on margin to pay creator commission. A focused catalogue concentrates sales and reviews, which helps ranking. Add the rest of the range once the first products have momentum and creators are asking for more.`}]},{id:`pricing`,h2:`Pricing and offers for TikTok Shop`,content:`TikTok Shop rewards impulse purchases, so pricing and offers carry more weight than on a typical Shopify store. But every sale has extra costs: platform fees, affiliate commission and often a promotional discount. Work out the margin before you set the price.

Example arithmetic only, using assumed rates rather than TikTok's actual fee schedule, which you should check for your market: a $40 product with $10 product cost, an assumed 8% platform fee, a 20% affiliate commission and $5 of shipping and packing.`,table:{headers:[`Line`,`Amount`],rows:[[`Selling price`,`$40.00`],[`Product cost`,`-$10.00`],[`Platform fee (assumed 8%)`,`-$3.20`],[`Affiliate commission (20%)`,`-$8.00`],[`Shipping and packing`,`-$5.00`],[`Contribution before ads`,`$13.80`]]},subsections:[{h3:`Offers that suit TikTok Shop`,list:[`Bundles and multi-packs that raise order value and leave room for commission.`,`Limited-time flash deals timed with LIVE sessions.`,`A clear crossed-out price so the deal is obvious within a second of the video.`]}]},{id:`affiliate-creators`,h2:`Affiliate creators and samples`,content:`For most brands, affiliate creators drive the bulk of TikTok Shop volume. Creators add your product to their showcase, post videos with a product link and earn a commission on each sale.

Start with an open collaboration so any eligible creator can promote the product at a set commission. Then run targeted collaborations with higher commissions for creators whose audience and past sales fit. Free samples are the main recruiting tool, so set approval rules: look at a creator's recent shop sales and content quality, not just follower count.

Every creator who gets a sample should also get a short brief: the problem the product solves, three hooks that have worked, what to show on camera and claims to avoid. Track which creators post after receiving a sample and which videos sell, then give your best creators higher commission, early access to new products and paid boosts. The recruiting and commission principles in our DTC affiliate program guide apply here too.`},{id:`tiktok-live`,h2:`Selling on TikTok LIVE`,content:`LIVE lets a host demonstrate the product, answer questions in real time and run deals that only exist during the stream. It suits products that need explaining or showing, such as skincare, gadgets and apparel.

Treat each LIVE as a show with a run-of-show plan: an opening hook, a demonstration, social proof, the offer, and a repeat of the offer every few minutes for people joining late. Pin the product, use flash deals with clear end times and have a second person handling comments. Consistency matters more than length, so a regular schedule your audience can learn beats occasional long streams. Clip the best moments into short videos afterwards so each LIVE also produces content.

Choose the host carefully. A founder or team member who knows the product and enjoys talking to an audience usually beats a scripted presenter. If no one on the team fits, some brands partner with experienced LIVE creators who host sessions for a commission. Review each stream afterwards: which moments drove orders, when viewers dropped and which questions came up repeatedly.`},{id:`spark-ads-gmv-max`,h2:`Spark Ads and GMV Max`,content:`Organic and affiliate content gets the loop started. Paid media makes it bigger. Two tools matter most for Shop sellers.`,table:{headers:[`Tool`,`What it does`,`Best use`],rows:[[`Spark Ads`,`Runs an existing organic or creator post as an ad, keeping its likes, comments and handle`,`Scaling creator videos that already sell organically`],[`GMV Max`,`TikTok's automated Shop campaign that optimises for total gross merchandise value across videos, LIVE and product cards`,`The main paid engine for Shop products once you have content and sales history`]]},subsections:[{h3:`How to feed GMV Max`,content:`GMV Max works with the content it can access, so its results depend on how many good videos exist for each product. Keep authorising strong creator posts, set a realistic return target based on your contribution margin and give the campaign time to learn before judging it. Our GMV Max guide covers ROI targets, Max delivery and what to do when a campaign will not spend.`}]},{id:`content-cadence`,h2:`Content cadence for your brand account and creators`,content:`TikTok rewards consistency, and every video is another way into your products. A workable weekly rhythm looks like this.`,list:[`Brand account: post short videos most days, mixing demonstrations, founder clips, customer questions and reposted creator content.`,`Creators: aim for a steady flow of new affiliate videos every week, refilling samples as creators post.`,`LIVE: hold sessions on a fixed schedule, and clip each one into shorter posts.`,`Paid: review the week's top organic and creator videos every Monday and move the best into Spark Ads or GMV Max.`,`Testing: try new hooks and angles weekly, borrowing what wins on TikTok for your Meta ads and the other way round.`]},{id:`shop-operations`,h2:`Operations that protect your shop`,content:`TikTok scores shops on how well they serve customers, and a weak score can reduce reach and restrict features. Operations is a growth lever, not a back-office task.

Ship within the handling time TikTok requires and upload tracking quickly. Keep inventory synced with Shopify so you never sell stock you do not have. Answer customer messages fast, process returns and refunds cleanly and fix the causes of bad reviews, such as sizing confusion or damaged packaging. Where available, fulfilment by TikTok can take shipping off your plate. Finally, review compliance before you launch: product category rules and claims policies, especially for supplements and skincare, are stricter than many founders expect.`}],faqs:[{question:`How do I get creators to promote my TikTok Shop products?`,answer:`Set up an open collaboration with a competitive commission, then invite well-matched creators to targeted collaborations with free samples and a higher rate. Give each creator a short brief with proven hooks. Creators choose products that sell, so strong listings, good reviews and a clear offer make recruiting much easier.`},{question:`What commission should I offer TikTok Shop affiliates?`,answer:`Work backwards from margin. Calculate contribution after product cost, platform fees, shipping and any discount, then decide how much you can share while still leaving room for ads and profit. Many brands run a base rate for open collaborations and a higher rate for proven creators who consistently drive sales.`},{question:`What is GMV Max on TikTok?`,answer:`GMV Max is TikTok's automated campaign type for Shop sellers. It optimises for total gross merchandise value, choosing between your product videos, LIVE streams and product cards automatically. It performs best with plenty of good content per product and a return target based on your real contribution margin.`},{question:`Do I need to go LIVE to succeed on TikTok Shop?`,answer:`No, but it helps for products that benefit from demonstration and questions. Many shops rely mainly on affiliate videos and ads. If you do go LIVE, a regular schedule, a clear run-of-show and exclusive flash deals matter more than long sessions, and every stream should be clipped into new short videos.`},{question:`Will TikTok Shop take sales away from my Shopify store?`,answer:`Some customers who would have bought on Shopify will buy in-app instead, but TikTok Shop also reaches people who would never visit your site. Track total new customers and blended contribution margin across both channels, and price so that a TikTok Shop sale is still profitable after fees and commission.`}]},{slug:`good-roas-for-ecommerce`,title:`What Is a Good ROAS for Ecommerce? Break-Even by Margin`,titleTag:`What Is a Good ROAS for Ecommerce? | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`What is a good ROAS? It depends on margin. See break-even ROAS by margin, new vs returning targets, platform vs blended ROAS and our published case data.`,primaryKeyword:`what is a good roas`,secondaryKeywords:[`good roas for ecommerce`,`break even roas by margin`,`good roas for facebook ads`,`target roas`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`7 min read`,category:`Metrics & Unit Economics`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/how-to-scale-facebook-ads`,title:`How to scale Meta ads for an 8-figure DTC brand`,text:`Consolidate, step budgets and feed creative so spend grows without CPA blowing up.`},{href:`/blog/what-is-roas`,title:`What Is ROAS?`,text:`The formula, ROAS vs ROI and the basics behind this guide.`},{href:`/benchmarks/ecommerce-roas`,title:`Ecommerce ROAS Benchmarks`,text:`ROAS figures from our published case studies by niche.`},{href:`/tools/break-even-roas-calculator`,title:`Break-Even ROAS Calculator`,text:`Enter your margins to find your own break-even line.`},{href:`/glossary/blended-roas`,title:`Blended ROAS`,text:`Why total revenue over total spend is the number to trust.`}],author:e,directAnswer:`What is a good ROAS? One that clears your break-even point with room for profit. Break-even ROAS equals 1 divided by your contribution margin before ad spend, so a brand with a 50% margin breaks even at 2x while a 30% margin brand needs 3.33x. A good target sits comfortably above that line.`,definitionBox:{term:`Break-even ROAS`,definition:`The ROAS at which ad spend uses up all of the margin on a sale, leaving zero profit. It equals 1 divided by contribution margin before ad spend, written as a decimal.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`good-roas-depends-on-margin`,text:`Why a good ROAS depends on margin`},{id:`break-even-by-margin`,text:`Break-even ROAS by margin`},{id:`new-vs-returning`,text:`New vs returning customers`},{id:`prospecting-vs-retargeting`,text:`Prospecting vs retargeting`},{id:`platform-vs-blended`,text:`Platform ROAS vs blended ROAS`},{id:`published-case-roas`,text:`ROAS in our published case studies`},{id:`when-lower-roas-works`,text:`When a lower ROAS is the right call`},{id:`set-your-target`,text:`How to set your own target`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`good-roas-depends-on-margin`,h2:`Why a good ROAS depends on your margin`,content:`What is a good ROAS? Founders ask it as if there were one number, usually 3x or 4x. But the same 3x ROAS can be excellent for one brand and a slow cash leak for another. The difference is margin.

If you have not worked with the metric before, our guide to what ROAS is covers the formula and the basics. This post focuses on the next question: how high it needs to be for your brand.

ROAS measures revenue per dollar of ad spend. It says nothing about what that revenue costs to deliver. A brand that keeps 70 cents of every dollar after product cost, shipping and payment fees can afford to spend far more to win a sale than a brand that keeps 30 cents. So a good ROAS is not a benchmark you borrow. It is a number you calculate from your own unit economics, then adjust for customer type, campaign type and how you measure.`},{id:`break-even-by-margin`,h2:`Break-even ROAS by margin`,content:`Break-even ROAS is 1 divided by your contribution margin before ad spend. Contribution margin here means what is left from each sale after product cost, shipping, packaging, payment fees and discounts. At break-even, ad spend uses up all of it.

The table below is calculated, not surveyed. The last column shows the ROAS needed to keep 15% of revenue as profit after ads, which is 1 divided by (margin minus 0.15).`,table:{headers:[`Contribution margin`,`Break-even ROAS`,`Margin on a $100 order`,`ROAS for 15% profit after ads`],rows:[[`30%`,`3.33x`,`$30`,`6.67x`],[`40%`,`2.50x`,`$40`,`4.00x`],[`50%`,`2.00x`,`$50`,`2.86x`],[`60%`,`1.67x`,`$60`,`2.22x`],[`70%`,`1.43x`,`$70`,`1.82x`]]},subsections:[{h3:`What the table tells you`,content:`Low-margin brands have very little room. At 30% margin, a 4x ROAS only just covers costs, and real profit needs well over 6x. At 60% margin, 2.5x is already healthy. If your break-even is high, raising margin through bundles, price or product cost often helps more than chasing a higher ROAS.`}]},{id:`new-vs-returning`,h2:`New vs returning customers`,content:`First-order ROAS treats every customer as worth one purchase. If customers come back, you can accept a lower ROAS on the first order and still make money over time.

Example arithmetic only: AOV is $60 and contribution margin is 50%, so each order contributes $30. First-order break-even ROAS is 2x. If customers place an average of 1.8 orders in their first year, each customer contributes $54. You could pay up to $54 to acquire them and break even over the year, which is a first-order ROAS of $60 divided by $54, about 1.11x.

That does not mean you should run at 1.11x. Repeat revenue arrives later, so you fund the gap in cash, and repeat rates are estimates until you have cohort data. Most brands set a first-order target between break-even and the full lifetime figure, and they measure ROAS on new customers separately, because returning customers inflate the blended number.`},{id:`prospecting-vs-retargeting`,h2:`Prospecting vs retargeting ROAS`,content:`Retargeting campaigns almost always report a higher ROAS than prospecting, because they reach people who already visited your store or added to cart. Many of those people would have bought anyway, so the ad gets credit for sales it did not fully create.

Prospecting campaigns find new buyers, so their ROAS looks lower while doing more of the real growth work. If you judge both against one target, you will move budget toward retargeting, the reported ROAS will rise and new customer growth will quietly stall.

Set separate expectations. Hold prospecting to a target based on new-customer economics, keep retargeting budgets small and capped, and judge the whole account on blended results. With broad Advantage+ campaigns mixing both audiences, check the new versus existing customer split in your reporting.`},{id:`platform-vs-blended`,h2:`Platform ROAS vs blended ROAS`,content:`Each ad platform reports the sales it believes it caused, using its own attribution window. When a shopper sees a Meta ad, then searches on Google and buys, both platforms may claim the same order. Platform ROAS added together often overstates the truth.

Example arithmetic only: Meta reports $120,000 in revenue and Google reports $50,000, a combined $170,000 on $40,000 of total spend, or 4.25x. Shopify shows $140,000 in actual revenue. Blended ROAS, also called MER, is $140,000 divided by $40,000, or 3.5x.

Use platform ROAS to compare campaigns and ads inside one platform. Use blended ROAS to decide whether the business is making money and whether to raise total budget. When the two drift apart, trust the one that matches your bank account.`},{id:`published-case-roas`,h2:`What ROAS looks like in our published case studies`,content:`Across 25 published case studies with a stated ROAS, the median is 3.97x. The middle half of results sits between 3.61x and 4.39x, and the full range runs from 2.06x to 11.6x. Treat these as success-biased: brands publish good months, not bad ones, so they show what strong performance looks like rather than an industry average.

Use the figures as context, not as a target. A supplement brand at 3.9x and a dropshipping store at 11.6x tell you little about your own break-even line, because their margins, prices and repeat rates differ from yours. The useful lesson is that strong accounts across very different niches cluster around 3.5x to 4.5x on the campaigns they chose to publish, while their own margins decided whether that was a good result.`,table:{headers:[`Niche`,`Published ROAS range`],rows:[[`Supplements`,`3.89x to 4.51x`],[`Fashion`,`3.56x to 5.49x`],[`Fitness`,`3.61x to 4.37x`],[`Skincare`,`3.64x to 4.39x`],[`Dropshipping`,`2.53x to 11.6x`],[`General DTC`,`2.06x to 3.8x`],[`Jewelry`,`4.43x`]]}},{id:`when-lower-roas-works`,h2:`When a lower ROAS is the right call`,content:`A good ROAS is not always the highest one you can get. Sometimes accepting a lower number is the smarter move, as long as it is a deliberate decision with a time limit.

Launching a new product is one example. Early campaigns are buying data and reviews as well as sales, so a ROAS near break-even can be fine for a few weeks. Q4 is another: customers acquired in November can buy again in January, so brands with strong repeat rates often accept a lower first-order ROAS during the peak. Clearing old stock is a third, because recovering cash can matter more than margin.

The opposite also happens. A ROAS far above target can mean you are under-spending. If every campaign returns well above break-even, you are probably leaving profitable customers on the table, and a controlled budget increase is worth testing. Profit in dollars, not ROAS as a ratio, is what pays the bills.`},{id:`set-your-target`,h2:`How to set your own good ROAS target`,content:`Turn the ideas above into one working number in five steps.

Example arithmetic only: a brand with a $75 AOV keeps $33.75 per order after product cost, shipping, packaging and fees, a 45% margin. Break-even ROAS is 1 divided by 0.45, about 2.22x. To keep 10% of revenue as profit after ads, the target is 1 divided by 0.35, about 2.86x. That brand would treat anything under 2.2x as a loss and anything near 3x as healthy.`,list:[`Calculate contribution margin per order after product cost, shipping, packaging, payment fees and average discount.`,`Find break-even ROAS as 1 divided by that margin.`,`Add the profit you need after ads to get a target ROAS for first orders.`,`Adjust for repeat purchases using real cohort data, not hopes.`,`Track blended ROAS weekly, and raise budgets only while it stays above target.`]}],faqs:[{question:`Is a 2x ROAS good for ecommerce?`,answer:`It depends on margin. A brand with a 60% contribution margin breaks even at 1.67x, so 2x is profitable. A brand with a 40% margin breaks even at 2.5x, so 2x loses money on every first order. Calculate your break-even ROAS as 1 divided by your margin before judging any number.`},{question:`Is a 4x ROAS good?`,answer:`For most DTC brands, yes, but not all. At 50% margin, 4x leaves roughly 25% of revenue as profit after ads. At 30% margin, 4x barely clears break-even of 3.33x. It also depends on whether 4x is platform-reported or blended, since platform figures often overstate results.`},{question:`What is a good ROAS for Facebook ads?`,answer:`A good Facebook ads ROAS is one above your break-even line that holds when checked against blended revenue. Our published case studies have a median of 3.97x, with the middle half between 3.61x and 4.39x, but those are success-biased. Your own margin decides what counts as good for you.`},{question:`Should retargeting have a higher ROAS target than prospecting?`,answer:`Retargeting will naturally report a higher ROAS because it reaches people already close to buying, many of whom would buy anyway. Rather than a higher target, give it a small capped budget and judge it on incremental impact. Hold prospecting to a target based on new-customer economics.`},{question:`How do I calculate break-even ROAS?`,answer:`Divide 1 by your contribution margin before ad spend, written as a decimal. If each $100 order leaves $40 after product cost, shipping, packaging and fees, the margin is 0.4 and break-even ROAS is 2.5x. Our break-even ROAS calculator does the arithmetic from your own numbers.`},{question:`Why is my blended ROAS lower than my Meta ROAS?`,answer:`Meta, Google and other platforms each claim sales using their own attribution windows, so the same order can be counted more than once. Blended ROAS divides actual store revenue by total ad spend, which removes the double counting. A gap is normal, but a widening gap is a warning sign.`}]},{slug:`meta-ads-creative-testing`,title:`Ad Creative Testing for Meta Ads: A Framework That Finds Winners`,titleTag:`Ad Creative Testing Framework for Meta Ads | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`An ad creative testing framework for Meta ads: concept vs iteration tests, one testing campaign, budget per test, kill rules and scaling winners.`,primaryKeyword:`ad creative testing`,secondaryKeywords:[`meta ads creative testing`,`facebook ad creative testing`,`creative testing framework`,`how to test ad creatives`,`ad creative kill rules`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Meta Ads`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/scale-ugc-production`,title:`How to Scale UGC Production`,text:`Produce enough footage to keep the testing pipeline full.`},{href:`/creative-strategy-agency`,title:`Creative Strategy Agency`,text:`Have the angle matrix, briefs and testing run for you.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-library`,title:`Meta Ads Library Guide`,text:`Find proven concepts to test before you brief creators.`},{href:`/blog/how-to-scale-facebook-ads`,title:`How to Scale Facebook Ads`,text:`What to do with winners once they graduate.`}],author:e,directAnswer:`Meta ads creative testing works best as a system: one testing campaign, a fixed budget per test and clear decision rules. Test new concepts first, then iterate on winners. Give each ad 3 to 5 days or 2 to 3 times target CPA in spend, cut ads with CTR under 1% and graduate winners to scale.`,definitionBox:{term:`Creative testing`,definition:`A structured process for launching new ads with a controlled budget, judging them on fixed rules and moving the winners into the campaigns that carry most of your spend.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`why-testing-system`,text:`Why creative testing needs a system`},{id:`concept-vs-iteration`,text:`Concept tests vs iteration tests`},{id:`testing-campaign`,text:`Set up one testing campaign`},{id:`budget-per-test`,text:`Budget per test`},{id:`decision-rules`,text:`Decision rules: kill, keep or graduate`},{id:`what-to-test-first`,text:`What to test first`},{id:`reading-results`,text:`Reading results without fooling yourself`},{id:`graduating-winners`,text:`Graduating winners to scale`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`why-testing-system`,h2:`Why Meta ads creative testing needs a system`,content:`Most ad accounts test creative, but few do it in a way that produces reliable answers. New ads get dropped into scaling campaigns, judged on a day of data and switched off by gut feel. The result is a pile of ads nobody learned anything from.

Meta ads creative testing works when it runs like a production line. New ideas go in at one end with a controlled budget, they are judged on rules agreed in advance, and winners come out the other end ready to scale. Losers still teach you something, because each test is designed to answer a specific question.

This matters more now than it did a few years ago. Targeting is broad, bidding is automated and Meta's Andromeda system matches ads to people based on the creative itself. Creative is the main input you still control, so the speed and accuracy of your testing decides how fast the account grows.`},{id:`concept-vs-iteration`,h2:`Concept tests vs iteration tests`,content:`There are two kinds of test, and mixing them up wastes budget. A concept test asks whether a new idea works at all. An iteration test asks whether a better version of a proven idea works even better.`,table:{headers:[``,`Concept test`,`Iteration test`],rows:[[`Question`,`Does this angle or idea sell?`,`Can we improve a proven ad?`],[`What changes`,`Angle, message, format, creator`,`Hook, first frame, length, caption, CTA`],[`Ads per test`,`3 to 5 genuinely different executions`,`3 to 5 variations of one winner`],[`Typical win size`,`Large but rare`,`Small but frequent`],[`When to run`,`Every week, for new audiences`,`As soon as a concept wins`]]},subsections:[{h3:`A healthy split`,content:`Keep both running every week. If you only iterate, you squeeze more out of a shrinking audience. If you only test concepts, you never get the most out of the ones that work. A balance of roughly half new concepts and half iterations is a sensible starting point for most accounts.`}]},{id:`testing-campaign`,h2:`Set up one testing campaign`,content:`Keep all tests in a single dedicated campaign, separate from the campaigns that carry most of your spend. That stops new ads stealing budget from proven ones, and it keeps the comparison fair.

A simple structure: one sales campaign optimised for purchases, broad targeting, and one ad set per concept with three to five ads inside. Use ad set budgets, or a campaign budget with minimum spend per ad set, so each concept gets enough money to be judged. Keep the optimisation event, landing page and offer the same across tests unless the offer is the thing you are testing.

Name every ad with the concept, angle, hook and format so results can be sorted later. Launch new tests on a fixed day each week, so the account always has a fresh batch moving through.

Keep a simple test log alongside the account: the date, the hypothesis, the ads, the spend and the decision. After a few months the log becomes your most useful creative asset, because it shows which angles, hooks and formats work for your buyers and which ones you can stop briefing.`},{id:`budget-per-test`,h2:`How much budget each test needs`,content:`Each ad needs enough spend to show whether it can win a purchase at your target cost. A practical rule is 2 to 3 times your target CPA per ad. Example arithmetic only, for a brand with a $40 target CPA.`,table:{headers:[`Item`,`Calculation`,`Budget`],rows:[[`Per ad`,`$40 x 2 to 3`,`$80 to $120`],[`Per concept (4 ads)`,`4 x $80 to $120`,`$320 to $480`],[`Per week (5 concepts)`,`5 x $320 to $480`,`$1,600 to $2,400`]]},subsections:[{h3:`If the budget is tight`,content:`Test fewer concepts with fewer ads each rather than starving every ad. Two well-funded concepts a week teach you more than six underfunded ones. As spend grows, increase how many concepts you test, not how much each ad gets.`}]},{id:`decision-rules`,h2:`Decision rules: when to kill, keep or graduate`,content:`Agree the rules before launch so nobody decides on mood. These are the rules we start from, adjusted to each brand's margins.`,list:[`Give every ad 3 to 5 days or 2 to 3 times target CPA in spend, whichever comes first.`,`Kill early if link CTR stays under 1% after spending about one CPA, because people are not interested enough to click.`,`Kill if the ad spends 2 times target CPA with no purchase.`,`Keep testing if CPA is close to target but the sample is small.`,`Graduate if CPA is at or below target after the full test budget, with at least a few purchases.`,`Check hook rate and hold rate on losing videos to see whether the opening or the body failed.`]},{id:`what-to-test-first`,h2:`What to test first: hooks, angles and formats`,content:`Test in order of impact. The angle decides who the ad speaks to, the hook decides whether they stop scrolling, and the format decides how the message is delivered.`,subsections:[{h3:`Angles`,content:`An angle is the reason to buy: a problem solved, a result, a comparison, a price argument or a gift. Test angles first, because a new angle can reach a new group of buyers. Customer reviews, support tickets and competitor ads in the Meta Ads Library are the best sources.`},{h3:`Hooks`,content:`Once an angle works, test different first three seconds: a bold claim, a question, a visual demonstration or a pattern interrupt. Hooks are cheap to produce because you can re-edit the same body footage.`},{h3:`Formats`,content:`Then try the winning message as UGC, founder video, static image, carousel or a demo. We map this as a 45-cell matrix of 5 angles, 3 hook styles and 3 formats, which keeps testing organised rather than random.`}]},{id:`reading-results`,h2:`Reading results without fooling yourself`,content:`Small samples mislead. An ad with three purchases and an 8x ROAS has not proven anything yet, and an ad with no purchases after half a CPA has not failed. Read results in order: did people stop (hook rate), did they watch (hold rate), did they click (CTR), did they buy (CPA and conversion rate).

That order tells you what to fix. A strong hook with weak clicks suggests the message or offer is not landing. Strong clicks with weak purchases point to the landing page, price or a mismatch between ad and page. Compare each test against your current best ad rather than against zero, and look for patterns across tests, such as one angle winning in several formats, which is worth more than any single result.

Finally, check results by placement and age range before drawing conclusions. A video that fails in Feed can win in Reels, and an angle that misses younger buyers can land with older ones. Those breakdowns often turn a loser into a brief for the next test.`},{id:`graduating-winners`,h2:`Graduating winners to scale`,content:`When an ad passes, move it into your scaling campaigns using the same post ID, so the likes and comments it has earned come with it. Add it alongside existing winners rather than replacing them, and let the campaign budget decide how much it gets.

Then put the winner straight back into testing as the base for iterations: new hooks, new creators, new lengths. Most of the long-term value of a winning concept comes from the variations built on it. Our partner brands ship 30 to 50+ new creatives and angles a month, and that volume only works because the testing system tells the team which ideas deserve more versions.`}],faqs:[{question:`How long should I run a Meta ads creative test?`,answer:`Run each ad for 3 to 5 days or until it has spent 2 to 3 times your target CPA, whichever comes first. Shorter tests rarely collect enough purchases to judge. Cut clear losers early, such as ads with link CTR under 1% after about one CPA of spend.`},{question:`Should I test creatives in a separate campaign?`,answer:`Yes. A dedicated testing campaign stops new ads taking budget from proven winners and makes comparisons fair. Keep the optimisation event, audience and landing page constant, give each concept its own ad set and budget, and move winners into scaling campaigns using their original post ID.`},{question:`How many creatives should I test each week?`,answer:`Test as many as your budget can fund properly. At 2 to 3 times target CPA per ad, a $40 CPA brand needs $80 to $120 per ad. Two or three well-funded concepts a week beat many underfunded ones. As spend grows, add more concepts rather than more budget per ad.`},{question:`What should I test first, the hook or the angle?`,answer:`Start with the angle, because it decides who the ad speaks to and can open a new audience. Once an angle wins, test hooks on it, since new openings are cheap to edit from the same footage. Then test the winning combination across formats such as UGC, static images and demos.`},{question:`Can I use ROAS to judge creative tests?`,answer:`Use it carefully. Early ROAS swings wildly with just a few purchases. Judge tests on CPA against target once the ad has spent enough, and use hook rate, hold rate and CTR to understand why an ad won or lost. Confirm winners on blended results after they scale.`}]},{slug:`shopify-conversion-rate-optimization`,title:`Shopify CRO Tips: A 10-Point Conversion Checklist`,titleTag:`Shopify CRO Tips: A 10-Point Checklist | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`Shopify CRO tips in one checklist: speed, above the fold, product pages, reviews, bundles, cart drawer, checkout, post-purchase, mobile and testing.`,primaryKeyword:`shopify cro tips`,secondaryKeywords:[`shopify conversion rate optimization`,`increase shopify conversion rate`,`shopify product page optimization`,`shopify checkout optimization`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`7 min read`,category:`Shopify & CRO`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/shopify-agency`,title:`Shopify Agency`,text:`The store build behind a 5.25% conversion rate and a $61,000 month.`},{href:`/cro-agency`,title:`Ecommerce CRO Agency`,text:`Have this checklist audited and implemented on your store.`},{href:`/tools/conversion-rate-calculator`,title:`Conversion Rate Calculator`,text:`See what a lift in conversion rate is worth in revenue.`},{href:`/tools/aov-calculator`,title:`AOV Calculator`,text:`Model how bundles and upsells change order value.`}],author:e,directAnswer:`The Shopify CRO tips that move revenue most are fast mobile load times, a clear offer above the fold, product pages that answer objections, visible reviews, bundles that lift order value, a cart drawer with upsells, a short checkout, post-purchase offers and a habit of testing one change at a time.`,definitionBox:{term:`Conversion rate optimization (CRO)`,definition:`The practice of increasing the share of visitors who buy, and how much they spend, by improving your store's speed, pages, offers and checkout based on data rather than opinion.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`cro-checklist`,text:`The 10-point checklist at a glance`},{id:`speed`,text:`Speed`},{id:`above-the-fold-product-page`,text:`Above the fold and the product page`},{id:`reviews-offers-bundles`,text:`Reviews, offers and bundles`},{id:`cart-drawer-checkout`,text:`Cart drawer and checkout`},{id:`post-purchase-mobile`,text:`Post-purchase and mobile`},{id:`testing-changes`,text:`Testing changes the right way`},{id:`cro-proof`,text:`What this looks like on a real store`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`cro-checklist`,h2:`The 10-point Shopify CRO checklist at a glance`,content:`These Shopify CRO tips are ordered the way a shopper moves through your store, from the first second of loading to the thank-you page. Work through them in order, because a fix early in the journey affects every step after it. If your traffic comes mainly from paid social, most of it lands on mobile product pages, so start there.

Before changing anything, record your baseline for a few weeks: conversion rate by device and traffic source, add to cart rate, checkout completion rate, AOV and revenue per session. Without a baseline you cannot tell whether a change helped, and you risk keeping changes that only looked good during a strong sales week.`,table:{headers:[`Area`,`What good looks like`],rows:[[`1. Speed`,`Pages load fast on a mid-range phone on mobile data`],[`2. Above the fold`,`Product, benefit, price, reviews and add to cart visible without scrolling`],[`3. Product page`,`Answers the top objections before the shopper has to ask`],[`4. Reviews`,`Star rating near the title, photo reviews lower down`],[`5. Offers and bundles`,`A clear reason to buy more than one`],[`6. Cart drawer`,`Opens on add to cart, shows progress to free shipping and one relevant add-on`],[`7. Checkout`,`Express payment options and no surprise costs`],[`8. Post-purchase`,`One-click upsell and a confirmation that builds trust`],[`9. Mobile`,`Thumb-friendly buttons and a sticky add to cart`],[`10. Testing`,`One change at a time, judged on real orders`]]}},{id:`speed`,h2:`Speed: the fix that helps every page`,content:`A slow store loses shoppers before they see the product, and paid traffic is the least patient traffic you have. Test your key pages in PageSpeed Insights on mobile, and look at real-user data where it exists rather than desktop scores.`,list:[`Remove apps you no longer use, because many leave scripts behind even after you stop using them.`,`Compress images, serve modern formats and avoid giant hero videos above the fold.`,`Load reviews, chat widgets and tracking scripts after the main content where possible.`,`Use a lightweight, well-maintained theme rather than stacking customisations on an old one.`,`Limit custom fonts to one or two weights.`],subsections:[{h3:`Check speed after every app install`,content:`Speed decays slowly as apps, sections and scripts pile up. Re-test your main product page whenever you add an app or a new section, and keep a short list of every script on the store and why it is there. Anything nobody can explain is a candidate for removal.`}]},{id:`above-the-fold-product-page`,h2:`Above the fold and the product page`,content:`On a phone, the first screen decides whether someone scrolls or leaves. Show the product clearly, a one-line benefit, the price, the star rating and the add to cart button without any scrolling. Match the headline to the ad that sent the visitor, so the promise in the ad continues on the page.

Below the fold, the product page has one job: answer every objection before the shopper has to ask. List the questions customers send your support team and answer them on the page.`,list:[`Who it is for and the result they can expect, in plain language.`,`How to use it, with a short video or image sequence.`,`Ingredients, materials, sizing or specifications, with a size guide where relevant.`,`Shipping times, returns and guarantee close to the add to cart button.`,`A comparison with the alternatives the shopper is weighing up.`,`An FAQ section built from real customer questions.`]},{id:`reviews-offers-bundles`,h2:`Reviews, offers and bundles`,content:`Reviews do the selling a salesperson would do in a shop. Put the star rating and review count next to the product title, then show photo and video reviews further down, ideally filterable by concern, size or skin type. Ask for reviews in your post-purchase emails so the count keeps growing.

Offers and bundles raise order value, which means each visitor is worth more even if conversion rate stays the same. Quantity breaks such as buy two, save more suit consumables. Curated bundles suit routines and sets. A free gift above a spend threshold works well when the gift costs little but feels valuable. Show the saving clearly in money, not just as a percentage, and make the bundle the default choice where it suits most buyers.

Check margin before you launch any offer. A bundle that lifts AOV but gives away most of the profit on each order can raise revenue while lowering contribution margin. Work out the profit on each bundle option at its discounted price, and design the ladder so the option you want most buyers to choose is also the one that earns you the most.`},{id:`cart-drawer-checkout`,h2:`Cart drawer and checkout`,content:`A cart drawer that slides open on add to cart keeps shoppers on the page and moving toward checkout. Use it well.`,list:[`Show a progress bar toward free shipping, set just above your current AOV.`,`Offer one relevant add-on, such as a refill or accessory, not a wall of products.`,`Repeat the guarantee and delivery estimate.`,`Make the checkout button the most obvious element in the drawer.`],subsections:[{h3:`Checkout`,content:`Shopify's checkout is already well optimised, so focus on what you control. Turn on express payment options such as Shop Pay, Apple Pay and Google Pay. Show shipping costs before checkout so there are no surprises. Keep discount code fields from sending shoppers off to hunt for codes, for example by applying offers automatically.`}]},{id:`post-purchase-mobile`,h2:`Post-purchase and mobile`,content:`The moment after purchase is the easiest time to sell more, because the customer has just decided to trust you. A one-click post-purchase upsell, shown before the thank-you page, adds revenue without touching conversion rate. Offer something that complements what they bought, at a modest discount, with no need to re-enter payment details. Then use the confirmation page and emails to set delivery expectations and reduce buyer's remorse.

Most paid social traffic is mobile, so review every step above on a real phone, not a desktop preview. Make buttons large enough for a thumb, add a sticky add to cart bar that appears on scroll, keep menus short, and check that pop-ups do not cover the product on small screens. Put a timer on your own checkout run: if it feels slow to you, it feels slower to a stranger.`},{id:`testing-changes`,h2:`Testing changes the right way`,content:`Change one thing at a time and measure it on real orders, not clicks. If traffic is high enough, run a proper A/B test. If it is not, make one change, keep traffic sources steady and compare a few weeks before and after, knowing the result is directional.

Small lifts are worth chasing. Example arithmetic only: a store with 20,000 monthly sessions and a $70 AOV.`,table:{headers:[`Conversion rate`,`Orders`,`Revenue`],rows:[[`2.5%`,`500`,`$35,000`],[`3.0%`,`600`,`$42,000`],[`3.5%`,`700`,`$49,000`]]},subsections:[{h3:`Why this matters for ads`,content:`In that example, half a point of conversion rate adds $7,000 a month with no extra ad spend. It also lowers CPA on every campaign, which lets you scale budgets further before you hit your break-even ROAS.`}]},{id:`cro-proof`,h2:`What this looks like on a real store`,content:`When we rebuilt a clinical skincare store around this checklist, focusing on speed, product page structure, reviews and bundles, it reached a 5.25% store conversion rate and a $61,000 month. The work was not a redesign for its own sake. Each change answered a specific question: why do visitors leave, what do they need to see, and what would make them buy more than one.

Run through the ten points on your own store this week. Most brands find two or three gaps they can close quickly, and those quick fixes often matter more than any new campaign.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is a good conversion rate for a Shopify store?`,answer:`It varies with traffic source, price point and product, so compare against your own history rather than a general number. Paid social traffic usually converts lower than email or returning visitors. Track conversion rate by source and device, then judge each change by whether it lifts revenue per session.`},{question:`Which Shopify CRO tip should I start with?`,answer:`Start with speed and the mobile product page, because most paid traffic lands there and every later step depends on it. Make sure the product, benefit, price, reviews and add to cart button are visible on the first screen of a phone, then work down the checklist in order.`},{question:`Do bundles really increase Shopify revenue?`,answer:`They often raise average order value, which makes each visitor worth more even if conversion rate stays flat. Quantity breaks suit consumables and curated sets suit routines. Show the saving in money, make the bundle easy to choose and check that margin still works after the discount.`},{question:`Are post-purchase upsells worth adding?`,answer:`Usually, yes. A one-click offer after payment adds revenue without risking the main conversion, because the customer has already bought. Keep it relevant to the first purchase, price it sensibly and avoid asking for payment details again. Measure take rate and any change in refunds.`},{question:`How do I A/B test on Shopify with low traffic?`,answer:`With low traffic, a formal A/B test can take too long to reach a reliable answer. Make one change at a time, hold traffic sources steady and compare several weeks before and after. Prioritise changes with a clear reason behind them, such as answering a frequent customer question.`}]}],n={name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},r=[{slug:`ecommerce-email-flows`,title:`Ecommerce Email Flows: The 8 Klaviyo Flows Every DTC Brand Needs`,titleTag:`Ecommerce Email Flows: The Klaviyo Setup | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`The ecommerce email flows every Shopify brand needs in Klaviyo: timing, email counts, SMS, segmentation and how to measure flow revenue honestly.`,primaryKeyword:`ecommerce email flows`,secondaryKeywords:[`klaviyo flows`,`abandoned cart email flow`,`welcome email series`,`post purchase email flow`,`win back email flow`,`email and sms flows`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Retention`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/klaviyo-email-sms-agency`,title:`Klaviyo Email & SMS Agency`,text:`Have the full flow stack built, written and tested for your store.`},{href:`/retention-marketing-agency`,title:`Retention Marketing Agency`,text:`Flows, campaigns and offers that raise repeat purchase rate.`},{href:`/glossary/ltv`,title:`What Is LTV?`,text:`The metric your post-purchase and win-back flows exist to grow.`},{href:`/blog/facebook-ads-for-shopify`,title:`Facebook Ads for Shopify`,text:`The paid traffic setup that fills the top of your flows.`}],author:n,directAnswer:`Ecommerce email flows are automated Klaviyo sequences triggered by what a shopper does: signing up, browsing, abandoning a cart or checkout, buying, running low or going quiet. The core eight are welcome, browse abandonment, cart and checkout abandonment, post-purchase, cross-sell, replenishment, win-back and sunset, each with SMS added where intent is highest.`,definitionBox:{term:`Email flow`,definition:`An automated series of emails (and often SMS) sent when a subscriber triggers an event or joins a segment, such as starting checkout or not buying for 90 days. Unlike campaigns, flows run every day without anyone pressing send.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-are-email-flows`,text:`What are ecommerce email flows?`},{id:`core-flows-table`,text:`The core flows, timing and email count`},{id:`pre-purchase-flows`,text:`Welcome, browse, cart and checkout flows`},{id:`post-purchase-flows`,text:`Post-purchase, cross-sell and replenishment`},{id:`win-back-sunset`,text:`Win-back and sunset flows`},{id:`sms-alongside-email`,text:`Where SMS fits alongside email`},{id:`flow-segmentation`,text:`Segmentation inside your flows`},{id:`measuring-flow-revenue`,text:`How to measure flow revenue`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-are-email-flows`,h2:`What are ecommerce email flows?`,content:`Ecommerce email flows are the automated messages that go out when a shopper does something on your store: joins your list, looks at a product, leaves a cart, places an order or stops buying. In Klaviyo each flow starts from a trigger (a list, a segment, a metric such as Started Checkout, or a date property) and then runs a fixed sequence of emails, SMS, delays and conditional splits.

Flows matter more than campaigns for most DTC brands because they talk to people at the moment their intent is highest, and they keep working while the team is busy with launches. Every customer you pay Meta or TikTok to acquire passes through them. When the flows are thin, a large share of the traffic you bought leaves without a second touch.

This guide covers the eight flows we build first, how many messages each needs, when SMS earns its place, and how to read flow revenue without fooling yourself.`},{id:`core-flows-table`,h2:`The core flows, timing and email count`,content:`These are the starting points we use. Treat the timing as a default to test against, not a rule. A low-priced consumable buys faster than a considered purchase like furniture or jewelry, so the delays stretch as price and consideration go up.`,table:{headers:[`Flow`,`Trigger`,`Emails`,`Typical timing`],rows:[[`Welcome`,`Joins list or signup form`,`3 to 5`,`Immediately, then day 1, 3, 5 and 7`],[`Browse abandonment`,`Viewed product, no cart`,`2 to 3`,`2 to 4 hours, then day 1 and day 3`],[`Cart abandonment`,`Added to cart, no checkout`,`2 to 3`,`1 to 4 hours, then day 1`],[`Checkout abandonment`,`Started checkout, no order`,`3`,`1 hour, 24 hours, 48 to 72 hours`],[`Post-purchase`,`Placed order`,`3 to 5`,`Order day, shipping, delivery, day 14`],[`Cross-sell`,`Order of product A`,`2 to 3`,`10 to 30 days after delivery`],[`Replenishment`,`Expected run-out date`,`2 to 3`,`5 to 7 days before run-out`],[`Win-back`,`No order in 60 to 180 days`,`3 to 4`,`Spread over 2 to 3 weeks`],[`Sunset`,`No opens or clicks in 90 to 180 days`,`1 to 2`,`Over one week, then suppress`]]}},{id:`pre-purchase-flows`,h2:`Welcome, browse, cart and checkout flows`,content:`The pre-purchase flows turn interest into a first order. Build them in order of intent: checkout first, then cart, then welcome, then browse.`,subsections:[{h3:`Welcome series`,content:`The welcome series does the job your product page could not finish. Email one delivers whatever the signup promised, usually a first-order offer, within minutes. The next emails explain why the product works, answer the top three objections from your reviews and support tickets, and show proof. Split the flow on Placed Order so buyers exit into post-purchase instead of receiving a discount they no longer need.`},{h3:`Browse abandonment`,content:`Browse abandonment only fires for identified visitors, so its size depends on how many subscribers you have. Keep it light: the product they viewed, two or three reviews and related items. A discount here trains people to browse and wait, so save incentives for later stages.`},{h3:`Cart and checkout abandonment`,content:`Run these as separate flows with flow filters so a shopper never receives both. Checkout abandoners are closer to buying, so they get the stronger sequence: a plain reminder, then objection handling (shipping times, returns, guarantee), then an incentive in the last message only if your margins allow it. Show the exact items left behind with a link back to a restored checkout.`}]},{id:`post-purchase-flows`,h2:`Post-purchase, cross-sell and replenishment`,content:`After the first order the job changes from conversion to lifetime value. This is where most stores leave the most money, because a second purchase costs far less to win than a new customer.

The post-purchase flow reduces regret and refunds: confirm the order, set delivery expectations, send how-to-use content timed to delivery, then ask for a review once the customer has had time to see results. Split first-time buyers from repeat buyers, since a fifth-time customer does not need onboarding.

The cross-sell flow recommends the product most often bought second. Pull this from your order data rather than guessing. A cleanser buyer might get the moisturizer, a hoodie buyer the matching joggers.

Replenishment only works for consumables. Set the delay from the real usage period of each SKU (a 30-day supply gets a reminder around day 23 to 25) and offer a subscription option in the final message.`,list:[`Exit anyone who reorders from replenishment and cross-sell flows immediately.`,`Use product-level conditional splits so each buyer sees the right next product.`,`Keep review requests out of the first week for products that take time to show results.`]},{id:`win-back-sunset`,h2:`Win-back and sunset flows`,content:`A win-back flow targets customers who have bought before but have gone past your normal repurchase window. Find that window from your own data: look at the typical gap between first and second orders and trigger the flow a little after it. Open with what is new, follow with social proof, and hold any offer for the third or fourth message.

The sunset flow protects deliverability. Subscribers who have not opened or clicked in months drag down inbox placement for everyone else. Send one or two "do you still want to hear from us" messages, then suppress anyone who does not engage. Your list gets smaller, but your campaigns land in the inbox more often, which is worth more than the raw count.`},{id:`sms-alongside-email`,h2:`Where SMS fits alongside email`,content:`SMS costs more per message and feels more personal, so use it where timing matters most rather than copying every email into a text. Collect SMS consent separately and in line with TCPA and carrier rules, and always include opt-out language.`,list:[`Checkout abandonment: one SMS between the first and second email often adds the most.`,`Welcome: a single text confirming the signup offer code.`,`Shipping and delivery updates inside post-purchase.`,`Replenishment: a short reminder with a direct reorder link.`,`Skip SMS in browse abandonment and sunset flows, where intent is low.`]},{id:`flow-segmentation`,h2:`Segmentation inside your flows`,content:`The same flow performs better when it splits people by what you know about them. Start with three splits and add more only when the data shows a difference.

Customer status: has the person ordered before? Prospects and customers need different messages in welcome and abandonment flows.

Product or category: what did they view or buy? This drives cross-sell, replenishment and the content in post-purchase.

Engagement and value: recent openers and high-spend customers can receive more messages and early access; low-engagement profiles get fewer and eventually enter the sunset flow.

Use the same segments in your paid media. Customer lists exported from Klaviyo feed exclusions and lookalike seed audiences on Meta, which keeps ad spend focused on new customers.`},{id:`measuring-flow-revenue`,h2:`How to measure flow revenue`,content:`Klaviyo attributes revenue to a flow when a recipient orders within its attribution window after opening or clicking. That number is useful for comparing flows with each other, but it overstates what the flows actually caused, because some of those buyers would have ordered anyway.

Read flow performance on three levels. First, revenue per recipient for each flow and each message, which shows where to rewrite. Second, the share of total store revenue that comes from flows over time. Third, and most honest, a holdout test: keep a small random group out of a flow for a few weeks and compare their order rate with the group that received it. The gap is the flow's real lift.

Then connect it to your unit economics. Better flows raise repeat purchase rate and LTV, which raises how much you can afford to pay for a new customer on paid social. That is the real return on email work: it widens the room your ads have to scale.`}],faqs:[{question:`What are the most important ecommerce email flows?`,answer:`Start with checkout and cart abandonment, then the welcome series, then post-purchase. Those four catch shoppers at the highest-intent moments and usually produce most flow revenue. Add browse abandonment, cross-sell, replenishment, win-back and sunset once the first four are live, tested and segmented by customer status.`},{question:`How many emails should an abandoned cart flow have?`,answer:`Two to three emails is the usual range for cart abandonment and three for checkout abandonment. Send the first within one to four hours, the second about a day later and an optional third with an incentive. More messages rarely add orders and they increase unsubscribes, so test removing emails as well as adding them.`},{question:`Should I offer a discount in abandoned cart emails?`,answer:`Only in the last message, and only if your margins can carry it. Leading with a discount teaches shoppers to abandon carts on purpose. Try objection handling first: shipping times, return policy, guarantees and reviews. If you do add an offer, use a unique code that expires so it cannot spread to coupon sites.`},{question:`Do I need SMS as well as email flows?`,answer:`Not to start, but SMS often adds orders in time-sensitive flows such as checkout abandonment and shipping updates. Collect consent separately, keep texts short and limit them to high-intent moments. Copying every email into SMS raises costs and opt-outs without a matching lift in revenue.`},{question:`How much revenue should come from email flows?`,answer:`There is no single right share; it depends on list size, repeat purchase behaviour and how much paid traffic you run. Track the trend in your own account instead and use holdout tests to measure true lift. Attributed flow revenue in Klaviyo overstates impact because some recipients would have bought anyway.`},{question:`How often should I update my Klaviyo flows?`,answer:`Review flow metrics monthly and rewrite the weakest message in each flow every quarter. Update immediately when offers, shipping times, products or brand positioning change. Run one A/B test per flow at a time, such as subject line or offer, and give it enough recipients to reach a clear result.`}]},{slug:`q4-ecommerce-ads-strategy`,title:`Q4 Ad Strategy for Ecommerce: October to January, Month by Month`,titleTag:`Q4 Ad Strategy for Ecommerce Brands | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`A Q4 ad strategy for DTC brands from October to January: creative and list building, the November peak, December gifting cutoffs, budgets and inventory.`,primaryKeyword:`q4 ad strategy`,secondaryKeywords:[`q4 ecommerce strategy`,`holiday ad strategy`,`q4 facebook ads`,`december shipping cutoff ads`,`q4 cpm`,`holiday marketing plan ecommerce`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Strategy & Scaling`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/bfcm-meta-ads-playbook`,title:`BFCM 2026 Meta Ads Playbook`,text:`Day-by-day campaign structure and budgets for the peak weekend.`},{href:`/bfcm-ads-management`,title:`BFCM Ads Management`,text:`Have your Q4 creative bank, offers and campaigns run for you.`},{href:`/tools/meta-ads-budget-calculator`,title:`Meta Ads Budget Calculator`,text:`Turn a Q4 revenue goal into a daily budget you can defend.`},{href:`/case-studies/dropshipping-dtc`,title:`Dropshipping & DTC Case Studies`,text:`Q4 run-up results, including $449,221 in 49 days.`}],author:n,directAnswer:`A strong Q4 ad strategy runs from October to January, not just Black Friday weekend. Use October to test creative and build email and SMS lists while auctions are calmer, scale proven ads into the November peak, switch to gifting and shipping-cutoff messaging in December, and use January to acquire customers cheaply and bring holiday buyers back.`,definitionBox:{term:`Q4 (fourth quarter)`,definition:`October, November and December, the busiest selling period for most ecommerce brands. Ad auctions get more competitive as retailers raise budgets, so the same spend usually buys less reach, while shoppers are more ready to buy.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`why-q4-is-different`,text:`Why Q4 ads behave differently`},{id:`q4-calendar`,text:`The Q4 calendar at a glance`},{id:`october-build`,text:`October: creative testing and list building`},{id:`november-peak`,text:`November: the peak`},{id:`december-gifting`,text:`December: gifting and shipping cutoffs`},{id:`january-reset`,text:`January: the quiet opportunity`},{id:`q4-budget-inventory`,text:`Q4 budgets and inventory`},{id:`q4-results`,text:`Q4 results from client accounts`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`why-q4-is-different`,h2:`Why Q4 ads behave differently`,content:`Most DTC brands treat Q4 as one weekend. The brands that finish the year strongest treat it as a four-month plan, and a good q4 ad strategy starts in early October.

The reason is the auction. Meta, TikTok and Google sell ads in real-time auctions, and in Q4 almost every retailer raises budgets at the same time. More advertisers bidding for roughly the same number of people pushes CPMs up, starting gradually in October, climbing through November and peaking around Black Friday and Cyber Monday. Prices then ease in late December and usually fall sharply in early January when big advertisers pull back.

Expensive attention is only half the picture. Shoppers are also more willing to buy, so conversion rates often rise alongside costs. The goal is not to avoid Q4 auctions but to arrive with tested creative, warm audiences and margins that can absorb higher costs.`},{id:`q4-calendar`,h2:`The Q4 calendar at a glance`,content:`Here is how we split the quarter for partner brands. Exact dates move each year, so set them against your own shipping carriers and promo calendar.`,table:{headers:[`Window`,`Main goal`,`Ad focus`,`Owned channel focus`],rows:[[`Early to mid October`,`Find winners`,`Weekly creative tests at controlled budgets`,`Grow email and SMS lists`],[`Late October to mid November`,`Warm the audience`,`Scale proven ads, run lead and teaser ads`,`VIP and early access lists`],[`Late November`,`Harvest demand`,`BFCM offers, scaled winners, cost controls`,`Early access, then sale sends`],[`Early to mid December`,`Gifting`,`Gift guides, bundles, shipping cutoff urgency`,`Gift flows and reminders`],[`Late December`,`Last-minute buyers`,`Gift cards, local pickup, digital gifts`,`Gift card and after-cutoff sends`],[`January`,`Cheap acquisition and repeat orders`,`Prospecting while CPMs fall`,`Post-holiday win-back and replenishment`]]}},{id:`october-build`,h2:`October: creative testing and list building`,content:`October is your last chance to learn at normal prices. Every winning ad you find now is one you can scale into November instead of testing at peak costs.

Run a weekly creative cadence with genuinely different concepts: new angles, hooks and formats, not colour swaps. We brief from a 45+ cell hook and angle matrix (5 angles x 3 hook styles x 3 formats) and aim to have a bank of proven ads and fresh variations ready before mid November. Include gift-angle creative now so you know which version works before December.

At the same time, build owned audiences. Run lead or signup ads for an early access list, add a pop-up offer for VIP access, and make sure your welcome flow is live. Subscribers you collect in October are customers you can reach in November without paying peak CPMs. Check tracking too: Conversions API, deduplication and event quality need to be clean before volume rises.`},{id:`november-peak`,h2:`November: the peak`,content:`November is where most Q4 revenue lands. We have written a full day-by-day plan for this window in the BFCM Meta ads playbook, covering campaign structure, budgets by week, retargeting and a creative checklist, so this guide only covers how November fits into the wider quarter.

Three principles carry over. First, open offers to your email and SMS lists early, before auctions peak, so you collect sales and purchase signals ahead of the crowd. Second, scale ads that already worked in October instead of launching untested concepts on the most expensive days. Third, protect margin with structured offers such as bundles, tiered spend-and-save and gifts with purchase rather than deep sitewide discounts.

Plan what happens on 1 December before the peak starts. Brands that go quiet after Cyber Monday lose the gifting weeks that follow.`},{id:`december-gifting`,h2:`December: gifting and shipping cutoffs`,content:`In December the buyer changes. Many shoppers are buying for someone else, so creative should show the product as a gift: wrapped, unboxed by the recipient, or framed as "for the person who has everything". Gift guides, bundles at clear price points and gift sets usually outperform the same hero ad you ran in November.

Shipping cutoffs drive December urgency. Publish your last order date for delivery by Christmas based on your carriers' real timelines, and put it in ad copy, on the product page and in the announcement bar. Urgency built on a real deadline converts better and keeps customer service calm.`,list:[`Two weeks before the cutoff: gift guides and bundles to prospecting and warm audiences.`,`Final week before the cutoff: countdown creative and "order by" messaging.`,`After the cutoff: switch to gift cards, digital gifts and local pickup if you offer it.`,`Pause or rework ads that promise delivery you can no longer meet.`]},{id:`january-reset`,h2:`January: the quiet opportunity`,content:`January is often underrated. Large advertisers cut budgets after the holidays, so auctions calm down and new customers can be cheaper to acquire. People also have gift cards, cash and new-year goals, which suits fitness, supplements, skincare and self-improvement products in particular.

Use January to prospect with your best evergreen creative and to bring Q4 buyers back. Holiday customers who bought once at a discount need a reason to come back at full price: post-purchase education, replenishment reminders and a cross-sell in your email flows. The second order is where most Q4 acquisitions turn profitable.

January is also the month to review the quarter honestly. Compare blended CAC, MER and new customer counts for each window against your plan, note which creative carried the peak, and record which offers protected margin and which only moved volume. Keep the winning ads running into the new year rather than switching everything off with the decorations. Ads that worked in November often keep working once the discount is removed and the copy shifts from "sale" to the core problem the product solves. Those notes become the starting point for next year's Q4 plan.`},{id:`q4-budget-inventory`,h2:`Q4 budgets and inventory`,content:`Set the Q4 budget backwards from margin, not from last year's spend. Work out break-even ROAS and target CPA at your discounted prices, because a 25% off offer changes the maths. Then plan spend by window: moderate in October for testing, rising through November, holding through the December cutoff and continuing in January if acquisition costs fall.

Scale in steps. In our process, budgets in the scale phase move in 20 to 30% steps while efficiency holds, with cost caps on the highest-spend days so the account cannot overspend when CPMs jump.

Inventory is the constraint most brands forget. Running out of a hero SKU in late November wastes the creative and audiences you built for two months.`,list:[`Forecast units by SKU from your revenue target and planned offers.`,`Order early: supplier and freight delays are common in Q4.`,`Build bundles from slower stock so you move it at better margins.`,`Turn off ads automatically when a product sells out.`]},{id:`q4-results`,h2:`Q4 results from client accounts`,content:`These are real Q4 runs from our published case studies. They are individual accounts, not guarantees, but they show what tested creative and an early start can do.

A dropshipping brand that started its Q4 run-up early generated $449,221 in 49 days. Another Q4 dropshipping account produced $133,420 in 7 days at 4.06x ROAS on $32,857 in spend over a weekend when many brands slowed down. A skincare brand made $146,184 in 27 days on $40,124 in spend, a 3.64x ROAS across 957 orders, as Q4 began.

The common thread was preparation: the creative and audiences were tested before costs peaked, so budget could follow results instead of hope.`}],faqs:[{question:`When should I start my Q4 ad strategy?`,answer:`Start planning in September and running tests in early October. That gives you four to six weeks to find winning creative, build email and SMS lists and fix tracking while auctions are calmer. Brands that start in mid November end up testing on the most expensive days of the year.`},{question:`Why do CPMs go up in Q4?`,answer:`Ad platforms run auctions, and in Q4 many more advertisers raise budgets to reach roughly the same audience. More competition per impression pushes prices up, most sharply around Black Friday and Cyber Monday. Prices usually ease late in December and fall in early January as large advertisers cut spend.`},{question:`Should I increase my ad budget in Q4?`,answer:`Usually yes, but only for ads that are already profitable at your discounted prices. Set break-even ROAS and target CPA for your Q4 offers first, then raise budgets in steps of 20 to 30% while efficiency holds. Use cost caps on the busiest days so higher CPMs cannot drain the account.`},{question:`What should I advertise in December?`,answer:`Lead with gifting: gift guides, bundles at clear price points and gift-ready creative. Add shipping-cutoff urgency in the last week before your Christmas delivery deadline. After the cutoff, switch to gift cards, digital gifts or local pickup, and stop running ads that promise delivery you cannot meet.`},{question:`Is January a good time to run ads?`,answer:`Often yes. Many large advertisers pull back after the holidays, so auctions are less crowded and new customers can cost less. Shoppers also have gift cards and new-year goals. Use January for prospecting with proven creative and for bringing Q4 buyers back through email and SMS flows.`}]},{slug:`how-to-lower-cac`,title:`How to Lower CAC: 7 Levers DTC Brands Can Pull (With the Math)`,titleTag:`How to Lower CAC: 7 Levers for DTC Brands | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`How to lower CAC for a DTC brand: creative, offer and AOV, conversion rate, tracking, audience consolidation, retention and channel mix, with worked maths.`,primaryKeyword:`how to lower cac`,secondaryKeywords:[`reduce customer acquisition cost`,`lower cac ecommerce`,`cac formula`,`blended cac`,`cac payback`,`customer acquisition cost dtc`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Metrics & Unit Economics`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/tools/cac-calculator`,title:`Free CAC Calculator`,text:`Plug in your spend and new customers to see CAC and payback.`},{href:`/glossary/cac`,title:`What Is CAC?`,text:`Definition, formula and blended versus paid CAC.`},{href:`/cro-agency`,title:`Ecommerce CRO Agency`,text:`Raise conversion rate, the lever that lowers CAC on every channel.`},{href:`/blog/meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda Explained`,text:`Why creative variety now drives acquisition cost on Meta.`}],author:n,directAnswer:`To learn how to lower CAC, start with the formula: paid CAC equals cost per click divided by conversion rate. Lower it by improving creative so clicks get cheaper, raising conversion rate on your store, fixing tracking so the algorithm gets clean signals, consolidating audiences, and growing retention and referral so not every customer has to be bought.`,definitionBox:{term:`CAC (customer acquisition cost)`,definition:`The average cost of winning one new customer: marketing spend divided by new customers in the same period. Blended CAC uses all spend and all new customers; paid CAC uses only ad spend and the customers those ads acquired.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`cac-basics`,text:`CAC basics: blended versus paid`},{id:`cac-math-example`,text:`A worked example of CAC math`},{id:`creative-lever`,text:`Lever 1: creative`},{id:`offer-aov-lever`,text:`Lever 2: offer and AOV`},{id:`conversion-rate-lever`,text:`Lever 3: conversion rate`},{id:`tracking-lever`,text:`Lever 4: tracking and signal quality`},{id:`audience-consolidation`,text:`Lever 5: audience consolidation`},{id:`retention-channel-mix`,text:`Levers 6 and 7: retention, referral and channel mix`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`cac-basics`,h2:`CAC basics: blended versus paid`,content:`Before working out how to lower CAC, agree on which CAC you mean. Blended CAC divides all marketing spend by all new customers, including the ones who arrived through organic search, word of mouth or email. Paid CAC divides ad spend by the new customers the ads acquired. Blended CAC is the number that pays the bills; paid CAC is the one you can act on inside an ad account.

Track both monthly, and always next to contribution margin per first order and LTV. A CAC of $60 is excellent for a brand whose customers spend $400 over a year and a problem for one whose customers buy once at $45. The aim is not the lowest possible CAC; it is a CAC that pays back within a timeframe your cash flow can handle.`},{id:`cac-math-example`,h2:`A worked example of CAC math`,content:`For paid traffic, CAC breaks down into two numbers you can improve separately. If you pay a cost per click (CPC) and a share of those clicks buy (conversion rate, CVR), then CAC = CPC / CVR. Here is an example brand spending $30,000 a month. These figures are illustrative, chosen to show the maths.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`CPC`,`Clicks`,`CVR`,`New customers`,`CAC`],rows:[[`Baseline`,`$1.25`,`24,000`,`2.5%`,`600`,`$50.00`],[`Better creative only`,`$1.10`,`27,273`,`2.5%`,`682`,`$44.00`],[`Better CVR only`,`$1.25`,`24,000`,`3.0%`,`720`,`$41.67`],[`Both improvements`,`$1.10`,`27,273`,`3.0%`,`818`,`$36.67`]]},subsections:[{h3:`What the example shows`,content:`Two modest improvements, a 12% cheaper click and half a point more conversion rate, cut CAC by about 27%, from $50.00 to $36.67, with the same budget.

Now add margin. Say average order value is $70 and contribution margin after product cost, shipping and fees is 60%, so each first order contributes $42 before ads. At the baseline CAC of $50 the brand loses $8 on every first order. At $36.67 it makes $5.33. If a bundle lifts AOV to $84, contribution rises to $50.40 and the same CAC returns $13.73 per first order. Run your own numbers in the CAC calculator before you choose which lever to work on first.`}]},{id:`creative-lever`,h2:`Lever 1: creative`,content:`On Meta and TikTok, creative now does most of the targeting. Ads that stop the scroll and speak to a clear problem earn better engagement, which the platforms reward with cheaper delivery, and they attract people more likely to buy.

Creative affects both halves of the CAC formula. A sharper hook lowers cost per click, and an ad that sets honest expectations about price, use and results sends visitors who convert at a higher rate once they land.

The practical move is volume and variety. Test new angles, hooks and formats every week rather than small edits of one ad. For partner brands we produce 30 to 50+ new creatives a month, planned from a 5 angles x 3 hook styles x 3 formats matrix, because a steady supply of different ads is what keeps CPC from creeping up as audiences tire of the old ones.`,list:[`Judge new ads on cost per purchase, not only click-through rate.`,`Refresh before fatigue shows: rising frequency and CPC are the early signs.`,`Match the landing page to the ad promise so cheaper clicks still convert.`]},{id:`offer-aov-lever`,h2:`Lever 2: offer and AOV`,content:`A stronger offer lowers CAC directly by converting more of the same visitors. Raising average order value does not change CAC itself, but it changes how much CAC you can afford, as the example showed.

Good offers add perceived value without cutting deep into margin: bundles of complementary products, quantity breaks on consumables, gifts with purchase above a threshold, and free shipping set just above your current AOV. Test them against a plain price, and judge by contribution margin per order, not just conversion rate.`},{id:`conversion-rate-lever`,h2:`Lever 3: conversion rate`,content:`Conversion rate improves CAC on every channel at once, which makes it the most underused lever. If your store converts 2.5% and you lift it to 3.0%, every ad, email and influencer post gets cheaper per customer.

Start where paid traffic lands. Speed up mobile load times, put the main benefit and price above the fold, show reviews with photos close to the add to cart button, answer shipping and return questions on the page, and remove steps from checkout. A clinical skincare store we worked on reached a 5.25% conversion rate in a $61,000 month after this kind of product page and store work.`},{id:`tracking-lever`,h2:`Lever 4: tracking and signal quality`,content:`Ad platforms optimise toward the conversions they can see. If your pixel misses purchases, double-counts them or reports them late, the algorithm learns from bad data and spends money on the wrong people.

Set up the Conversions API alongside the pixel, deduplicate events with a shared event ID, send as much customer information as your privacy policy allows, and check event match quality in Events Manager. Clean signals do not show up as a line item, but they often lower CAC more than any single campaign change because every optimisation decision gets better.`,list:[`Compare purchases in Events Manager with Shopify orders for the same days; a large gap means missing or duplicated events.`,`Make sure the purchase value includes the real order total, so value-based bidding has accurate data.`,`Fire add to cart and initiate checkout events too, so the platform has more signals while purchase volume is low.`,`Recheck tracking after every theme change, checkout app install or consent banner update.`]},{id:`audience-consolidation`,h2:`Lever 5: audience consolidation`,content:`Many accounts split budget across dozens of small ad sets with narrow interests, lookalikes and overlapping audiences. Each one gets too little data to exit learning, and they bid against each other in the same auctions.

Consolidating into fewer campaigns with broad targeting gives the algorithm more conversions per ad set to learn from, which usually stabilises and lowers CAC. Keep separate structures only where there is a real reason, such as a different country, product line or margin. Use customer list exclusions so prospecting budget is not spent on people who already bought.

A simple structure works for most brands spending in the tens of thousands a month: one testing campaign where new creative gets a fair share of spend, one scaling campaign that receives the proven winners, and a small retargeting budget only if your prospecting campaigns do not already reach warm visitors. Fewer moving parts also make CAC easier to read, because you can see which change caused a shift instead of guessing across twenty ad sets.`},{id:`retention-channel-mix`,h2:`Levers 6 and 7: retention, referral and channel mix`,content:`The cheapest customer is one you do not have to buy twice. Strong email and SMS flows, replenishment reminders and post-purchase cross-sells raise repeat purchase rate, which raises LTV and lets you accept a higher first-order CAC while blended CAC falls.`,subsections:[{h3:`Referral`,content:`A simple give-and-get referral programme, sent after customers have had time to see results, brings in new customers at a known, fixed cost. Affiliate and creator programmes work the same way at larger scale.`},{h3:`Channel mix`,content:`Relying on one channel ties your CAC to its auction. Adding Google Shopping and search captures people already looking for your product, TikTok can open a younger or different audience, and organic content lowers blended CAC over time. Add channels one at a time and judge each on blended CAC and MER, not only platform-reported numbers.`}]}],faqs:[{question:`What is a good CAC for an ecommerce brand?`,answer:`A good CAC is one your margins and LTV can support. Compare CAC with contribution margin per first order and with 12-month LTV. If the first order covers CAC you can scale freely; if not, you need enough repeat purchases to pay it back within a period your cash flow can handle.`},{question:`What is the fastest way to lower CAC?`,answer:`Usually conversion rate and creative. Conversion rate improvements on high-traffic product pages lower CAC on every channel immediately. New creative angles can cut cost per purchase within a couple of weeks. Tracking fixes are also fast if your pixel or Conversions API setup is currently missing or duplicating purchases.`},{question:`How do you calculate CAC?`,answer:`Divide marketing spend by new customers acquired in the same period. For blended CAC use all marketing spend and all new customers. For paid CAC use ad spend and the customers those ads acquired. For paid traffic you can also think of it as cost per click divided by conversion rate.`},{question:`Does raising AOV lower CAC?`,answer:`Not directly. CAC is cost per customer, and AOV is revenue per order. But a higher AOV raises the contribution each first order brings in, so the same CAC becomes more profitable and you can afford to bid more. Bundles and quantity breaks can also lift conversion rate, which does lower CAC.`},{question:`Why is my CAC going up?`,answer:`Common causes are creative fatigue, seasonal auction costs such as Q4, tracking problems that hide conversions, too many small overlapping ad sets, a falling store conversion rate, or scaling budget faster than new creative arrives. Check frequency, CPC and site conversion rate first to see which part of the formula moved.`}]},{slug:`ugc-ads-for-dtc`,title:`UGC Ads for DTC Brands: Formats, Hooks and Scripts That Sell`,titleTag:`UGC Ads for DTC: Formats, Hooks and Scripts | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`UGC ads for DTC brands explained: formats and hooks that work, script structure, where UGC fits in the funnel, ad policy limits and usage rights basics.`,primaryKeyword:`ugc ads for dtc`,secondaryKeywords:[`ugc ads`,`ugc ad examples`,`ugc script template`,`ugc hooks`,`ugc usage rights`,`what are ugc ads`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Creative Production`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/scale-ugc-production`,title:`How to Scale UGC Production`,text:`The system for making 30 to 50 UGC ads a month once you know what works.`},{href:`/viral-creatives`,title:`Direct-Response Creatives & UGC`,text:`Hooks and UGC ads from our creative engine.`},{href:`/glossary/ugc`,title:`What Is UGC?`,text:`A short definition of user-generated content in advertising.`},{href:`/creative-strategy-agency`,title:`Creative Strategy Agency`,text:`Angles, briefs and testing plans for paid social creative.`}],author:n,directAnswer:`UGC ads for DTC brands are paid social ads built from phone-filmed videos of real customers or paid creators talking about a product. The formats that work best are testimonials, unboxings, demos, problem-solution stories and founder videos, each opening with a hook in the first two seconds and following a simple script: hook, problem, product, proof, call to action.`,definitionBox:{term:`UGC ad`,definition:`A paid ad that uses content filmed by a customer or creator in a native, non-studio style. In DTC it usually means paid creators producing content that looks like an organic post, run from the brand account or the creator handle.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-are-ugc-ads`,text:`What are UGC ads?`},{id:`ugc-formats`,text:`UGC ad formats that work`},{id:`ugc-hooks`,text:`Hooks for UGC ads`},{id:`ugc-script-structure`,text:`How to structure a UGC script`},{id:`founder-vs-creator`,text:`Founder videos versus creator videos`},{id:`ugc-funnel`,text:`Where UGC fits in the funnel`},{id:`before-after-policy`,text:`Before and after ads: policy limits`},{id:`compliance-usage-rights`,text:`Compliance and usage rights basics`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-are-ugc-ads`,h2:`What are UGC ads?`,content:`UGC ads for DTC brands are ads made from videos that look like they were filmed by a normal person on a phone, because they were. The person on camera might be a real customer, a paid creator or the founder. What makes it UGC is the style: direct to camera, natural light, real homes and bathrooms, honest language, no studio polish.

UGC works in paid social because it fits the feed. People scroll past anything that looks like a TV ad, but they stop for someone who looks like a friend explaining why they switched products. It also lets you test many messages cheaply, since one creator can film several angles in a single session.

This guide covers what to make: the formats, hooks and script structure that sell, and the rules to stay within. If you already know what works and need to make more of it each month, read our guide on scaling UGC production, which covers creator sourcing, briefs, batch editing and weekly systems.`},{id:`ugc-formats`,h2:`UGC ad formats that work`,content:`Most winning UGC ads fall into a handful of formats. Test several, because the format that works for a skincare serum may not suit a hoodie.`,table:{headers:[`Format`,`What it shows`,`Best for`],rows:[[`Testimonial`,`A customer explains the problem they had and what changed`,`Products with a clear result, such as skincare or supplements`],[`Unboxing`,`Opening the package, first reactions, what is inside`,`Giftable products, premium packaging, bundles`],[`Demo`,`The product in use, close up, step by step`,`Gadgets, tools, apparel fit, anything visual`],[`Problem-solution`,`A frustrating moment, then the product fixing it`,`Products that solve an everyday annoyance`],[`Comparison`,`Your product next to the usual alternative`,`Categories where shoppers already use something`],[`Founder story`,`Why the product exists and how it is made`,`Newer brands, mission-led products, premium price points`]]}},{id:`ugc-hooks`,h2:`Hooks for UGC ads`,content:`The hook is the first one to three seconds, the words on screen and the first visual. It decides whether anyone watches the rest. Pair a spoken line with a visual that makes sense with the sound off, and test several hooks on the same body footage before writing a new script.`,list:[`Call out the viewer: "If you have tried every serum and still have dark spots, watch this."`,`Confession: "I was so wrong about this product."`,`Bold claim you can back up: "This replaced three things in my bathroom."`,`Visual pattern break: the product in an unexpected place or a fast close-up.`,`Objection first: "I thought it was overpriced until I used it for a month."`,`Result first: the end result on screen before any explanation.`]},{id:`ugc-script-structure`,h2:`How to structure a UGC script`,content:`Good UGC sounds unscripted, but it follows a structure. Give creators the structure and key points rather than word-for-word lines, so the delivery stays natural.`,subsections:[{h3:`The five-part script`,list:[`Hook (0 to 3 seconds): one of the hook types above.`,`Problem (3 to 8 seconds): the specific frustration, in the creator's own words.`,`Product (8 to 20 seconds): what it is, how it works, shown in use.`,`Proof (20 to 30 seconds): their result, reviews, a guarantee or a demonstration.`,`Call to action (last 3 to 5 seconds): what to do now and why today.`]},{h3:`An example for a supplement`,content:`Hook: creator holds the tub up and says "I stopped needing my afternoon coffee." Problem: the 3pm slump at work. Product: shows the scoop, mixes it, explains the main ingredient in plain words. Proof: what changed for them over a few weeks, plus a close-up of reviews. Call to action: try it with the money-back guarantee. Keep claims to the creator's own experience and make sure anything said about the product is supported.`},{h3:`An example for an apparel product`,content:`Hook: creator walks toward the camera and says "These are the only leggings that passed my squat test." Problem: see-through fabric and waistbands that roll down mid-workout. Product: a close-up of the fabric, a squat in good light, the waistband staying put. Proof: how many times they have worn and washed them, plus a quick look at the size chart they used. Call to action: find your size and use the free exchange if it is wrong.

Notice that the structure stays the same while the details change. Once a script works, you can keep the problem, product and proof sections and test new hooks on top of the same footage, which is the cheapest way to find a second winner.`}]},{id:`founder-vs-creator`,h2:`Founder videos versus creator videos`,content:`Founder videos build trust: they explain why the product exists, how it is made and why it costs what it does. They suit newer brands, premium prices and moments like a launch or a sale announcement. Their limit is range: one founder is one face and one voice.

Creator videos give you range. Different ages, styles and situations let you find which customer types respond, and they look like peer recommendations rather than a sales pitch. Most DTC accounts run both: founder content for trust and offers, creator content for volume and testing. If you need creator content made for you, our sister studio SevenFigureStudio produces UGC video ads for DTC brands.`},{id:`ugc-funnel`,h2:`Where UGC fits in the funnel`,content:`UGC works at every stage, but the format should change with how much the viewer already knows. A cold viewer needs to understand what the product is and why it matters in a few seconds. A warm viewer already knows that and is weighing doubts about price, quality or whether it will work for them. In practice, most accounts now run broad campaigns where the platform decides who sees which ad, so think of this as a mix to include rather than separate ad sets to build.`,list:[`Cold prospecting: problem-solution, call-out hooks and demos that explain the product fast.`,`Warm audiences (engaged or visited): testimonials, comparisons and objection-handling videos.`,`Retargeting and cart visitors: reviews, guarantees, unboxings and offer reminders.`,`Existing customers: how-to-use videos, cross-sell ideas and new product launches.`]},{id:`before-after-policy`,h2:`Before and after ads: policy limits`,content:`Before and after content is persuasive, which is exactly why platforms restrict it. Meta and TikTok limit before and after images for weight loss, and both are strict about ads that imply negative self-perception or make health claims. Rules change often, so check the current advertising policies for your category before you brief.

Safer approaches: show the product in use rather than a body transformation, let the creator describe their experience without dramatic split screens, avoid language that asserts personal attributes such as "Are you overweight?", and never edit footage to exaggerate a result. If you sell supplements or skincare, expect closer review and plan alternative versions of each ad.`},{id:`compliance-usage-rights`,h2:`Compliance and usage rights basics`,content:`UGC ads are still advertising, so endorsement rules apply. In the US, the FTC requires that paid or gifted relationships are disclosed and that testimonials reflect the creator's honest experience with the product. Creators should actually use what they talk about, and any claim about results needs support.

Usage rights decide where and how long you can run the content. Agree them in writing before filming.`,list:[`Paid usage: the right to run the video as an ad, not just organic posts.`,`Duration: how many months you can run it, with a renewal option.`,`Whitelisting or partnership ads: permission to run ads from the creator's handle.`,`Raw footage: whether you receive unedited clips to re-cut into new versions.`,`Exclusivity: whether the creator can promote competitors during the term.`]}],faqs:[{question:`What makes a good UGC ad?`,answer:`A good UGC ad has a hook that stops the scroll in the first two seconds, a believable person describing a specific problem, the product shown in use, proof such as results or reviews, and one clear call to action. It should look native to the feed and make sense with the sound off.`},{question:`How long should a UGC ad be?`,answer:`Most UGC ads that work run 15 to 45 seconds. Shorter cuts suit cold audiences and fast demos, while longer testimonials can work for warm audiences who already know the brand. Test the same footage cut to different lengths instead of assuming one length is always better.`},{question:`Do UGC creators need to disclose that ads are paid?`,answer:`Yes, in the US the FTC expects material connections such as payment or free products to be disclosed, and many other countries have similar rules. When content runs as a paid ad from the brand account the ad label helps, but organic posts and creator-handle ads should still carry a clear disclosure.`},{question:`Can I use before and after photos in UGC ads?`,answer:`Sometimes, but with care. Meta and TikTok restrict before and after images for weight loss and are strict about health claims and negative self-image. Check current policies for your category, avoid edited or exaggerated results, and prepare versions that show the product in use instead of a transformation.`},{question:`What usage rights do I need for UGC ads?`,answer:`At minimum you need paid usage rights for the platforms you advertise on and a clear duration. Many brands also ask for whitelisting or partnership ad access, raw footage for re-editing and a short exclusivity period. Put everything in writing before filming so there are no disputes later.`},{question:`Are UGC ads better than studio ads?`,answer:`For most DTC brands on Meta and TikTok, UGC is the better starting point because it looks native, costs less per asset and is easier to test in volume. Studio content still has a role for brand assets, product pages and some retargeting. The best accounts usually run a mix and let results decide.`}]},{slug:`meta-advantage-plus-guide`,title:`Advantage Plus Shopping Campaigns: A Practical Guide for DTC Brands`,titleTag:`Advantage Plus Shopping Campaigns Guide | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`Advantage plus shopping campaigns explained: how they work, setup, existing customer budget caps, creative inputs, reporting and when manual campaigns win.`,primaryKeyword:`advantage plus shopping campaigns`,secondaryKeywords:[`advantage+ shopping campaigns`,`advantage+ sales campaigns`,`meta asc`,`advantage plus existing customer budget cap`,`advantage+ vs manual campaigns`,`meta advantage plus setup`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Meta Ads`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/how-to-scale-facebook-ads`,title:`How to scale Meta ads for an 8-figure DTC brand`,text:`Consolidate, step budgets and feed creative so spend grows without CPA blowing up.`},{href:`/blog/meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda Explained`,text:`The ad retrieval system that makes creative variety matter inside Advantage+.`},{href:`/glossary/advantage-plus`,title:`What Is Advantage+?`,text:`A short definition of Meta's automated campaign options.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`Have your Advantage+ and manual campaigns built and managed.`},{href:`/blog/facebook-ads-for-shopify`,title:`Facebook Ads for Shopify`,text:`Pixel, Conversions API and catalog setup to finish first.`}],author:n,directAnswer:`Advantage plus shopping campaigns are Meta's automated sales campaigns for ecommerce. You supply budget, a conversion goal, a catalog and a set of creatives, and Meta decides who sees which ad across Facebook and Instagram. They suit accounts with clean purchase tracking and varied creative, with manual campaigns kept for tight control and testing.`,definitionBox:{term:`Advantage+ shopping (sales) campaign`,definition:`A Meta campaign type that automates targeting, placements and much of the budget allocation for purchase-focused ecommerce campaigns. Meta has renamed and merged these options over time, so the label in your Ads Manager may differ.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-is-advantage-plus`,text:`What are Advantage+ shopping campaigns?`},{id:`advantage-vs-manual`,text:`Advantage+ versus manual campaigns`},{id:`advantage-setup`,text:`How to set up an Advantage+ campaign`},{id:`existing-customer-cap`,text:`The existing customer budget cap`},{id:`creative-inputs`,text:`Creative inputs: what to feed it`},{id:`when-manual-wins`,text:`When to use manual campaigns instead`},{id:`advantage-reporting`,text:`Reporting on Advantage+ campaigns`},{id:`advantage-mistakes`,text:`Common Advantage+ mistakes`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-is-advantage-plus`,h2:`What are Advantage+ shopping campaigns?`,content:`Advantage plus shopping campaigns, often shortened to ASC, are Meta's automated campaign type for ecommerce sales. Instead of building ad sets with interests, lookalikes and placement choices, you give Meta a budget, a purchase goal, your country and your creative. Meta's systems then decide who to show ads to, on which placements, and which creative to serve to each person.

Meta has changed the names and setup of these campaigns more than once. In many accounts the option now appears as an Advantage+ sales campaign, and some controls have moved or merged. The principles in this guide hold across versions: automation works best when you give it clean signals and good creative, and you keep control through budget, creative and a few settings rather than through targeting.

Advantage+ depends on how Meta finds people for each ad. Our guide to Meta Andromeda explains why creative variety, not targeting, now decides how far these campaigns can reach.`},{id:`advantage-vs-manual`,h2:`Advantage+ versus manual campaigns`,content:`The main difference is who makes the targeting and allocation decisions.`,table:{headers:[`Area`,`Advantage+ shopping`,`Manual sales campaign`],rows:[[`Targeting`,`Mostly automated, broad by default`,`You choose audiences, interests and exclusions`],[`Placements`,`Automatic across Meta placements`,`Automatic or chosen manually`],[`Structure`,`One campaign, few or no ad set choices`,`Multiple ad sets you design`],[`New versus existing customers`,`Controlled through customer settings or a budget cap`,`Controlled through audience exclusions`],[`Best use`,`Scaling proven creative efficiently`,`Testing, specific audiences, tight control`]]}},{id:`advantage-setup`,h2:`How to set up an Advantage+ campaign`,content:`Get the foundations right before launching. Advantage+ amplifies whatever signal you give it, including bad data.`,list:[`Confirm tracking: pixel and Conversions API both sending Purchase events, deduplicated, with good event match quality.`,`Connect your product catalog if you plan to use catalog or dynamic ads.`,`Define existing customers by uploading or syncing customer lists, and add your website and engagement audiences where the setting allows.`,`Choose the sales objective and the Advantage+ option, with purchase as the conversion event.`,`Set one daily budget large enough to produce regular purchases for learning.`,`Add 5 to 10 genuinely different creatives at launch: video, static and catalog formats.`,`Leave it alone for the first several days while it learns, unless spend is clearly wasted.`]},{id:`existing-customer-cap`,h2:`The existing customer budget cap`,content:`Left alone, an automated campaign often finds the easiest conversions: people who already know and buy from you. That inflates ROAS while bringing in few new customers.

To manage this, Meta has offered a way to tell it who your existing customers are and to limit the share of budget spent on them, often called the existing customer budget cap. Depending on your account and the current version, it may appear as a percentage cap, as customer segment settings, or as reporting that splits results by new and existing customers. Set it up as soon as it is available in your account.

A practical starting point for a prospecting-focused campaign is a low cap on existing customers, then adjusting based on blended results. If email and SMS already handle repeat purchases well, there is less reason to pay Meta for them.`},{id:`creative-inputs`,h2:`Creative inputs: what to feed it`,content:`With targeting automated, creative becomes the main lever you control. Each distinct ad helps Meta find a different slice of buyers, while near-duplicates compete for the same people.

Feed the campaign variety: different angles (problem, result, objection, offer), different hook styles and different formats (UGC video, demo, static, carousel, catalog). Add new creative regularly so the campaign does not lean on one ad until it fatigues. For partner brands we produce 30 to 50+ new creatives a month, and the best performers from testing graduate into Advantage+.

Check the creative enhancement settings. Meta can apply automatic edits such as cropping, music or text variations. Some help, some change how your brand looks, so review them rather than accepting every default.`,subsections:[{h3:`Formats and placements`,content:`Supply vertical 9:16 video for Reels and Stories and a 4:5 or 1:1 version for the feed, so Meta does not have to crop your ads awkwardly. Keep key text and the product away from the edges where interface elements sit. If you sell a wide catalog, add a catalog or collection ad alongside your videos so the campaign can show people products similar to what they viewed.`},{h3:`Copy and landing pages`,content:`Write several primary texts and headlines that match the angles in your videos, rather than one generic line for every ad. Send each ad to the page that fits its promise: a bundle ad to the bundle page, a single-product demo to that product page. Automation can find the right person, but it cannot fix a landing page that does not deliver what the ad promised.`}]},{id:`when-manual-wins`,h2:`When to use manual campaigns instead`,content:`Advantage+ is not always the answer. Keep manual campaigns for jobs where control matters more than automation. The two are not rivals: in a healthy account manual campaigns find and prove ads, and Advantage+ scales them. If you notice that every result depends on one automated campaign and you cannot explain why it works, that is a sign to rebuild some manual structure so you can learn again.`,list:[`Creative testing: a manual testing campaign gives each new ad a fair, readable share of spend before it graduates.`,`Low purchase volume: with few conversions a week, automation has too little data to learn from.`,`Specific audiences: launches to a waitlist, regional offers or a product meant for a narrow group.`,`Strict cost control: bid caps and cost caps on separate campaigns during peaks such as BFCM.`,`New markets: when you want clean, separate reads on a new country.`]},{id:`advantage-reporting`,h2:`Reporting on Advantage+ campaigns`,content:`Advantage+ reporting shows fewer levers, so read it carefully. Look at results by creative to see which ads are carrying spend, and use the breakdowns for new versus existing customers where they are available. A high ROAS driven mostly by existing customers is not growth.

Judge the campaign against your blended numbers, not only Ads Manager. Compare new customer revenue, blended CAC and MER before and after launch. Platform-reported ROAS includes conversions that would have happened anyway, and automated campaigns can be especially good at claiming them. If blended results hold or improve as Advantage+ spend grows, it is working.`,list:[`Weekly: spend and cost per purchase by creative, frequency, new versus existing customer split.`,`Monthly: blended CAC, MER and new customer revenue against the previous month.`,`Before scaling: confirm efficiency held for at least a week, then raise budget in 20 to 30% steps.`,`Every time: compare purchase counts in Ads Manager with Shopify orders to catch tracking drift.`]},{id:`advantage-mistakes`,h2:`Common Advantage+ mistakes`,content:`Most underperforming Advantage+ campaigns fail for the same few reasons. Almost all of them come back to the same idea: the campaign can only optimise with the signals, creative and time you give it. When results drop, check those three inputs before you blame the algorithm or rebuild the account.`,list:[`Launching with weak or missing Conversions API data.`,`Not defining existing customers, so budget goes to repeat buyers.`,`Too few creatives, or several near-identical versions of one ad.`,`Editing budgets and creative daily, which keeps the campaign relearning.`,`Running several Advantage+ campaigns that compete for the same buyers.`,`Judging success on platform ROAS alone instead of blended CAC and MER.`]}],faqs:[{question:`What is the difference between Advantage+ and a regular campaign?`,answer:`In Advantage+ shopping or sales campaigns, Meta automates targeting, placements and much of the budget allocation. In a regular manual campaign you build the ad sets, choose audiences and set exclusions. Advantage+ is usually better for scaling proven creative, while manual campaigns suit testing and situations that need tighter control.`},{question:`How much budget does an Advantage+ campaign need?`,answer:`Enough to generate regular purchases so the system can learn, which depends on your cost per purchase. A common approach is a daily budget that can fund several purchases per day at your target CPA. Run your numbers in a budget calculator and start lower if purchase volume is limited.`},{question:`Should I exclude existing customers from Advantage+?`,answer:`Usually you should limit them rather than fully exclude them. Define existing customers with your customer lists, then cap the share of budget spent on them using the setting available in your account. If your email and SMS flows already drive repeat orders, keep that cap low.`},{question:`How many creatives should I add to Advantage+?`,answer:`Start with about 5 to 10 genuinely different creatives covering several angles and formats, then add new ones regularly. Different concepts help Meta reach different buyers, while near-identical versions mostly compete for the same people. Move proven winners in from your testing campaign rather than launching untested ads.`},{question:`Can I run Advantage+ and manual campaigns together?`,answer:`Yes, and most ecommerce accounts should. A common structure uses a manual campaign for creative testing, an Advantage+ campaign for scaling winners and, at peak times, cost-controlled campaigns for extra volume. Keep the number of campaigns small so they do not compete against each other in the same auctions.`},{question:`Why did my Advantage+ campaign stop performing?`,answer:`The usual causes are creative fatigue, too few new ads, rising seasonal costs, budget changes that reset learning, or tracking problems. Check frequency and results by creative first, then confirm Purchase events are still reporting correctly. Adding fresh, distinct creative fixes most slowdowns.`}]}],i={name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},a=[{slug:`retargeting-for-dtc-brands`,title:`Retargeting for DTC Brands: How to Use It Without Stalling Growth`,titleTag:`Retargeting for DTC Brands: 2026 Guide | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`Retargeting for DTC brands: audience windows, creative by stage, Advantage+ caps, frequency, holdout tests and why retargeting alone never scales.`,primaryKeyword:`retargeting for dtc brands`,secondaryKeywords:[`scaling dtc brands with retargeting`,`meta retargeting audiences`,`retargeting windows`,`dynamic remarketing`,`incrementality testing`,`existing customer budget cap`],publishDate:`2026-11-06`,updatedDate:`2026-11-06`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Meta Ads`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda guide`,text:`What Andromeda changed for ecommerce ads and how to structure accounts for it.`},{href:`/blog/bfcm-meta-ads-playbook`,title:`BFCM ads playbook for Meta`,text:`Dates, budget plan, last-minute checklist and Cyber Monday ads for 2026.`},{href:`/blog/meta-advantage-plus-guide`,title:`Meta Advantage+ Guide`,text:`How Advantage+ sales campaigns treat new and existing customers.`},{href:`/blog/ecommerce-email-flows`,title:`Ecommerce Email Flows`,text:`The Klaviyo flows that do your cheapest retargeting for you.`},{href:`/glossary/attribution-window`,title:`What Is an Attribution Window?`,text:`Why retargeting looks better in Ads Manager than it really is.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency`,text:`Full-funnel Meta campaigns built for new customer growth.`}],author:i,directAnswer:`Retargeting for DTC brands means showing ads to people who already viewed, engaged with, carted or bought from your store. It converts cheaply but only harvests demand that prospecting created, so it should take a small, capped share of budget, use stage-specific creative and be judged with holdout tests rather than platform ROAS.`,definitionBox:{term:`Retargeting`,definition:`Advertising aimed at people who have already interacted with your brand, such as site visitors, video viewers, social engagers, cart abandoners or past customers. Also called remarketing, especially on Google.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`why-retargeting-alone-does-not-scale`,text:`Why retargeting alone does not scale`},{id:`retargeting-audience-windows`,text:`Retargeting audiences and windows`},{id:`creative-by-stage`,text:`Creative for each retargeting stage`},{id:`advantage-plus-and-broad`,text:`How Advantage+ and broad campaigns change retargeting`},{id:`frequency-and-exclusions`,text:`Frequency and exclusions`},{id:`measuring-incrementality`,text:`Measuring incrementality with holdouts`},{id:`tiktok-and-google-retargeting`,text:`Retargeting on TikTok and Google`},{id:`email-sms-retargeting`,text:`Email and SMS: the cheapest retargeting`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`why-retargeting-alone-does-not-scale`,h2:`Why retargeting alone does not scale`,content:`Retargeting for DTC brands is the part of the ad account that always looks best. Cart abandoners and recent visitors already know the product, so the ROAS on those ad sets is usually several times higher than on prospecting. That is exactly why it misleads founders. The audience is small, it only refills when new people arrive, and a large share of those buyers were going to come back anyway.

Think of retargeting as a harvest, not a planting. Prospecting campaigns, organic content, creators and word of mouth put people into the pool. Retargeting collects the ones who were close to buying. If you shift budget from prospecting into retargeting because the dashboard says it is more efficient, the pool shrinks, retargeting ROAS falls a few weeks later and total revenue goes flat.

The brands we see scale treat retargeting as a support layer: a modest, capped share of spend, built to close objections, with most budget going to finding new customers. The rest of this guide covers how to build that layer and how to check whether it is actually adding orders.`},{id:`retargeting-audience-windows`,h2:`Retargeting audiences and windows`,content:`Group warm audiences by how close they are to buying, then pick a window long enough to give the platform volume but short enough that the intent is still real. Smaller stores need longer windows to reach a usable audience size. These are the defaults we start from and then adjust to the purchase cycle of the product.`,table:{headers:[`Audience`,`Typical window`,`Intent level`,`Main job of the ad`],rows:[[`Video viewers (50 percent or more)`,`7 to 30 days`,`Low`,`Move from interest to a site visit`],[`Social engagers (Instagram and Facebook)`,`30 to 60 days`,`Low to medium`,`Show the product working and the offer`],[`Site visitors and product viewers`,`7 to 30 days`,`Medium`,`Answer the main objection and show proof`],[`Add to cart`,`3 to 14 days`,`High`,`Remove friction: shipping, returns, guarantee`],[`Initiated checkout`,`1 to 7 days`,`Very high`,`Short reminder, often handled by email and SMS first`],[`Past purchasers`,`30 to 180 days`,`Depends on product`,`Cross-sell, replenishment or new launch`]]},list:[`Exclude each deeper stage from the stage above it so one person is not hit by three ads at once.`,`Match the past purchaser window to how often people actually reorder, not to a round number.`,`Feed purchase and checkout events through the Conversions API so these audiences fill accurately.`]},{id:`creative-by-stage`,h2:`Creative for each retargeting stage`,content:`The most common retargeting mistake is running the same ads people already saw in prospecting. A warm viewer needs a new reason to act, and that reason changes as they get closer to checkout.

Early stage viewers and engagers respond to demonstration and proof: a UGC clip of the product in use, a before and after, or a creator answering the question people ask most in the comments. Site visitors and product viewers need objection handling: how it compares with what they use now, what reviewers say, sizing or ingredient detail and how returns work. Cart abandoners usually need the least persuasion and the most reassurance, so short ads about delivery times, guarantees and payment options tend to work, with an offer only when margins allow it.

Past buyers are a different audience entirely. Show them what goes with what they bought, a refill reminder timed to their usage, or a new product. Never show them the acquisition offer for the product they already own; it wastes money and teaches them to wait for discounts.

Refresh warm creative on a schedule, because small audiences see the same ads often and fatigue arrives faster than in prospecting.`},{id:`advantage-plus-and-broad`,h2:`How Advantage+ and broad campaigns change retargeting`,content:`On Meta, the classic split of separate prospecting and retargeting campaigns matters less than it used to. Broad and Advantage+ sales campaigns already serve ads to warm users when the system predicts they are likely to convert. If you also run a dedicated retargeting campaign, the two often compete for the same people and you pay twice for the same order.

Advantage+ sales campaigns let you tell Meta who your existing customers are by connecting customer lists, pixel purchasers and engaged audiences, and then set a cap on how much of the budget can be spent on that group. The exact controls and naming change as Meta updates the product, so check the current settings in your account. The principle is stable: cap existing customer spend so the campaign does not drift toward easy repeat buyers and report that as growth.

In practice we keep one small, tightly defined retargeting ad set or campaign for high-intent stages such as add to cart, with its own stage-specific creative, and let broad campaigns handle the rest. We watch the new customer share of purchases each week. If it falls while ROAS rises, the account is retargeting more and acquiring less.`},{id:`frequency-and-exclusions`,h2:`Frequency and exclusions`,content:`Warm audiences are small, so frequency climbs quickly. High frequency on a cart abandoner audience for a few days is fine. High frequency on a 60-day engager audience for weeks means the same people are seeing the same ads long after they decided not to buy. Watch frequency together with click-through rate and cost per purchase: when frequency rises and click-through falls, refresh the creative or shorten the window.`,list:[`Exclude recent purchasers from every acquisition and cart retargeting ad set, using a window that matches your delivery time plus a buffer.`,`Exclude people already inside an active email or SMS abandonment flow from paid cart retargeting for the first day or two, then test whether adding paid ads changes anything.`,`Exclude employees, customer service contacts and existing wholesale buyers where you can upload them.`,`Cap the share of total spend going to warm audiences and review it weekly instead of letting it creep up.`]},{id:`measuring-incrementality`,h2:`Measuring incrementality with holdouts`,content:`Platform ROAS on retargeting is inflated by design. Ads Manager credits a purchase to an ad if the buyer clicked or viewed it within the attribution window, even if they had already decided to buy. Retargeting audiences are full of those people, so their reported ROAS includes orders you would have received anyway.

The honest check is a holdout. Split a warm audience at random, show ads to one group and withhold them from the other for a few weeks, then compare the purchase rate. The gap is the real lift. Meta offers conversion lift studies for accounts with enough volume, and smaller brands can run a simpler version by pausing retargeting in one period and comparing total store revenue and new customer orders against the prior period, keeping prospecting spend steady.

Also track blended numbers. MER, which is total revenue divided by total marketing spend, does not care which campaign claims the order. If cutting retargeting spend in half barely moves MER or total orders, most of that spend was not incremental. Our ecommerce ROAS benchmark page explains why platform ROAS and blended ROAS often tell different stories.`},{id:`tiktok-and-google-retargeting`,h2:`Retargeting on TikTok and Google`,content:`TikTok lets you build custom audiences from video viewers, profile visitors, ad engagers and pixel events, and it supports dynamic product ads from a catalog. Warm TikTok audiences tend to be smaller than on Meta for most DTC brands, so many accounts get more from letting broad campaigns find warm users and using Spark Ads to rerun the creator content that already earned engagement.

On Google, retargeting is called remarketing. Dynamic remarketing shows shoppers the exact products they viewed, pulled from your Merchant Center feed, across display, YouTube and Gmail placements. Performance Max uses your audience signals, including customer lists and site visitors, to find people across Google surfaces, and it will happily spend on warm users unless you set it up to favour new customers. Branded search is a form of retargeting too: people searching your name have already decided to look for you.

The same rule applies on every platform. Treat warm spend as a cost of closing demand that other channels created, cap it, and judge it on whether total new customer orders go up.`},{id:`email-sms-retargeting`,h2:`Email and SMS: the cheapest retargeting`,content:`The best retargeting most DTC brands have is not an ad. A browse abandonment email, a cart abandonment SMS and a checkout reminder reach the same high-intent shoppers as paid retargeting for a fraction of the cost, because you are not buying the impression. They also work when ad tracking misses the visit.

So build the owned layer first. Capture emails and phone numbers early with a clear reason to sign up, make sure Klaviyo receives viewed product, added to cart and started checkout events from Shopify, and run the core abandonment flows before scaling paid retargeting. Then use paid retargeting to reach the people email cannot: visitors who never gave you an address, and engagers who never reached the site.

When we audit accounts, an oversized retargeting budget sitting on top of weak email flows is one of the most common patterns. Fixing the flows and cutting the warm budget usually frees money for prospecting, which is the part that actually grows the brand. Our guide to ecommerce email flows covers the timing and message count for each flow.`}],faqs:[{question:`What percentage of ad spend should go to retargeting?`,answer:`There is no fixed number, but most scaling DTC accounts keep warm spend to a small minority of the budget and push the rest into prospecting. Set a cap, review it weekly and lower it if holdout tests or blended MER show little lift. If retargeting creeps up while new customer orders fall, the account is shrinking, not growing.`},{question:`Do I still need separate retargeting campaigns on Meta?`,answer:`Often not as many as before. Broad and Advantage+ sales campaigns already reach warm users when they are likely to buy. Many accounts run one small retargeting ad set for high-intent stages such as add to cart, with its own creative, and cap existing customer spend inside Advantage+ so new customer acquisition stays the priority.`},{question:`What retargeting window should a DTC brand use?`,answer:`Use short windows for high intent and longer windows for low intent: roughly 3 to 14 days for add to cart, 7 to 30 days for site visitors and 30 to 60 days for social engagers. Stores with lower traffic need longer windows to reach a usable audience. Match past buyer windows to your real reorder cycle.`},{question:`Why is my retargeting ROAS so much higher than prospecting?`,answer:`Because retargeting audiences are full of people who were already likely to buy, and the platform credits the ad if they saw or clicked it within the attribution window. Some of those orders would have happened anyway. Run a holdout test or compare blended MER before moving budget away from prospecting based on that number.`},{question:`Is email or paid retargeting better for cart abandoners?`,answer:`Start with email and SMS, because you already own those channels and they cost far less per message. Paid retargeting adds value for shoppers who never gave you contact details and for low-intent visitors. Test both together with a short delay on the paid ads so you can see whether the ads add orders on top of the flows.`}]},{slug:`how-to-scale-a-dtc-brand`,title:`How to Scale a DTC Brand: A Stage-by-Stage Playbook`,titleTag:`How to Scale a DTC Brand: Stage by Stage | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`How to scale a DTC brand in stages: prove product-market fit, fix unit economics, add channels, build retention, then plan team and cash flow.`,primaryKeyword:`how to scale a dtc brand`,secondaryKeywords:[`scaling a dtc brand`,`dtc growth strategy`,`scale ecommerce brand`,`dtc unit economics`,`dtc channel expansion`,`dtc cash flow`],publishDate:`2026-11-06`,updatedDate:`2026-11-06`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Strategy & Scaling`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/how-to-scale-facebook-ads`,title:`How to Scale Facebook Ads`,text:`Budget steps and structure once Meta is profitable.`},{href:`/tools/break-even-roas-calculator`,title:`Break-Even ROAS Calculator`,text:`Find the ROAS your margins need before you raise spend.`},{href:`/glossary/contribution-margin`,title:`What Is Contribution Margin?`,text:`The number that tells you whether growth is making money.`},{href:`/growth`,title:`Growth Engine`,text:`Meta, TikTok, Google and CRO run as one scaling system.`}],author:i,directAnswer:`How to scale a DTC brand comes down to fixing one constraint at a time: prove product-market fit, make unit economics repeatable so each new customer is profitable, then expand channels, build retention to raise lifetime value, and plan team and cash flow so growth does not outrun inventory or working capital. Skipping a stage usually stalls everything after it.`,definitionBox:{term:`Scaling a DTC brand`,definition:`Growing revenue from direct-to-consumer sales faster than costs grow, so that each additional dollar of marketing and operations produces profitable new customers rather than just more volume.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-scaling-means`,text:`What scaling a DTC brand actually means`},{id:`scaling-stages-table`,text:`The five scaling stages at a glance`},{id:`product-market-fit`,text:`Stage 1: Product-market fit`},{id:`repeatable-unit-economics`,text:`Stage 2: Repeatable unit economics`},{id:`channel-expansion`,text:`Stage 3: Channel expansion`},{id:`retention-and-ltv`,text:`Stage 4: Retention and lifetime value`},{id:`team-and-cash-flow`,text:`Stage 5: Team and cash flow`},{id:`common-scaling-mistakes`,text:`Common scaling mistakes`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-scaling-means`,h2:`What scaling a DTC brand actually means`,content:`Most founders asking how to scale a DTC brand mean one thing: how do I spend more on ads without the numbers falling apart. That is part of it, but ad spend is usually the last lever, not the first. Revenue that grows while contribution margin shrinks is not scaling, it is buying a bigger problem.

Real scaling happens when the brand can add customers profitably and keep them. That depends on a chain of things: a product people want and come back for, margins that leave room to pay for acquisition, creative that keeps finding new buyers, more than one channel, and enough cash and people to fulfil the orders. Each link becomes the constraint at a different point in a brand's life.

After 12+ years in ecommerce and $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, the pattern we see most often is a brand trying to solve a stage 2 problem with a stage 3 tactic, such as launching on TikTok or Google when Meta is unprofitable because the margins do not work. This guide walks through each stage, what to fix and what to measure, so you can work on the constraint that is actually holding you back.`},{id:`scaling-stages-table`,h2:`The five scaling stages at a glance`,content:`Use this table to locate your brand. Most brands are mostly in one stage with a little overlap into the next. Work on the earliest stage where the measure is still weak.`,table:{headers:[`Stage`,`Main constraint`,`What to fix`,`What to measure`],rows:[[`1. Product-market fit`,`Demand is unproven`,`Offer, positioning, product page, reviews`,`Conversion rate, repeat rate, unprompted reviews`],[`2. Repeatable unit economics`,`Each customer costs too much`,`Margins, AOV, tracking, creative testing`,`Contribution margin per order, break-even ROAS, CAC`],[`3. Channel expansion`,`One channel caps out`,`New channels, new audiences, more creative volume`,`MER, new customer share, CAC by channel`],[`4. Retention and LTV`,`Customers buy once`,`Email and SMS flows, subscriptions, cross-sell`,`Repeat purchase rate, 90 and 180 day LTV`],[`5. Team and cash flow`,`Operations and cash`,`Inventory planning, hiring, agency roles`,`Cash conversion cycle, stockouts, payback period`]]}},{id:`product-market-fit`,h2:`Stage 1: Product-market fit`,content:`Before scaling, you need evidence that strangers want the product at a price that works. The signs are practical: people buy from cold traffic without heavy discounts, they leave reviews without being chased, some come back to reorder and the comments under your ads ask about the product rather than argue with it.

If those signs are missing, more spend just buys more expensive proof that the offer is not ready. Fix the basics first. Sharpen the positioning so the ad and the product page make one clear promise. Make the product page answer the questions buyers actually ask. Test the offer: bundles, a starter kit, a guarantee or a price point that changes perceived value.

This is also where tracking needs to be right. Without Conversions API and clean purchase events, you cannot tell whether a test worked. The typical clients we take on already have product-market fit and are doing about $20K a month or more, because before that point the work is mostly product and offer, not media buying.`},{id:`repeatable-unit-economics`,h2:`Stage 2: Repeatable unit economics`,content:`Once demand is proven, the question becomes whether you can buy customers profitably, again and again. Start with contribution margin per order: revenue minus product cost, shipping, payment fees, returns and discounts. From that you get break-even ROAS, the minimum return each ad dollar must produce before you start losing money on the first order.

Then work on both sides of the equation. Raise what each order is worth with bundles, volume tiers and post-purchase upsells. Lower what each customer costs with better creative, which is the biggest lever on Meta and TikTok today, and a store that converts more of the traffic you already pay for. One clinical skincare store we worked with reached a 5.25 percent conversion rate in a $61,000 month after product page and checkout work, which changed what it could afford to spend per click.

Creative testing becomes a system at this stage. Our partner brands typically get 30 to 50+ new ad creatives and angles a month, built from a hook and angle matrix so each test teaches something. Our guides on Meta ads creative testing and lowering CAC go deeper on both.`,list:[`Know your break-even ROAS and target ROAS before raising budget.`,`Track contribution margin weekly, not only revenue and ROAS.`,`Test offers and AOV levers alongside creative, not after.`]},{id:`channel-expansion`,h2:`Stage 3: Channel expansion`,content:`When Meta is profitable and stable, scale it first. It is usually cheaper to grow a channel that already works than to open a new one. Raise budgets in steps of about 20 to 30 percent, use cost caps where volume allows and keep feeding new creative so the account does not fatigue. Our guide on how to scale Facebook ads covers the budget mechanics.

Add channels when the main one starts to cap out: CAC rising faster than budget, frequency climbing, or new customer share falling. The usual next steps for DTC brands are TikTok, through in-feed ads, Spark Ads and TikTok Shop with creator affiliates, and Google, through Shopping, Performance Max and brand search to capture the demand that social creates. New markets such as the UK, Canada or Australia are another form of expansion once fulfilment and currency are sorted.

Some of our case results came from this stage. A UK skincare brand generated EUR 118,000 in its first month with us, and one DTC brand reached $2.3M in sales at 11.6x ROAS on $200K of Meta spend over a year. Neither number is typical, and both depended on the earlier stages already working. Judge expansion on blended MER and total new customers, not on each platform's own report.`},{id:`retention-and-ltv`,h2:`Stage 4: Retention and lifetime value`,content:`Acquisition gets harder as you scale because you move past the easiest buyers. Retention is what lets you keep paying more for each new customer without losing money, because each one is worth more over time.

Start with the owned channels. Klaviyo email and SMS flows (welcome, abandonment, post-purchase, replenishment and win-back) should be live and tested before you push spend hard. Then add structural reasons to return: subscriptions or subscribe and save for consumables, refill reminders timed to real usage, a product range that gives buyers a logical second purchase, and loyalty perks that cost less than a discount.

Measure LTV by cohort, for example the revenue per customer 90 and 180 days after their first order, and compare it with what you paid to acquire them. When that ratio improves, you can afford higher CAC targets, which means more room to scale on paid social. Our LTV calculator and the ecommerce email flows guide are good starting points.`},{id:`team-and-cash-flow`,h2:`Stage 5: Team and cash flow`,content:`Many profitable DTC brands stall not because the ads stop working but because the business runs out of cash or capacity. Inventory has to be paid for weeks or months before it sells, ad platforms bill quickly and a strong month can empty the warehouse. Plan inventory against your spend plan, keep a buffer for peak periods such as BFCM and Q4, and know your payback period so you can see how long each new customer takes to return their acquisition cost.

The team question is about roles, not headcount. Someone has to own media buying, creative production, the store, retention and operations. Early on that is often the founder plus freelancers. As spend grows, brands usually choose between hiring in-house specialists and working with an agency, or a mix. Our comparison of agency versus in-house media buyers covers the trade-offs, and the guide to DTC agency costs explains the common pricing models.

Scaling also raises the cost of mistakes. A tracking error at $2,000 a day is annoying; at $20,000 a day it is expensive. Weekly reporting on contribution margin, MER and cash keeps decisions grounded as volume rises.`},{id:`common-scaling-mistakes`,h2:`Common scaling mistakes`,content:`These are the patterns we see most often when brands come to us for a Brand Growth Audit. Each one is fixable, but each one also wastes months if it goes unnoticed.`,list:[`Raising budgets based on platform ROAS while contribution margin quietly falls.`,`Launching new channels to escape a creative or offer problem on the main channel.`,`Running the same few ads for months and blaming the algorithm when performance drops.`,`Leaning on discounts to hit revenue targets, which trains customers to wait for sales.`,`Ignoring retention until acquisition costs force the issue.`,`Ordering inventory without linking it to the ad spend plan, leading to stockouts at peak.`,`Measuring each channel in isolation instead of tracking blended MER and new customer growth.`]}],faqs:[{question:`When is a DTC brand ready to scale?`,answer:`When cold traffic converts at a price that leaves a healthy contribution margin, customers leave reviews and some reorder, and tracking is reliable enough to trust test results. If you need deep discounts to sell or every campaign is below break-even ROAS, fix the offer, product page and margins before raising spend.`},{question:`What is the fastest way to scale a DTC brand?`,answer:`Scale the channel that already works before adding new ones, and feed it more creative. For most DTC brands that means Meta, with budgets raised in steps of about 20 to 30 percent and a steady supply of new ads. Speed comes from fixing the current constraint, not from launching everything at once.`},{question:`How much should a DTC brand spend on ads to scale?`,answer:`Work backwards from margins instead of picking a number. Calculate break-even ROAS from contribution margin, set a target CAC that your first order or 90 day LTV can support, then raise spend while blended MER stays above target. Our typical clients spend at least $10K to $20K a month on paid ads when we start.`},{question:`Should I add TikTok or Google before scaling Meta further?`,answer:`Usually not. If Meta is profitable but not yet capped, more creative and budget there is cheaper than opening a new channel. Add TikTok or Google when Meta CAC rises faster than spend, frequency climbs or new customer share drops. Google brand search and Shopping also help capture demand that social ads create.`},{question:`Why does ROAS drop when I scale?`,answer:`As budgets grow, platforms reach beyond your most likely buyers, so each extra dollar finds slightly less ready customers. Creative fatigue and audience overlap add to it. Some decline is normal. Judge it against break-even ROAS and blended MER, and offset it with new creative, better conversion rate, higher AOV and stronger retention.`},{question:`Do I need an agency to scale a DTC brand?`,answer:`No, but you need the skills an agency provides: media buying, creative production, CRO, retention and reporting. Some brands hire in-house, others use an agency or a mix. The right choice depends on spend level, how fast you need results and whether you can recruit and manage specialists yourself.`}]},{slug:`choose-google-ads-agency-dtc`,title:`How to Choose a Google Ads Agency for DTC Brands`,titleTag:`Choosing a Google Ads Agency for DTC Brands | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`How to pick a Google Ads agency for DTC brands: what they should run, questions to ask, red flags, pricing models and a checklist for evaluation.`,primaryKeyword:`google ads agency for dtc brands`,secondaryKeywords:[`best google ads agency for dtc ecommerce`,`google shopping agency`,`performance max agency`,`google ads for shopify brands`,`ecommerce ppc agency`,`merchant center feed management`],publishDate:`2026-11-06`,updatedDate:`2026-11-06`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Growth Channels`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/google-shopping-ads-agency`,title:`Google Shopping Ads Agency`,text:`Shopping, Performance Max and feed work for Shopify brands.`},{href:`/compare/meta-ads-vs-google-ads`,title:`Meta Ads vs Google Ads`,text:`How the two channels differ and where each fits for DTC.`},{href:`/blog/how-much-does-a-dtc-agency-cost`,title:`How Much Does a DTC Agency Cost?`,text:`Retainers, percentage of spend and performance fees explained.`},{href:`/blog/performance-marketing-agency`,title:`What Is a Performance Marketing Agency?`,text:`What to expect from any performance agency and how to choose one.`}],author:i,directAnswer:`The right Google Ads agency for DTC brands runs Shopping, Performance Max, brand and non-brand search and YouTube from a well-maintained Merchant Center feed, reports on contribution margin and new customers rather than platform ROAS alone, gives you a senior strategist with a manageable client load, and works on terms that let you leave if results do not come.`,definitionBox:{term:`DTC Google Ads agency`,definition:`An agency that manages Google advertising for direct-to-consumer ecommerce brands, usually covering Shopping and Performance Max campaigns, product feed management, search, YouTube and measurement tied to store revenue and margin.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-a-google-ads-agency-should-do`,text:`What a Google Ads agency for DTC brands should do`},{id:`feed-management`,text:`Why feed management matters most`},{id:`brand-vs-non-brand`,text:`Brand vs non-brand search`},{id:`questions-to-ask`,text:`Questions to ask before you sign`},{id:`red-flags`,text:`Red flags to watch for`},{id:`pricing-models`,text:`How Google Ads agencies charge`},{id:`google-and-meta`,text:`How Google fits with Meta`},{id:`evaluation-checklist`,text:`Agency evaluation checklist`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-a-google-ads-agency-should-do`,h2:`What a Google Ads agency for DTC brands should do`,content:`A good Google Ads agency for DTC brands does more than set up campaigns and send a monthly report. Google is mostly a demand capture channel for ecommerce: people search for a product type, compare prices in Shopping results or look up your brand after seeing an ad elsewhere. The agency's job is to show up for the searches that lead to profitable orders, with the right product, at a bid your margins can carry.

We will not rank or name other agencies here. Instead, this guide sets out what the work should include, what to ask and what to avoid, so you can judge any agency, including ours, on the same terms. At minimum, the scope should cover these campaign types.`,list:[`Shopping campaigns, standard or within Performance Max, built from your Merchant Center product feed.`,`Performance Max, set up with clear asset groups, audience signals and product groupings by margin or category.`,`Brand search, protected and kept cheap, reported separately from everything else.`,`Non-brand search for category, problem and competitor-adjacent terms, with tight negative keyword lists.`,`YouTube and Demand Gen for reach and remarketing, using the same creative thinking that works on social.`,`Conversion tracking through Google tags, enhanced conversions and a clean link to Shopify orders.`]},{id:`feed-management`,h2:`Why feed management matters most`,content:`For Shopping and Performance Max, the product feed is the targeting. Google decides which searches to show your products for largely from the titles, descriptions, product types, images and attributes in Merchant Center. A feed that copies Shopify defaults, with short titles and missing attributes, limits what the campaigns can do no matter how they are structured.

Feed work an agency should be doing includes rewriting titles to lead with the words shoppers search (product type, key attribute, size or colour, then brand where it helps), filling Google product categories and custom labels, fixing disapprovals, keeping prices and stock in sync and adding supplementary feeds for promotions. Custom labels are especially useful for DTC brands because they let you split campaigns by margin, best sellers or seasonality, so budget follows profit rather than revenue.

Ask any agency to show you a before and after of a feed they have improved. If the answer is that they mostly leave the feed alone, the Shopping side of your account will run below its potential.`},{id:`brand-vs-non-brand`,h2:`Brand vs non-brand search`,content:`Brand search, people typing your brand name, nearly always shows a very high ROAS. That is because many of those buyers already planned to purchase after seeing your Meta or TikTok ads, a creator post or a friend's recommendation. Brand campaigns are worth running to protect your name from competitors bidding on it, but they say little about the agency's skill.

Non-brand search and Shopping are where an agency earns its fee: reaching people who do not know you yet. Insist that reports split brand and non-brand performance, and that Performance Max is set up so it does not quietly fill its results with branded searches. Brand exclusions and separate brand campaigns help with this.

A useful question is: what share of new customers came from non-brand traffic last month, and what did each one cost? An agency that can answer clearly is managing growth. One that only quotes blended account ROAS may be taking credit for demand your other channels created.`},{id:`questions-to-ask`,h2:`Questions to ask before you sign`,content:`Use these in your first calls. Good agencies answer them directly and specifically. Vague answers or a quick pivot to a case study slide are worth noting.`,list:[`Who will run my account day to day, and how many other brands do they manage?`,`What will you change in our Merchant Center feed in the first month?`,`How do you separate brand and non-brand results in reporting?`,`Which metric will you report against: platform ROAS, MER, contribution margin or new customer CAC?`,`How do you structure Performance Max, and how do you stop it relying on branded searches?`,`How do Google results get compared with Meta and TikTok so we do not double count orders?`,`What does the first 90 days look like, and what would make you recommend spending less?`,`What is the notice period, and who owns the ad account, feed and data if we part ways?`]},{id:`red-flags`,h2:`Red flags to watch for`,content:`Some warning signs show up in the sales process; others only appear once the account is running. Watch for both.

An account manager on too many brands is the most common problem. Google accounts need weekly search term reviews, feed fixes and bid changes, and a manager spread across a large roster will do the minimum. Ask the number directly.

Reporting only platform ROAS is another. Google's own conversion numbers include branded searches, view-through conversions from YouTube and orders that Meta also claims. Without brand and non-brand splits and a link to Shopify revenue, you cannot tell whether the agency added anything.

No feed work is a sign that Shopping is being treated as an afterthought. Long lock-in contracts, such as six or twelve months with no performance exit, shift the risk entirely onto you. And an agency that runs ads in its own account rather than yours means you could lose your history and data if you leave. You should always own the Google Ads account, Merchant Center and Analytics property.`},{id:`pricing-models`,h2:`How Google Ads agencies charge`,content:`Google Ads agencies use the same few pricing models as other performance agencies. Each changes the agency's incentives, so pick the one that matches what you care about. We do not publish our own prices because they depend on scope, which we set after a Brand Growth Audit; our guide to DTC agency costs explains the models in more detail.`,table:{headers:[`Model`,`How it works`,`Watch for`],rows:[[`Monthly retainer`,`Fixed fee for an agreed scope`,`Scope creep, or effort dropping once the fee is set`],[`Percentage of ad spend`,`Fee rises with the budget managed`,`Incentive to raise spend rather than efficiency`],[`Performance fee`,`Fee tied to revenue, profit or new customers`,`How the metric is defined and who measures it`],[`Hybrid`,`Base retainer plus a performance or spend component`,`Make sure the variable part rewards the right metric`]]}},{id:`google-and-meta`,h2:`How Google fits with Meta`,content:`For most DTC brands, Meta and TikTok create demand and Google captures it. Someone sees a UGC ad on Instagram, does not buy, then searches the brand or the product type a few days later and clicks a Shopping ad. Both platforms will claim that order. A Google agency that does not understand this will either overrate its own work or argue against the social spend that feeds it.

That is why the Google agency and the Meta team, whether in-house or another agency, need shared reporting. Agree on blended MER and new customer CAC as the numbers that decide budget, and use each platform's report only for optimisation inside that platform. When Meta spend rises, brand search and Shopping volume usually rise too; when it falls, Google often softens a few weeks later.

Google also tends to scale differently. Shopping and search volume is limited by how many people search, so Google often grows more slowly than paid social once the obvious terms are covered. Our Meta ads vs Google ads comparison covers where each channel fits by brand stage, and our Google Shopping ads agency page explains how we run Google alongside Meta for the brands we work with.`},{id:`evaluation-checklist`,h2:`Agency evaluation checklist`,content:`Score each agency you speak with on these points. A strong candidate should be clearly good on most of them, and honest about the ones they do not cover.`,table:{headers:[`Area`,`What good looks like`,`Question to confirm it`],rows:[[`Feed management`,`Titles, categories, custom labels and disapprovals handled monthly`,`Show me a feed you improved`],[`Campaign scope`,`Shopping, PMax, brand, non-brand and YouTube covered`,`Which campaign types will you run for us?`],[`Reporting`,`Brand and non-brand split, tied to Shopify revenue and margin`,`Can I see a sample report?`],[`Account ownership`,`You own Google Ads, Merchant Center and Analytics`,`Whose account will the ads run in?`],[`Team load`,`Senior strategist with a manageable roster`,`How many brands does my manager run?`],[`Cross-channel view`,`Works with Meta and TikTok data, uses MER`,`How do you handle double-counted orders?`],[`Contract terms`,`Short notice period or a performance exit`,`What happens if results miss after 90 days?`],[`DTC experience`,`Shopify and ecommerce accounts, not lead generation`,`Which ecommerce categories have you run?`]]}}],faqs:[{question:`Which Google Ads agencies are best for scaling DTC ecommerce brands?`,answer:`The best fit is an agency that manages your Merchant Center feed, runs Shopping, Performance Max, brand and non-brand search and YouTube, reports on margin and new customers, gives you a senior strategist with a reasonable client load and works on fair terms. Use the evaluation checklist above to compare agencies on the same criteria.`},{question:`Is Performance Max enough for a DTC brand on Google?`,answer:`It can be a strong core campaign, but rarely the whole account. Most DTC brands also need a separate brand search campaign, some non-brand search for high-intent terms and clear product groupings by margin. Without those controls, Performance Max can lean on branded searches and remarketing, which makes results look better than the real growth.`},{question:`How long does it take a Google Ads agency to show results?`,answer:`Expect the first month to go mostly on tracking, feed fixes and campaign structure, with clearer trends in months two and three. Shopping and Performance Max need conversion data to learn. Ask the agency for a 90 day plan with specific checkpoints, and judge progress on non-brand new customers and blended MER.`},{question:`Should my Meta agency also run Google Ads?`,answer:`It helps when one team sees both channels, because Meta often creates the demand that Google captures and both claim the same orders. A single agency can set budgets on blended MER. If you use separate agencies, require shared reporting and agree which numbers decide budget so neither side overstates its results.`},{question:`What should a DTC brand spend on Google Ads?`,answer:`It depends on search demand for your category and brand, not a fixed percentage. Brands with strong social spend usually need enough Google budget to cover brand search and core Shopping terms, then expand into non-brand as long as new customer CAC stays within target. Search volume sets a ceiling that paid social does not have.`}]}],o={name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},s=[{slug:`black-friday-discount-strategy`,title:`Black Friday Discount Strategy: How Deep Can a DTC Brand Afford to Go?`,titleTag:`Black Friday Discount Strategy for DTC | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`A Black Friday discount strategy built on contribution margin: how deep you can go, break-even ROAS after the offer and better options than 40% off.`,primaryKeyword:`black friday discount strategy`,secondaryKeywords:[`how much to discount for black friday`,`black friday discount margin`,`break-even roas after discount`,`bfcm offer ideas`,`black friday bundles`,`cyber monday discount strategy`],publishDate:`2026-11-09`,updatedDate:`2026-11-09`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Strategy & Scaling`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/tools/discount-break-even-calculator`,title:`Discount Break-Even Calculator`,text:`Enter your price, costs and discount to see margin and break-even ROAS.`},{href:`/blog/bfcm-meta-ads-playbook`,title:`BFCM Meta Ads Playbook`,text:`Campaign structure, timeline and budget split for the peak week.`},{href:`/bfcm-ads-management`,title:`BFCM Ads Management`,text:`Have us plan the offer, creative and Meta campaigns for Q4.`},{href:`/blog/q4-ecommerce-ads-strategy`,title:`Q4 Ecommerce Ads Strategy`,text:`How to prepare the whole quarter around the peak weekend.`}],author:o,directAnswer:`A Black Friday discount strategy should start from contribution margin, not from what competitors are offering. Work out profit per order before and after the discount, then how many extra orders and what ROAS you need to stay ahead. Often a moderate discount, a bundle or a gift with purchase earns more than a deep percentage off.`,definitionBox:{term:`Black Friday discount strategy`,definition:`The plan for which offer a brand runs over Black Friday and Cyber Monday, how deep it goes, who sees it first and when it ends, set so that the extra orders more than replace the margin the discount gives away.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`start-with-margin`,text:`Why your discount starts with margin`},{id:`discount-margin-math`,text:`The contribution margin math behind a discount`},{id:`worked-example`,text:`Worked example: 0 to 40 percent off an $80 order`},{id:`how-deep-to-discount`,text:`How deep a discount you can actually afford`},{id:`alternatives-to-deep-discounts`,text:`Alternatives to deep percentage discounts`},{id:`meta-cpa-roas-targets`,text:`How the discount changes CPA and ROAS targets on Meta`},{id:`email-sms-first`,text:`Send the offer to email and SMS first`},{id:`after-cyber-monday`,text:`What to do after Cyber Monday`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`start-with-margin`,h2:`Why your Black Friday discount strategy starts with margin`,content:`Most founders pick a Black Friday discount strategy by looking at what everyone else is doing. A competitor runs 30 percent off, so 30 percent feels like the minimum, and 40 percent feels like the way to win. The problem is that a discount does not come out of revenue in a neat proportion. It comes straight out of the money left after product, shipping and fees, which is a much smaller number. A 25 percent discount can remove close to half of the profit on an order.

That is why the right question is not "what discount will people respond to?" but "how much margin do I have, and how many extra orders do I need to replace what the discount removes?" Once you can answer that for your own products, the choice between 15, 25 and 40 percent stops being a guess.

This guide covers the offer itself: the math, the alternatives and how the offer changes your ad targets. For campaign structure, launch timing and the creative checklist for the peak week, use our BFCM Meta ads playbook. In 2026, Black Friday falls on November 27 and Cyber Monday on November 30, so there is still time to run these numbers before the offer is locked.`},{id:`discount-margin-math`,h2:`The contribution margin math behind a discount`,content:`Contribution margin per order is what is left from an order after the costs that only exist because the order happened: the product, getting it to the customer and the payment fee. Ad cost is left out here on purpose, because the point is to find out how much you can afford to pay for each order. Five formulas cover everything you need.`,list:[`Margin before discount = price minus COGS minus shipping minus (price times the payment fee rate).`,`Discounted price = price times (1 minus the discount rate).`,`Margin after discount = discounted price minus COGS minus shipping minus (discounted price times the payment fee rate).`,`Orders needed for the same profit = margin before discount divided by margin after discount.`,`Break-even ROAS after discount = discounted price divided by margin after discount.`],subsections:[{h3:`Why the fee moves but COGS does not`,content:`Payment processing fees are a percentage of what the customer pays, so they shrink a little when the price drops. COGS and shipping do not. You still buy the same product and send the same parcel whether the customer paid full price or 40 percent less. That fixed chunk of cost is why margin falls much faster than price as the discount gets deeper.`}]},{id:`worked-example`,h2:`Worked example: 0 to 40 percent off an $80 order`,content:`This is an example, not a benchmark. It uses an $80 order with $24 COGS, $9 shipping and fulfilment and a 3 percent payment fee. At full price, the fee is $2.40, so the contribution margin is $44.60 per order. Here is what happens at three common discount levels.`,table:{headers:[`Discount (example)`,`Price paid`,`Contribution margin per order`,`Break-even ROAS`,`Orders needed for same profit`],rows:[[`0 percent`,`$80.00`,`$44.60`,`1.79x`,`1.00`],[`15 percent`,`$68.00`,`$32.96`,`2.06x`,`1.35`],[`25 percent`,`$60.00`,`$25.20`,`2.38x`,`1.77`],[`40 percent`,`$48.00`,`$13.56`,`3.54x`,`3.29`]]},list:[`At 15 percent off, the price falls by 15 percent but margin per order falls by about 26 percent, so you need 1.35 orders for every one you would have sold at full price.`,`At 25 percent off, you need 1.77 times the orders to make the same contribution profit, before any ad cost.`,`At 40 percent off, you need 3.29 times the orders, and the ROAS needed just to break even rises from 1.79x to 3.54x.`]},{id:`how-deep-to-discount`,h2:`How deep a discount you can actually afford`,content:`Read the last column of the table as a demand test. A 40 percent discount only pays off if the offer more than triples order volume compared with what the same traffic would buy at full price. Some brands do see that kind of lift on hero products with strong pent-up demand, but most should not plan around it. A 15 to 25 percent offer needs a far smaller lift to work, which is a much safer bet when ad costs are also rising.

Run the numbers per product, not per store. A product with high COGS and heavy shipping can lose money at a discount that is comfortable on a lighter, higher-margin product. Many brands end up with a mix: a strong offer on the products where margin can carry it and a smaller offer, or a bundle, on the rest.

Also count the orders you would have had anyway. Customers who were going to buy in late November at full price will happily take the discount, and that lost margin is real even though no report shows it. The deeper the offer, the more of those orders you pay away. If you want to check your own numbers quickly, our discount break-even calculator runs the same formulas for any price, cost and discount.`},{id:`alternatives-to-deep-discounts`,h2:`Alternatives to deep percentage discounts`,content:`A strong Black Friday offer does not have to be a big number off everything. These options often feel just as generous to the customer while protecting more margin, because they raise order value or use costs you control.`,subsections:[{h3:`Bundles`,content:`Bundle two or three products that are often bought together at a price below buying them separately. The customer gets a clear saving, while your shipping and fee cost is spread across a bigger order, so margin per order often ends up higher than a straight percentage off a single item.`},{h3:`Gift with purchase`,content:`A free item added to the order costs you its COGS, not its retail price. A sample size, accessory or slow-moving product can carry a high perceived value at a low real cost. Make the gift visible in the creative and on the product page, not just at checkout.`},{h3:`Tiered spend thresholds`,content:`Offers such as a smaller saving above one spend level and a bigger saving above a higher one push customers to add items. Set the tiers just above your current average order value so the discount is earned by a bigger basket.`},{h3:`Free shipping thresholds`,content:`If shipping is a large cost line, a free shipping threshold set above your normal order value both removes a common checkout objection and lifts basket size. It can stack with a modest percentage offer.`},{h3:`Early access for subscribers`,content:`Give email and SMS subscribers the offer a day or two before it goes public. It rewards the list, grows sign-ups in the weeks before and moves orders to your cheapest channel. One of our published results, $112,866 in 13 days on $28,391 of spend, came from a brand that launched early BFCM offers.`}]},{id:`meta-cpa-roas-targets`,h2:`How the discount changes CPA and ROAS targets on Meta`,content:`Your contribution margin per order is the most you can pay to acquire that order and break even. In the example, that is $44.60 at full price but only $25.20 at 25 percent off. If your normal cost per purchase on Meta is $30, it is comfortably profitable at full price and loses money during the sale unless the offer lowers it.

That can happen, because a good offer usually lifts conversion rate. In this example, getting a $30 CPA down to $25.20 at the same cost per click needs a conversion rate about 19 percent higher just to break even. Ad costs also tend to rise in late November as more brands compete for the same placements, so that lift has to cover both the thinner margin and pricier traffic.

So before the sale starts, reset every target in the account to the discounted numbers. Update cost caps and bid caps to the new break-even CPA minus the profit you want per order. Change the ROAS you judge campaigns against, because 2.0x might be profitable at full price in this example and unprofitable at 25 percent off. Our guides to what ROAS is and what a good ROAS for ecommerce looks like explain how to set those targets. The budget split and campaign structure for the week itself are in the BFCM Meta ads playbook.`},{id:`email-sms-first`,h2:`Send the offer to email and SMS first`,content:`The cheapest Black Friday orders come from people who already know you. Email and SMS reach them without paying for the impression, which matters most in the one week when paid traffic is most expensive. Plan the owned channel sequence before you plan the ads.

A typical sequence starts with a teaser and a sign-up push in the weeks before, then early access for subscribers, the public launch, reminders over the weekend, a Cyber Monday message and a clear last call. Segment it: past buyers can get cross-sell bundles rather than the acquisition offer on what they already own, and engaged non-buyers get the main offer with the strongest proof.

Run paid ads mostly to new customers and to warm visitors who never gave you an email or phone number. Exclude recent purchasers from discount ads where you can, so you are not paying to show a discount to people who would open the email anyway.`},{id:`after-cyber-monday`,h2:`What to do after Cyber Monday`,content:`End the offer when you said it would end. Extending a sale again and again teaches customers that the deadline is not real, which weakens every future promotion. If you want a softer landing, move to a smaller offer such as a gift with purchase for the shipping cutoff period rather than keeping the full discount live.

Then reset the ad account. Return cost caps, bid caps and ROAS targets to full-price margins, swap discount creative for evergreen and gifting creative, and expect conversion rate to drop for a few days as shoppers pause. The new customers you acquired are the real return on the sale, so move them into a post-purchase flow with product education, a review request and a timed second-purchase offer that is smaller than the Black Friday one.

Finally, write down the real numbers while they are fresh: orders, AOV, contribution margin after the discount, ad spend and how many buyers were new. That record makes next year's discount decision a calculation instead of a guess. If you would like us to plan the offer and run the campaigns, see our BFCM ads management service.`}],faqs:[{question:`How much should a DTC brand discount for Black Friday?`,answer:`Work it out from your contribution margin. Calculate profit per order at full price and at each discount you are considering, then how many extra orders each one needs to earn the same profit. In our $80 example, 25 percent off needs 1.77 times the orders and 40 percent off needs 3.29 times. Choose the deepest offer your realistic demand can carry.`},{question:`Is 40 percent off ever worth it for Black Friday?`,answer:`It can be when margins are very high or when the goal is clearing stock or acquiring customers with strong repeat purchase rates. For most products with meaningful COGS and shipping, a 40 percent discount needs several times the normal order volume just to match full-price profit, and the break-even ROAS rises sharply. Test the math first.`},{question:`How do I calculate break-even ROAS after a discount?`,answer:`Divide the discounted price by the contribution margin after the discount. Contribution margin is the discounted price minus COGS, shipping and the payment fee on the discounted price. In the example in this guide, an $80 order at 25 percent off sells for $60, leaves $25.20 of margin and needs a 2.38x ROAS to break even.`},{question:`What are good Black Friday offers besides a percentage discount?`,answer:`Bundles, a gift with purchase, tiered savings based on spend, a free shipping threshold above your average order value and early access for email and SMS subscribers. These often feel generous to customers while protecting more margin, because they raise order value or cost you the product cost of a gift rather than a cut in price.`},{question:`Should I change my Meta cost caps during Black Friday?`,answer:`Yes. A discount lowers the most you can pay for an order, so cost caps, bid caps and ROAS targets set for full-price margins will let campaigns overspend during the sale. Reset them to the discounted break-even numbers before launch, then set them back to full-price targets as soon as the offer ends after Cyber Monday.`},{question:`When should a Black Friday sale end?`,answer:`On the date you announced, usually the end of Cyber Monday, which is November 30 in 2026. Repeated extensions train customers to ignore deadlines. If you want to keep some momentum into December, switch to a smaller offer such as a gift with purchase and focus messaging on gifting and shipping cutoffs.`}]},{slug:`ecommerce-profit-margin`,title:`Ecommerce Profit Margin: Gross, Contribution and Net Margin per Order`,titleTag:`Ecommerce Profit Margin per Order | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`Ecommerce profit margin explained per order: gross, contribution and net margin, every cost line, a worked example and how margin sets break-even ROAS.`,primaryKeyword:`ecommerce profit margin`,secondaryKeywords:[`dtc profit margin`,`contribution margin per order`,`gross margin vs net margin`,`ecommerce net margin`,`how to increase ecommerce profit margin`,`profit per order`],publishDate:`2026-11-09`,updatedDate:`2026-11-09`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Metrics & Unit Economics`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/cro-agency`,title:`Ecommerce CRO agency`,text:`Product page, offer and checkout testing built for paid traffic.`},{href:`/tools/profit-margin-calculator`,title:`Profit Margin Calculator`,text:`Enter price and costs to see gross, contribution and net margin per order.`},{href:`/glossary/contribution-margin`,title:`What Is Contribution Margin?`,text:`The margin that tells you what each order can afford to spend on ads.`},{href:`/tools/break-even-roas-calculator`,title:`Break-Even ROAS Calculator`,text:`Turn your margin into the ROAS your ads need to make money.`},{href:`/cases/185936-21-days-56-profit-margin-scaled-dropshipping-store-beyond`,title:`$185,936 in 21 Days at 56% Margin`,text:`A store scaled while keeping profit margin in view.`}],author:o,directAnswer:`Ecommerce profit margin is the share of each order you keep after costs. Gross margin subtracts only product cost, contribution margin also subtracts shipping, fulfilment and payment fees, and net margin per order then subtracts ad cost, returns and discounts. Contribution margin is the one that sets your break-even CPA and ROAS.`,definitionBox:{term:`Ecommerce profit margin`,definition:`The percentage of revenue a store keeps as profit. For a DTC brand it is most useful per order, measured at three levels: gross margin (after product cost), contribution margin (after all variable costs of fulfilling the order) and net margin (after ad cost, returns and discounts too).`},tableOfContents:[{id:`why-margin-per-order`,text:`Why measure ecommerce profit margin per order`},{id:`gross-contribution-net`,text:`Gross vs contribution vs net margin`},{id:`cost-lines`,text:`The cost lines that eat your margin`},{id:`worked-example`,text:`Worked example: margin on a $70 order`},{id:`margin-and-break-even-roas`,text:`How margin sets your break-even ROAS`},{id:`how-to-raise-margin`,text:`How to raise your margin`},{id:`margins-in-our-cases`,text:`Profit margins in our published cases`},{id:`track-margin-weekly`,text:`Track margin weekly, not yearly`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`why-margin-per-order`,h2:`Why measure ecommerce profit margin per order`,content:`Ecommerce profit margin is usually discussed as one number on a yearly profit and loss statement. That is useful for your accountant and nearly useless for running ads. By the time the annual figure tells you something went wrong, you have spent months buying orders that lost money.

For a DTC brand, the more useful view is margin per order. Every order carries the same set of costs: the product, getting it to the customer, the payment fee, the ad spend it took to win it and a share of returns and discounts. If you know those numbers for a typical order, you know what you can afford to pay for a customer, which campaigns are really profitable and which levers will move profit the most.

This guide breaks margin into three levels, walks through each cost line, works through a full example and shows how margin turns into the break-even ROAS your ads have to beat. It does not quote industry averages, because margins vary so much by product, price and channel that an average tells you very little about your own store.`},{id:`gross-contribution-net`,h2:`Gross vs contribution vs net margin`,content:`The three margins answer different questions. Gross margin tells you whether the product is priced well against what it costs to make. Contribution margin tells you how much each order leaves to pay for marketing and overhead. Net margin per order tells you what you actually keep once marketing, returns and discounts are paid.`,table:{headers:[`Margin`,`Formula per order`,`What it tells you`],rows:[[`Gross margin`,`(Price minus COGS) divided by price`,`Whether pricing covers product cost with room to spare`],[`Contribution margin (before ads)`,`Price minus COGS, shipping and fulfilment, and payment fees`,`The most you can spend to acquire the order and break even`],[`Net margin per order`,`Contribution margin minus ad cost per order, returns and discounts, divided by price`,`What each order really earns before fixed overhead`]]},list:[`Fixed overhead such as salaries, software and rent sits below all three. It is paid from the total net profit of all orders, not from any single one.`,`Some teams call the figure after ad cost "contribution margin after marketing". The label matters less than using the same definition every week.`]},{id:`cost-lines`,h2:`The cost lines that eat your margin`,content:`Most margin mistakes come from leaving a cost out, not from bad arithmetic. List every line below for your top products, using real invoices and platform reports rather than estimates.`,list:[`COGS: the landed cost of the product, including manufacturing, packaging, inbound freight and duties.`,`Shipping and fulfilment: postage, pick and pack fees, packaging materials and any free shipping you absorb.`,`Payment fees: the percentage and fixed fee your payment processor and any buy now, pay later provider charges per order.`,`Ad cost per order: total ad spend divided by orders, which is your blended cost per acquisition.`,`Returns: refunded revenue, return shipping and any stock you cannot resell, spread across all orders.`,`Discounts: every code, sale price and automatic offer, which come straight off the price before any cost is covered.`]},{id:`worked-example`,h2:`Worked example: margin on a $70 order`,content:`This is an example, not a benchmark. It uses a $70 order with $21 COGS, $8 shipping and fulfilment, a 3 percent payment fee, a $25 ad cost per order and a 6 percent return rate. To keep it simple, returns are treated as full refunds, so the expected return cost per order is 6 percent of $70, or $4.20. There is no discount in this example.`,table:{headers:[`Line (example)`,`Per order`,`Running total`],rows:[[`Price`,`$70.00`,`$70.00`],[`COGS`,`minus $21.00`,`$49.00 (gross margin 70.0%)`],[`Shipping and fulfilment`,`minus $8.00`,`$41.00`],[`Payment fee (3% of $70)`,`minus $2.10`,`$38.90 contribution before ads`],[`Ad cost per order (CPA)`,`minus $25.00`,`$13.90`],[`Returns (6% of $70)`,`minus $4.20`,`$9.70 net profit per order`],[`Net margin`,`$9.70 divided by $70`,`13.9%`]]},subsections:[{h3:`What the example shows`,content:`A 70 percent gross margin looks healthy, yet the order keeps only $9.70, a 13.9 percent net margin. Ad cost is by far the largest line after COGS. That is typical of paid-acquisition DTC brands and is why the ad cost per order deserves as much attention as product cost.`}]},{id:`margin-and-break-even-roas`,h2:`How margin sets your break-even ROAS`,content:`Your contribution margin is the most you can pay in ads for an order before it loses money. Divide price by that margin and you get break-even ROAS: the return on ad spend at which ads exactly pay for themselves.

In the example, contribution before ads is $38.90, so break-even ROAS is $70 divided by $38.90, or 1.80x. If you also take the $4.20 expected return cost out first, the margin available for ads is $34.70 and break-even ROAS rises to 2.02x. The actual ROAS at a $25 CPA is $70 divided by $25, or 2.80x, which is why the order is profitable.

The lower your margin, the higher the ROAS your ads must reach. This is why two brands can run the same ROAS and get very different results: one is profitable and the other loses money on every order. Our break-even ROAS calculator does this for your own numbers, and our guides to what ROAS is and what a good ROAS for ecommerce looks like explain how to set a target above break-even.`},{id:`how-to-raise-margin`,h2:`How to raise your margin`,content:`There are only a few ways to make each order more profitable: earn more per order, spend less to deliver it, spend less to acquire it or earn more from the customer later. These are the levers we work on most often with partner brands.`,subsections:[{h3:`Raise average order value`,content:`Shipping, fulfilment and a large part of acquisition cost are roughly fixed per order, so a bigger basket spreads them further. Post-purchase upsells, cart add-ons and tiered offers all lift AOV without raising your cost to acquire the order.`},{h3:`Use bundles`,content:`Bundles of products that are often bought together raise order value and let you offer a clear saving without discounting single items. They also give you a better offer for paid traffic than a percentage code.`},{h3:`Fix shipping costs`,content:`In the example, $8 of shipping is 11.4 percent of a $70 order. On a $100 order with the same shipping cost, it would be 8 percent. Set a free shipping threshold above your current AOV, renegotiate carrier rates and check packaging size and weight.`},{h3:`Improve conversion rate`,content:`CRO lowers ad cost per order without touching the budget. If the same traffic converts better, every order costs less to acquire. Faster pages, clearer product pages and a simpler checkout are usually where the gains are.`},{h3:`Build retention`,content:`A repeat order through email or SMS carries almost no ad cost, so its net margin is close to its contribution margin. Klaviyo flows for replenishment, cross-sell and win-back raise lifetime margin even when first-order margin is thin.`}]},{id:`margins-in-our-cases`,h2:`Profit margins in our published cases`,content:`Instead of quoting averages, here are three real results from our published case studies where the brand reported its profit margin. Each is a single account and a specific period, and each brand defines margin its own way, so treat them as examples of what is possible, not as targets.

A dropshipping store generated $185,936 in 21 days at a 56 percent profit margin, covered in our case study on scaling that store. Another brand did $148K in one day and a $294K month while ending at a 37 percent net margin, in our case study on its best day. A third spent $26,594 to generate $99,576 in 5 days at a 67 percent profit margin, in our five-day case study.

What these share is that margin was tracked during the scale, not checked afterwards. Spend went up on products and offers where the numbers held, which is the habit this guide is built around. You can find all three on our case studies page.`},{id:`track-margin-weekly`,h2:`Track margin weekly, not yearly`,content:`Build a simple weekly sheet with revenue, orders, COGS, shipping, fees, ad spend, refunds and discounts. Work out contribution margin per order and net profit per order each week, alongside blended ROAS or MER. When net profit per order falls, the sheet shows you which line moved.

Review it per product as well as per store, because a bestseller with thin margins can hide behind a healthy store average. Recheck COGS and shipping whenever a supplier or carrier changes prices, and recheck ad cost per order whenever you raise budget. Our profit margin calculator gives you the per-order figures, and the contribution margin glossary entry explains the definition in more detail.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is a good ecommerce profit margin?`,answer:`There is no single good number, because margin depends on product, price, channel and how much you spend on ads. A better test is whether your contribution margin leaves enough room to acquire customers at your current cost per order and still keep a net profit. Work out your own figures per order instead of comparing with averages.`},{question:`What is the difference between gross margin and contribution margin?`,answer:`Gross margin subtracts only the cost of goods from the price. Contribution margin also subtracts the other variable costs of each order, such as shipping, fulfilment and payment fees. Contribution margin is more useful for ads because it shows the most you can spend to acquire an order before it loses money.`},{question:`How do I calculate net profit margin per order?`,answer:`Start with price, subtract COGS, shipping and fulfilment and payment fees to get contribution margin, then subtract ad cost per order, returns and discounts. Divide what is left by the price. In the $70 example in this guide, net profit is $9.70 per order, a 13.9 percent net margin before fixed overhead.`},{question:`Should returns be included in profit margin?`,answer:`Yes. Returns cost you refunded revenue, return shipping and sometimes stock you cannot resell. Spread the expected cost across all orders using your return rate. A simple method treats returns as full refunds, so a 6 percent return rate on a $70 order adds $4.20 of cost to every order.`},{question:`How does profit margin affect break-even ROAS?`,answer:`Break-even ROAS equals price divided by contribution margin. A lower margin means each order can afford less ad spend, so ads must return more revenue per dollar to break even. In the example in this guide, a $38.90 contribution margin on a $70 order gives a 1.80x break-even ROAS.`},{question:`What is the fastest way to improve ecommerce margin?`,answer:`It depends on which cost line is largest. For most paid-acquisition DTC brands, ad cost per order is the biggest lever, so improving conversion rate and creative often moves margin fastest. Raising AOV with bundles and upsells, setting a smarter free shipping threshold and building repeat purchases through email and SMS follow close behind.`}]}],c={name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},l=[{slug:`meta-ads-learning-phase`,title:`Meta Ads Learning Phase: What It Is, Learning Limited and How to Exit It`,titleTag:`Meta Ads Learning Phase: How to Exit It | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`The Meta ads learning phase explained: the 50 conversions per week rule, what Learning Limited means, what resets learning and how to budget your way out.`,primaryKeyword:`meta ads learning phase`,secondaryKeywords:[`learning limited`,`how long is the facebook learning phase`,`50 conversions per week`,`exit learning phase`,`facebook ads learning phase`,`what resets the learning phase`],publishDate:`2026-11-10`,updatedDate:`2026-11-10`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Meta Ads`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/tools/meta-ads-budget-calculator`,title:`Meta Ads Budget Calculator`,text:`Work out the daily budget your target CPA needs to reach enough purchases each week.`},{href:`/blog/how-to-scale-facebook-ads`,title:`How to Scale Facebook Ads`,text:`Budget steps and structure once an ad set is out of learning and profitable.`},{href:`/blog/meta-advantage-plus-guide`,title:`Meta Advantage+ Guide`,text:`When a consolidated Advantage+ campaign beats many small ad sets.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`Have us restructure the account so ad sets can exit learning.`}],author:c,directAnswer:`The Meta ads learning phase is the period after you launch or significantly edit an ad set when Meta is still working out who to show it to. Meta says an ad set generally needs about 50 optimization events within 7 days of its last significant edit to exit learning. If it is not on track to get there, Meta labels it Learning Limited.`,definitionBox:{term:`Meta ads learning phase`,definition:`The period when Meta's delivery system is exploring the best people, placements and times to show a new or significantly edited ad set. Performance is less stable and cost per result is usually higher until the ad set collects enough optimization events, which Meta puts at roughly 50 in 7 days.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-is-the-learning-phase`,text:`What is the Meta ads learning phase?`},{id:`fifty-conversions-rule`,text:`The 50 conversions per week rule`},{id:`how-long-is-learning`,text:`How long is the Facebook learning phase?`},{id:`learning-limited`,text:`What Learning Limited means`},{id:`what-resets-learning`,text:`What resets the learning phase`},{id:`budget-for-learning`,text:`How to size a budget that can exit learning`},{id:`how-to-exit-learning`,text:`How DTC brands get out of the learning phase`},{id:`scale-without-resets`,text:`How to scale budgets without resetting learning`},{id:`when-learning-is-not-the-problem`,text:`When learning is not the real problem`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-is-the-learning-phase`,h2:`What is the Meta ads learning phase?`,content:`The Meta ads learning phase is the stretch of time after an ad set launches, or after a significant edit, when Meta's delivery system is still testing who to show your ads to. You will see "Learning" in the Delivery column of Ads Manager while it happens.

During this period Meta spends part of your budget exploring different people, placements and times of day, and uses the results to predict who is most likely to take the action you optimize for, such as a purchase. Until it has enough results, delivery is less stable and cost per result is usually higher and more volatile.

Learning happens at the ad set level. Each ad set learns on its own, even when several sit inside the same campaign. That detail matters for DTC brands, because the most common reason an account never leaves learning is a budget split across too many ad sets for any one of them to collect enough data.`},{id:`fifty-conversions-rule`,h2:`The 50 conversions per week rule`,content:`Meta's documented guideline is that an ad set generally needs about 50 optimization events within 7 days of its last significant edit to exit the learning phase. An optimization event is whatever conversion the ad set is set to optimize for. For most ecommerce ad sets that is a purchase, so in practice the rule reads as roughly 50 purchases per ad set per week.

Two parts of that rule are easy to miss. First, it counts events for the ad set, not the campaign or the account. Ten ad sets with 10 purchases each add up to 100 purchases a week, but none of them is close to exiting learning. Second, the 7-day window restarts after every significant edit, so an ad set that keeps getting changed keeps starting over. Meta describes 50 as a guide rather than a hard switch, so treat it as the planning target and check the Meta Business Help Center if your account shows something different.`},{id:`how-long-is-learning`,h2:`How long is the Facebook learning phase?`,content:`The Facebook learning phase lasts until the ad set collects about 50 optimization events, and Meta gives it a 7-day window from the last significant edit to get there. With a large enough budget, an ad set can leave learning in two or three days. With a small budget, it may never leave.

So the honest answer to "how long" is that it depends on your budget and your cost per result, not on the calendar. An ad set spending enough to buy 15 purchases a day will usually exit learning within about four days. An ad set buying three purchases a day will collect about 21 in a week and stay stuck. Plan for this before launch: if your budget cannot buy about 50 events a week, change the structure or the optimization event first.`},{id:`learning-limited`,h2:`What Learning Limited means`,content:`Learning Limited means Meta expects the ad set will not get enough optimization events to exit the learning phase. It is a status, not a penalty. Your ads keep delivering, but Meta is telling you that the ad set is unlikely to reach stable performance with its current setup.

When you hover over the status in Ads Manager, Meta usually suggests a cause. The common ones are:`,list:[`Budget too low for the cost of the optimization event.`,`Audience too small, often from stacked interests, tight age or location limits or heavy exclusions.`,`A cost cap, bid cap or ROAS goal set so tight that Meta cannot win enough auctions.`,`Too many ad sets in the account competing for similar people, so each gets a thin slice of the results.`,`An optimization event that is too rare, such as purchases on a product that sells only a few times a week.`],subsections:[{h3:`Is Learning Limited always bad?`,content:`Not always. A small retargeting ad set or a test ad set with a deliberate budget can sit in Learning Limited and still be profitable. The status only says the ad set is not getting enough events for Meta's system to optimize fully. If the ad set hits your target CPA consistently, you do not have to fix it. If it is your main prospecting ad set and results swing from day to day, you should.`}]},{id:`what-resets-learning`,h2:`What resets the learning phase`,content:`A significant edit resets the learning phase and restarts the 7-day window. Meta treats the following as significant edits:`,list:[`Changing targeting, including audiences, locations, age ranges and Advantage+ audience settings.`,`Changing creative, or adding a new ad to the ad set.`,`Changing the optimization event, for example from purchase to add to cart.`,`Changing the bid strategy or making a large change to a bid, cost cap or ROAS goal.`,`Making a large budget change on the ad set, or on the campaign when you use Advantage campaign budget.`,`Pausing the ad set for 7 days or longer and then turning it back on.`],subsections:[{h3:`What Meta does not publish`,content:`Meta does not publish an exact percentage at which a budget change becomes significant. Advice such as "raise budgets by no more than 20 percent" is a rule of thumb from practitioners, not Meta's rule. Small, spaced changes are less likely to reset learning than one large jump, which is why gradual increases are the safe default. Small edits such as fixing a typo in ad copy may or may not count, so batch your edits and make them together rather than one at a time over several days.`}]},{id:`budget-for-learning`,h2:`How to size a budget that can exit learning`,content:`Set the daily budget of each ad set to at least your expected cost per optimization event, times 50, divided by 7. That is the spend needed to buy about 50 events in a week. If your ad set's budget is below that number, it is unlikely to exit learning.

Daily budget needed = expected CPA x 50 / 7.

Worked example: a DTC brand sells a $60 product and expects a cost per purchase of about $30 from prospecting. $30 times 50 is $1,500 a week, and $1,500 divided by 7 is about $214 a day, so round up to $215. The table shows how different structures with the same CPA compare. These are illustrative numbers, not benchmarks.`,table:{headers:[`Setup (example)`,`Daily budget per ad set`,`Weekly spend per ad set`,`Events per ad set per week`,`Enough to exit learning?`],rows:[[`One ad set optimizing for purchase, $30 CPA`,`$90`,`$630`,`21 purchases`,`No`],[`Four ad sets at $60 a day each, $30 CPA`,`$60`,`$420`,`14 purchases`,`No, in any of them`],[`The same $240 a day in one ad set, $30 CPA`,`$240`,`$1,680`,`56 purchases`,`Yes`],[`Budget from the formula, $30 CPA`,`$215`,`$1,505`,`50 purchases`,`Just, with no buffer`],[`One ad set optimizing for add to cart, $9 per add to cart`,`$65`,`$455`,`50 add to carts`,`Yes, for that event`]]},list:[`The four-ad-set account spends $240 a day and buys about 56 purchases a week in total, but each ad set only sees about 14, so all four stay in learning.`,`Moving the same $240 into one ad set gets that ad set past 50 without spending a dollar more.`,`CPA during learning is usually higher than the stable CPA, so budget 20 to 30 percent above the formula if you can. In this example that is roughly $260 to $280 a day.`],subsections:[{h3:`Check the budget against your margin`,content:`The formula tells you what learning needs. It does not tell you whether that CPA is profitable. Before raising budget to hit 50 a week, check that your expected CPA sits below your break-even CPA. Our break-even ROAS calculator turns your price and costs into that limit, and the Meta ads budget calculator works out the daily budget for a target number of purchases.`}]},{id:`how-to-exit-learning`,h2:`How DTC brands get out of the learning phase`,content:`To exit the learning phase, give each ad set more optimization events per week: fewer ad sets, broader audiences, enough budget and fewer edits. If even that cannot reach 50 a week, temporarily optimize for a more frequent event. These are the moves that work most often in DTC accounts.`,subsections:[{h3:`Consolidate ad sets`,content:`Merge ad sets that target similar people or test small variations. Put several creatives in one ad set instead of one creative per ad set, and let Meta spread delivery across them. Consolidation is the fastest fix because it raises events per ad set without raising total spend, as the worked example shows.`},{h3:`Use broader audiences`,content:`Narrow interest stacks and tight lookalikes limit how many people Meta can reach at a reasonable cost. Broad targeting or Advantage+ audience gives the system more room to find buyers, which usually brings more events for the same budget. Keep exclusions for recent purchasers, but avoid stacking so many conditions that the audience shrinks to a few hundred thousand people.`},{h3:`Optimize for a higher-funnel event for a while`,content:`If your budget cannot buy 50 purchases a week, optimize for an event that happens more often, such as add to cart or initiate checkout, until purchase volume grows. In the example, 50 add to carts a week need about $65 a day instead of $215. The trade-off is that Meta then finds people who add to cart, not necessarily people who buy, so watch cost per purchase closely and switch back to purchase optimization once you can afford it. Remember that switching the event is itself a significant edit.`},{h3:`Stop editing during learning`,content:`Every significant edit restarts the clock. Decide what you will change before launch, then leave the ad set alone for at least three to five days unless it is clearly losing money. Judge results on the full week, not on the first day, when learning makes costs noisy.`},{h3:`Fix tracking first`,content:`If purchases are not reaching Meta reliably, the ad set learns from fewer events than you actually sold. Check that the Meta pixel and the Conversions API are both sending purchase events and that event match quality is healthy before you blame the budget.`}]},{id:`scale-without-resets`,h2:`How to scale budgets without resetting learning`,content:`The safest way to scale without resetting learning is to raise budgets in small, spaced steps on ad sets that are already out of learning and profitable. When you need a bigger jump than that, duplicate or move spend into a consolidated campaign instead of making one large edit to a winner.`,subsections:[{h3:`Gradual budget increases`,content:`Raise the budget of a stable ad set by a modest amount, many teams use about 20 to 30 percent, then wait two or three days before the next step. This is a rule of thumb rather than a Meta threshold, but it keeps each change small enough that delivery usually stays stable. If CPA rises above target after a step, hold the budget there until it settles.`},{h3:`Duplicating an ad set`,content:`Duplicating a winning ad set at a higher budget leaves the original untouched, so its learning is not reset. The copy starts its own learning phase, though, and it competes in the same auctions as the original, which can raise costs for both. Use duplication for one clear step up, not as a habit that fills the account with near-identical ad sets.`},{h3:`Advantage campaign budget and Advantage+ sales campaigns`,content:`With Advantage campaign budget, previously called campaign budget optimization or CBO, you set one budget at campaign level and Meta shifts spend between ad sets. That makes it easier for the strongest ad set to collect enough events. Advantage+ sales campaigns, previously Advantage+ shopping campaigns, go further by consolidating targeting and placements into one unit, which suits brands that want fewer, bigger ad sets. A large change to the campaign budget can still count as a significant edit, so the same rule of gradual steps applies. Our Meta Advantage+ guide covers when this structure fits.`}]},{id:`when-learning-is-not-the-problem`,h2:`When learning is not the real problem`,content:`Sometimes an ad set exits learning and still loses money, or stays in learning because nobody wants the offer. Learning is about how stable delivery is, not about whether the product, price or creative can sell.

If you have consolidated, broadened and funded an ad set properly and CPA is still above break-even, look at the creative, the offer and the landing page before touching structure again. More events will not fix a weak hook or an unclear product page. Our guide to Meta ads creative testing explains how to find new winners without breaking learning on the ads that already work, and our guide to what ROAS is covers how to set the target your ads need to hit.

If you want a second pair of eyes on an account that never seems to leave learning, our Meta ads agency team can review the structure, tracking and budgets and show you where the events are being split.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is the learning phase in Meta ads?`,answer:`It is the period after an ad set launches or is significantly edited when Meta is still testing who to show the ads to. Performance is less stable and cost per result is usually higher. The ad set exits learning once it collects about 50 optimization events within 7 days of its last significant edit.`},{question:`How many conversions do you need to exit the learning phase?`,answer:`Meta's guideline is about 50 optimization events per ad set within 7 days of the last significant edit. For an ad set optimizing for purchases, that means roughly 50 purchases a week in that one ad set. Events in other ad sets in the same campaign do not count toward it.`},{question:`How long is the Facebook learning phase?`,answer:`It lasts until the ad set reaches about 50 optimization events, with a 7-day window from the last significant edit. A well-funded ad set can exit in two or three days. An ad set whose budget buys only a few events a day may never exit and will be labelled Learning Limited.`},{question:`How do I fix Learning Limited?`,answer:`Give the ad set more events per week. Consolidate similar ad sets, broaden the audience, loosen any cost cap or bid cap that is too tight and set the daily budget to at least your expected CPA times 50 divided by 7. If that is not affordable, optimize for a more frequent event such as add to cart for a while.`},{question:`Does changing the budget reset the learning phase?`,answer:`A large budget change can, on the ad set or on the campaign when you use Advantage campaign budget. Meta does not publish an exact threshold. Many advertisers raise budgets by about 20 to 30 percent at a time and wait a few days between steps, which usually keeps delivery stable.`},{question:`Should I duplicate an ad set to scale it?`,answer:`It can be useful for one clear step up, because the original ad set keeps its learning. The duplicate starts learning from scratch and competes with the original in the same auctions. For ongoing scaling, gradual budget increases on the winner or a consolidated Advantage campaign budget setup are usually cleaner.`}]},{slug:`tiktok-shop-vs-shopify`,title:`TikTok Shop vs Shopify: Which Should a DTC Brand Sell On?`,titleTag:`TikTok Shop vs Shopify for DTC Brands | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`TikTok Shop vs Shopify compared on checkout, traffic, fees, customer data, creators and fulfilment, and when a DTC brand should run both together.`,primaryKeyword:`tiktok shop vs shopify`,secondaryKeywords:[`should i sell on tiktok shop or shopify`,`tiktok shop fees`,`tiktok shop or shopify`,`tiktok shopify integration`,`tiktok shop for shopify brands`,`is tiktok shop worth it`],publishDate:`2026-11-10`,updatedDate:`2026-11-10`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Growth Channels`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/tiktok-shop-strategy`,title:`TikTok Shop Strategy for DTC Brands`,text:`Listings, pricing, affiliate creators, LIVE and GMV Max once you decide to sell there.`},{href:`/tiktok-shop-agency`,title:`TikTok Shop Agency`,text:`Have your shop setup, creators and ads run on your real margins.`},{href:`/shopify-agency`,title:`Shopify Agency`,text:`Keep your Shopify store converting as the hub for every channel.`},{href:`/tools/break-even-roas-calculator`,title:`Break-Even ROAS Calculator`,text:`Check what each channel can afford to spend per order.`}],author:c,directAnswer:`TikTok Shop and Shopify are not really either-or. Shopify is your own store: you own the checkout and the customer relationship, but you have to bring the traffic. TikTok Shop is a marketplace inside TikTok: it brings shoppers and creators, but takes a fee per order and controls the customer data. Most DTC brands are best served using Shopify as the hub and TikTok Shop as a sales channel.`,definitionBox:{term:`TikTok Shop vs Shopify`,definition:`A comparison between selling inside TikTok, where shoppers buy without leaving the app and TikTok runs the checkout, and selling on your own Shopify store, where you run the checkout, set the experience and keep the customer relationship but must drive the traffic yourself.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`short-answer`,text:`TikTok Shop vs Shopify: the short answer`},{id:`what-each-is`,text:`What TikTok Shop and Shopify actually are`},{id:`checkout`,text:`Checkout: in the app vs on your own store`},{id:`traffic`,text:`Where the traffic comes from`},{id:`fees`,text:`TikTok Shop fees vs Shopify fees`},{id:`customer-data`,text:`Who owns the customer data`},{id:`creators`,text:`Affiliates and creators`},{id:`fulfilment`,text:`Fulfilment and shipping`},{id:`using-both`,text:`Using both: Shopify as the hub, TikTok Shop as a channel`},{id:`decision-guide`,text:`Decision guide: which should you sell on?`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`short-answer`,h2:`TikTok Shop vs Shopify: the short answer`,content:`If you can only pick one, pick Shopify when you want to own the brand, the customer list and the margin, and pick TikTok Shop when your product sells on impulse from short videos and you need demand more than control. For most DTC brands that already have a store, the better question is not which one but how to run TikTok Shop as a channel without hurting the store.

The two do different jobs. Shopify is infrastructure: a store, a checkout and a back office that you control. TikTok Shop is a marketplace with its own audience, its own checkout and its own rules. Comparing them is closer to comparing your own website with Amazon than comparing two website builders.`},{id:`what-each-is`,h2:`What TikTok Shop and Shopify actually are`,content:`TikTok Shop is an ecommerce marketplace built into the TikTok app, and Shopify is a platform for running your own online store. That difference sits under every point in this comparison.

On TikTok Shop, you list products in TikTok's Seller Center. Shoppers find them in videos, LIVE streams, the Shop tab and search, and pay inside TikTok, which sets the rules on listings, checkout, shipping, returns and customer contact. It is only available in some countries, so check that your market is supported.

On Shopify, you pay for the software and build a store on your own domain. You choose the theme, the checkout settings, the apps and the payment options, and you decide how to talk to customers after they buy. Shopify does not send you shoppers. Traffic comes from your ads, search, email, social and word of mouth.`},{id:`checkout`,h2:`Checkout: in the app vs on your own store`,content:`TikTok Shop checkout happens inside TikTok, while Shopify checkout happens on your store. That is the biggest practical difference for conversion.

A TikTok shopper can watch a video, tap the product and pay without opening a browser. Saved payment and address details make it fast, which suits impulse purchases at lower price points. The downside is that you cannot customize the checkout, add your own upsells in the same way or show your full brand story at the moment of purchase.

A Shopify checkout sends the shopper from the ad or post to your site. That extra step loses some people, especially on mobile and in-app browsers. In return, you control the product page, bundles, post-purchase offers and subscription options, and you can test and improve every step. Brands with higher prices, longer consideration or a strong bundle and subscription model usually do better with that control.`},{id:`traffic`,h2:`Where the traffic comes from`,content:`TikTok Shop can bring you shoppers through TikTok's own feed, search and creators, while a Shopify store gets only the traffic you send it. That is TikTok Shop's main advantage.

On TikTok Shop, a product can get sales from organic videos, from affiliate creators who post about it and from the Shop tab and search inside the app. Paid campaigns, including GMV Max and Spark Ads, can add more. None of that is guaranteed, and organic reach rises and falls with the algorithm, but the potential audience is already in the app and already shopping.

On Shopify, every visitor has a cost or an effort behind it: Meta ads, TikTok ads, Google, SEO, email, influencers. The upside is that traffic you build, such as an email list or search rankings, keeps working regardless of one platform's policy changes. A brand that relies only on TikTok Shop is exposed if the algorithm, the fee structure or the rules change.`},{id:`fees`,h2:`TikTok Shop fees vs Shopify fees`,content:`TikTok Shop mainly charges a referral fee on each sale, a percentage of the order that varies by category and region, while Shopify mainly charges a monthly plan fee plus payment processing. Both change over time, so check TikTok's current seller fee page for your market and Shopify's pricing page before you model margins. We do not quote exact rates here because they differ by country, category and date.

The bigger cost difference is usually not the platform fee itself. On TikTok Shop, creator commission and seller-funded promotions often cost more per order than the referral fee. On Shopify, paying for traffic usually costs more than the subscription and processing combined.`,table:{headers:[`Cost type`,`TikTok Shop`,`Shopify`],rows:[[`Platform fee per sale`,`Referral fee as a percentage of the order, by category and region. Check TikTok's seller fee page.`,`No sales commission when you use Shopify Payments. An extra transaction fee applies if you use a third-party payment provider.`],[`Subscription`,`Check your region's seller terms.`,`Monthly plan fee that depends on the plan you choose.`],[`Payment processing`,`Check whether it is included in the referral fee or charged separately in your region.`,`Card processing rate per order, set by your plan and region.`],[`Creator and affiliate commission`,`Set by you per product, paid on sales creators drive.`,`Only if you run your own affiliate or influencer program.`],[`Promotions`,`Seller-funded discounts and campaign participation.`,`Discounts you choose to run.`],[`Traffic`,`Optional ads such as GMV Max and Spark Ads.`,`You pay for or earn all traffic: ads, SEO, email, social.`],[`Apps and tools`,`Seller Center tools are included.`,`Many brands pay monthly for apps on top of the plan.`]]},subsections:[{h3:`Compare margin per order, not fee rates`,content:`To compare the two fairly, work out contribution margin per order on each channel. For TikTok Shop, subtract product cost, shipping, the referral fee, creator commission and any discount. For Shopify, subtract product cost, shipping, processing and the ad cost it takes to win the order. Our break-even ROAS calculator helps with the Shopify side, and our TikTok Shop strategy guide has a worked example of TikTok Shop margin with assumed rates.`}]},{id:`customer-data`,h2:`Who owns the customer data`,content:`On Shopify you own the customer relationship, while on TikTok Shop the buyer is TikTok's customer first. For a DTC brand that plans to grow through repeat purchases, this is often the deciding factor.

A Shopify order gives you the customer's email, and if they opt in, their phone number for SMS. You can add them to post-purchase flows, ask for reviews, offer a subscription and bring them back with a timed second-purchase offer. That repeat revenue costs very little in ads, which is where much of a DTC brand's lifetime value comes from.

A TikTok Shop order gives you what you need to ship it, but TikTok limits how you can contact or market to that buyer off the platform. Check TikTok's current seller data policy for the details in your region. You can still build a relationship inside TikTok through your account, LIVE streams and follow-up products in your shop, but you cannot simply move every buyer into your email and SMS flows the way you would from Shopify.`},{id:`creators`,h2:`Affiliates and creators`,content:`TikTok Shop has a built-in affiliate system that makes it much easier to get creators selling your product, while Shopify needs a separate program and tooling. This is the second big reason brands add TikTok Shop.

On TikTok Shop, you set a commission rate on each product and creators can request samples, add the product to their showcase and earn on the sales they drive. Tracking, payment and attribution all happen inside TikTok. A good product with a fair commission can attract dozens or hundreds of creators without separate contracts.

On Shopify, you can run an affiliate program through an app, send discount codes to influencers or pay creators for UGC to use in your own ads. That works, but you handle recruitment, tracking and payment yourself, and the creator's audience still has to leave the app to buy. Our guide to building a DTC affiliate program covers that side, and many brands run both: TikTok Shop affiliates for in-app sales and paid UGC for ads that send traffic to Shopify.`},{id:`fulfilment`,h2:`Fulfilment and shipping`,content:`TikTok Shop sets strict shipping and service rules that affect your shop's standing, while Shopify lets you set your own shipping promises. You need to be ready for TikTok's rules before you turn the channel on.

On TikTok Shop, you can usually ship orders yourself or through your 3PL, and in some markets you can use Fulfilled by TikTok. Either way, TikTok tracks metrics such as late dispatch, cancellations and returns, and poor performance can lead to warnings or limits on the shop. Viral moments also create sudden spikes in orders, so stock and fulfilment capacity matter more than on a steady paid traffic channel.

On Shopify, you choose your shipping times, carriers and return policy. Customers still expect speed, and slow shipping hurts reviews and repeat purchases, but there is no platform scoring your account. If you already ship well from Shopify, the main TikTok Shop work is meeting its dispatch deadlines and keeping inventory in sync so you do not oversell.`},{id:`using-both`,h2:`Using both: Shopify as the hub, TikTok Shop as a channel`,content:`Most DTC brands should run Shopify as the hub for products, inventory and orders, and connect TikTok Shop to it as a sales channel. That gives you TikTok's reach without splitting your operations in two.

Shopify's TikTok app can connect your store to TikTok Shop in supported markets. Depending on your region and the current version, it can sync your product catalogue and inventory and bring TikTok Shop orders into Shopify for fulfilment. Check what the app supports in your market before you rely on it. The point is to manage stock and orders in one place, so a viral video on TikTok does not oversell items your Shopify store is also selling.

A sensible setup looks like this:`,list:[`List a small number of hero products on TikTok Shop first, the ones that are easy to show on camera and can carry creator commission.`,`Keep prices consistent across channels where you can, or have a clear reason when they differ, such as a TikTok-only bundle.`,`Use TikTok Shop for discovery and impulse sales, and use Shopify for bundles, subscriptions, higher price points and repeat purchases.`,`Measure each channel on contribution margin per order, not on revenue alone.`,`Watch for overlap. Some TikTok Shop buyers would have bought on Shopify anyway, so check whether total sales grow or just move between channels.`]},{id:`decision-guide`,h2:`Decision guide: which should you sell on?`,content:`Use Shopify if control, margin and repeat customers drive your business, use TikTok Shop if impulse demand and creators drive it, and use both if you have the operations to support two channels. The table below matches common situations to a starting point.`,table:{headers:[`Your situation`,`Start with`,`Why`],rows:[[`New brand, low-priced product that demonstrates well on video`,`TikTok Shop, with a basic Shopify store`,`TikTok brings shoppers and creators without paid traffic; Shopify gives you a home base.`],[`Established Shopify brand with steady Meta ads`,`Shopify, then add TikTok Shop as a channel`,`Keep what works and test TikTok Shop on hero products.`],[`Higher price, longer consideration or subscription model`,`Shopify`,`You need the product page, checkout control and customer data.`],[`Brand relies on email, SMS and repeat purchases`,`Shopify as the hub`,`You keep the customer contact that drives lifetime value.`],[`Thin margins that cannot carry creator commission and fees`,`Shopify, until margins improve`,`TikTok Shop costs per order may leave nothing after the sale.`],[`Market where TikTok Shop is not available`,`Shopify`,`You can still use TikTok ads to send traffic to your store.`],[`Strong product and capacity for creators and LIVE selling`,`Both, connected`,`Shopify runs operations; TikTok Shop adds reach and creator sales.`]]},subsections:[{h3:`If you decide to add TikTok Shop`,content:`Start small, with a few hero products, a commission rate your margin can carry and a plan for creator samples. Our TikTok Shop strategy guide covers listings, pricing and LIVE selling in detail. If you would rather have it run for you, our TikTok Shop agency team handles setup, creators and GMV Max, and our Shopify agency keeps the store converting as the hub for every channel.`}]}],faqs:[{question:`Should I sell on TikTok Shop or Shopify?`,answer:`If you can only choose one, choose Shopify when you want control of the brand, checkout and customer data, and TikTok Shop when your product sells on impulse from short videos and you need demand. Most DTC brands do best with Shopify as the hub and TikTok Shop added as a sales channel.`},{question:`What are the TikTok Shop fees?`,answer:`TikTok Shop mainly charges a referral fee on each order, a percentage that varies by product category and country, and the rates have changed over time. You also pay any creator commission you set and any discounts you fund. Check TikTok's current seller fee page for your market before modelling margins.`},{question:`Can I connect TikTok Shop to my Shopify store?`,answer:`Yes, in supported markets. Shopify's TikTok app can connect your store to TikTok Shop and, depending on your region and the app version, sync products and inventory and bring TikTok Shop orders into Shopify for fulfilment. Check what it supports in your country before you launch.`},{question:`Do I get customer emails from TikTok Shop orders?`,answer:`Not in the way you do on Shopify. TikTok Shop gives you the details needed to fulfil the order but limits how you can contact or market to buyers off the platform. Check TikTok's current seller data policy. If email and SMS retention matter to your business, keep Shopify as your main store.`},{question:`Is TikTok Shop cheaper than Shopify?`,answer:`It depends on what you count. Shopify charges a plan fee and processing but you pay for all traffic. TikTok Shop charges a fee per order and often creator commission, but can bring shoppers without ad spend. Compare contribution margin per order on each channel, including ads, fees and commission, rather than the headline fees.`}]}],u={name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},d=[{slug:`questions-to-ask-meta-ads-agency`,title:`Questions to Ask a Meta Ads Agency Before You Hire One`,titleTag:`Questions to Ask a Meta Ads Agency | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`19 questions to ask a Meta ads agency before you hire, grouped by proof, creative, access, reporting, pricing and onboarding, with the red flags to watch for.`,primaryKeyword:`questions to ask a meta ads agency`,secondaryKeywords:[`how to choose a meta ads agency for ecommerce`,`meta ads agency red flags`,`facebook ads agency questions`,`hiring a facebook ads agency`,`meta ads agency pricing models`],publishDate:`2026-11-11`,updatedDate:`2026-11-11`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Meta Ads`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda guide`,text:`What Andromeda changed for ecommerce ads and how to structure accounts for it.`},{href:`/blog/performance-marketing-agency`,title:`DTC performance marketing agency`,text:`What one does, how it charges and how to choose the right one.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`See how we run Meta ads for DTC brands, and put these questions to us.`},{href:`/meta-ads-audit`,title:`Meta Ads Audit`,text:`A fixed-scope review of your account before you commit to any agency.`},{href:`/blog/how-much-does-a-dtc-agency-cost`,title:`How Much Does a DTC Agency Cost?`,text:`Pricing models and how to judge value against your margins.`},{href:`/compare/agency-vs-in-house-media-buyer`,title:`Agency vs In-House Media Buyer`,text:`When hiring your own media buyer makes more sense than an agency.`},{href:`/blog/choose-google-ads-agency-dtc`,title:`How to Choose a Google Ads Agency for DTC`,text:`The same vetting approach, applied to Google Ads.`}],author:u,directAnswer:`Before you hire a Meta ads agency, ask questions in six areas: proof that they have run accounts like yours, how they make and test creative, who owns the ad account and data, what they measure and report, how they charge and what the contract locks you into, and what happens in the first 30 days. Good agencies answer with specifics and show you their work. Vague answers, guaranteed results and any plan to run ads in an account they own are red flags.`,tableOfContents:[{id:`how-to-use-these-questions`,text:`How to use these questions`},{id:`proof-and-fit`,text:`Proof and fit`},{id:`creative`,text:`Creative`},{id:`account-ownership`,text:`Account ownership and access`},{id:`measurement-reporting`,text:`Measurement and reporting`},{id:`pricing-contract`,text:`Pricing and contract`},{id:`how-pricing-models-work`,text:`How Meta ads agency pricing models work`},{id:`onboarding`,text:`Onboarding and the first 30 days`},{id:`scorecard`,text:`A simple agency scorecard`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`how-to-use-these-questions`,h2:`How to use these questions`,content:`Use these questions on a sales call with every agency on your shortlist, write down the answers, and score them the same way so you compare like with like. The goal is not to catch anyone out. It is to find out how the agency actually works before you hand over your ad account and budget.

Pay as much attention to how an agency answers as to what it says. A team that knows its process will answer quickly and with examples. A team that is selling a promise will change the subject, talk about "proprietary systems" without explaining them, or tell you what results to expect before it has seen your numbers.

Send the account ownership and pricing questions in writing before the call, so the call itself can focus on proof, creative and measurement.`},{id:`proof-and-fit`,h2:`Proof and fit`,content:`The first thing to find out is whether the agency has run Meta ads for brands like yours, at a similar spend, and can show it. An agency that is great at lead generation for local businesses may not know how to scale a Shopify store with a $50 product. Ask:`,list:[`1. Which ecommerce brands at our stage and price point have you run Meta ads for, and can we speak to one of them?`,`2. Can you walk us through a real account, with the client's permission, rather than showing screenshots of ROAS?`,`3. Who will run our account day to day, what is their experience, and how many other accounts do they manage?`],subsections:[{h3:`Good answer vs red flag`,content:`A good answer names relevant brands or categories, offers a reference call, and is clear about who does the work. Many agencies cannot share client accounts openly, and that is fine, but they should be able to show results in context: spend, time period, what changed and what the margin looked like.

Red flags: only screenshots of a single great day, results with no spend or time frame attached, a senior person on the sales call who disappears after signing, or a vague answer about how many accounts each media buyer looks after.`}]},{id:`creative`,h2:`Creative`,content:`Ask how the agency makes and tests ads, because creative is now the main lever in most Meta accounts. Targeting is now broad and largely automated, so the ads do most of the work of finding buyers. Ask:`,list:[`4. Who makes the ads: your team, freelancers, creators or us? How many new concepts will you test each month?`,`5. How do you decide what to test, and how do you decide whether a test won or lost?`,`6. Who owns the creative, the raw footage and the creator usage rights when we stop working together?`],subsections:[{h3:`Good answer vs red flag`,content:`A good answer describes a testing rhythm, how new concepts differ from small variations of the same ad, and the metrics used to judge a test, such as cost per purchase and thumb-stop rate, over a set amount of spend. It should also be clear on who pays for creators and that you keep the files and usage rights you paid for.

Red flags: "we will use your existing ads", no answer on volume, testing judged on likes or clicks, or creative that stays with the agency when you leave. If the agency does not make creative, that can still work, but ask who will, how fast, and who writes the creative strategy behind the briefs.`}]},{id:`account-ownership`,h2:`Account ownership and access`,content:`Your ad account, pixel, catalogue and business portfolio should always belong to you, with the agency added as a partner. Getting this wrong can cost you your ad history and data if the relationship ends. Ask:`,list:[`7. Will the ad account, pixel or dataset, catalogue and Page all stay in our business portfolio?`,`8. What access level do you need, and how do we remove it if we part ways?`,`9. Whose payment method will be on the ad account, and who pays Meta directly?`],subsections:[{h3:`Good answer vs red flag`,content:`A good answer is simple: everything lives in your business portfolio, the agency requests partner access to the assets it needs, and your card or invoicing pays Meta directly. Removing a partner should take you a few clicks.

Red flags: running your ads from an ad account the agency owns, asking for your personal Facebook login, asking to be the only admin, or paying Meta on your behalf without a clear reason in the contract. If an agency insists on its own account, you lose the history and data when you leave, and a policy problem on its side can affect your ads too.`}]},{id:`measurement-reporting`,h2:`Measurement and reporting`,content:`Ask what number the agency will manage your account to, because an agency that optimizes to Meta-reported ROAS alone can look good while your bank balance does not. The target should come from your margins and be checked against your store data, not only against Ads Manager. Ask:`,list:[`10. What is the main number you manage to, and how will you set the target from our margins?`,`11. How do you handle the gap between what Meta reports and what Shopify shows?`,`12. Will you check our pixel and Conversions API setup, including event deduplication, before spending more?`,`13. What goes in a weekly report, and who walks us through it?`],subsections:[{h3:`Good answer vs red flag`,content:`A good answer talks about break-even ROAS or a target cost per acquisition built from your product cost, shipping and fees, and about checking results at a total business level, such as revenue against total ad spend, alongside platform numbers. The agency should plan to audit tracking first, because every decision after that depends on it. Reports should explain what changed, why, and what happens next.

Red flags: promising a specific ROAS before seeing your account, reporting only Meta's numbers, no interest in your margins, or reports that are a dashboard link with no commentary.`}]},{id:`pricing-contract`,h2:`Pricing and contract`,content:`Ask exactly how the agency gets paid, what the fee covers and how long you are committed for, and get the answers in writing. Ask:`,list:[`14. How do you charge, and what exactly does the fee include?`,`15. What is the minimum term, the notice period, and what happens to our assets when it ends?`,`16. Is creative production included, or is it a separate cost? Are there setup fees?`],subsections:[{h3:`Good answer vs red flag`,content:`A good answer gives a clear fee structure, a written scope, and a notice period you can live with. Longer commitments are not automatically bad, since Meta accounts take time to test properly, but there should be a sensible exit if the work is not delivered.

Red flags: pricing that changes once you ask for detail, long lock-ins with no performance review points, hidden creative or "tech" fees, and guaranteed results. No agency controls Meta's auction, your product or your margins, so a guarantee usually means fine print you will not like.`}]},{id:`how-pricing-models-work`,h2:`How Meta ads agency pricing models work`,content:`Meta ads agencies usually charge in one of three ways, or a mix of them: a percentage of ad spend, a flat monthly retainer, or a performance fee. Each model rewards the agency for something different, so the right one depends on your stage and what you want the agency to focus on. The table compares them without market prices, because rates vary widely by scope, spend and what is included.`,table:{headers:[`Model`,`How it works`,`What it rewards`,`Watch out for`],rows:[[`Percentage of ad spend`,`The fee is a share of what you spend on Meta each month, often with a minimum fee.`,`Managing and growing spend.`,`The agency earns more when spend rises, whether or not that spend is profitable.`],[`Flat retainer`,`A fixed monthly fee for an agreed scope of work.`,`Predictable work and planning.`,`Scope creep, and no direct link between the fee and results.`],[`Performance fee`,`Part or all of the fee is tied to an outcome, such as revenue or profit above a baseline.`,`Hitting the agreed outcome.`,`How the outcome is measured. Revenue-based fees can reward discounting and attributed sales that would have happened anyway.`],[`Hybrid`,`A base retainer plus a performance or spend-based element.`,`A balance of stable work and growth.`,`Complex terms that are hard to check. Make sure you can audit the numbers.`]]},list:[`If you choose a performance model, agree in writing what data the fee is based on, such as Shopify revenue or contribution margin, and what the baseline is.`,`Whatever the model, compare total cost, including creative, against the contribution margin the agency needs to add to pay for itself.`,`Ask how the fee changes as you scale, so you know what you will pay at double your current spend.`],subsections:[{h3:`Agency, freelancer or in-house?`,content:`An agency is not always the right answer. If you spend enough to keep one person busy full time and you can manage them, an in-house media buyer can work well. Our comparison of agency and in-house options covers the trade-offs, and our guide to DTC agency cost goes deeper on judging value.`}]},{id:`onboarding`,h2:`Onboarding and the first 30 days`,content:`Ask what the agency will do in the first 30 days, because a good onboarding plan tells you more about how it works than any pitch deck. The first month should be about understanding the business, fixing tracking and setting up a testing plan, not about doubling your budget. Ask:`,list:[`17. What will you do in the first 30 days, week by week?`,`18. What do you need from us, such as margins, product data, past ads, access and approvals?`,`19. When, and on what numbers, should we judge whether this is working?`],subsections:[{h3:`Good answer vs red flag`,content:`A good answer covers an account and tracking review, a look at your margins and offers, a creative and testing plan, and a clear first round of launches. It sets a realistic review point and admits that not every early test will work.

Red flags: rebuilding the whole account on day one with no review, no questions about your margins or customers, and no agreed point at which you will judge progress. If you want an independent view before you commit, a fixed-scope Meta ads audit is a lower-risk first step than a long contract.`}]},{id:`scorecard`,h2:`A simple agency scorecard`,content:`Score each agency from 0 to 2 in each area after the call: 0 for a red flag or no answer, 1 for a reasonable but vague answer, and 2 for a specific answer backed by evidence. An agency that scores 0 on account ownership should be ruled out whatever its total, because that risk is hard to undo.`,table:{headers:[`Area`,`Scores 2 when the agency`,`Scores 0 when the agency`,`Score (0 to 2)`],rows:[[`Proof and fit`,`Shows relevant work in context and offers a reference`,`Shows only screenshots or results with no context`,``],[`Creative`,`Explains volume, testing method and ownership`,`Has no plan for new creative`,``],[`Account ownership`,`Works as a partner in your business portfolio`,`Wants to run ads from its own account`,``],[`Measurement`,`Sets targets from your margins and audits tracking`,`Promises a ROAS before seeing the account`,``],[`Pricing and contract`,`Gives a clear written scope and exit terms`,`Guarantees results or hides fees`,``],[`Onboarding`,`Has a week-by-week first month and review point`,`Plans to scale spend straight away`,``]]},subsections:[{h3:`Ask us the same questions`,content:`We are a Meta ads agency, so we are on the other side of these calls too. Put every one of these questions to us. If you would like a starting point before any agency conversation, our Brand Growth Audit reviews your account, tracking, creative and margins and gives you the plan whether or not you hire us.`}]}],faqs:[{question:`What questions should I ask a Meta ads agency?`,answer:`Ask about six areas: proof that they have run accounts like yours, how they make and test creative, who owns the ad account and data, what they measure and report, how they charge and what the contract commits you to, and what they will do in the first 30 days.`},{question:`What are the red flags when hiring a Meta ads agency?`,answer:`The biggest red flags are running your ads from an account the agency owns, asking for your personal Facebook login, guaranteeing a ROAS or revenue figure, reporting only Meta's numbers, hiding creative or setup fees, and long lock-ins with no review point.`},{question:`How do I choose a Meta ads agency for ecommerce?`,answer:`Shortlist agencies that have run Meta ads for ecommerce brands at your stage, ask each the same questions, score the answers on proof, creative, ownership, measurement, pricing and onboarding, and speak to a reference client before you sign.`},{question:`Should the agency own my Facebook ad account?`,answer:`No. The ad account, pixel or dataset, catalogue and Page should stay in your business portfolio, with the agency given partner access. That way you keep your data and history if you change agencies, and you can remove their access yourself.`},{question:`How do Meta ads agencies charge?`,answer:`Most charge a percentage of ad spend, a flat monthly retainer, a performance fee tied to revenue or profit, or a hybrid of these. Rates vary widely by scope and spend, so compare total cost, including creative, against the margin the agency needs to add.`}]},{slug:`meta-ad-account-restricted`,title:`Meta Ad Account Restricted or Disabled: What It Means and How to Get It Back`,titleTag:`Meta Ad Account Restricted? How to Fix It | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`Meta ad account restricted or disabled? Learn the four types of restriction, where to check status, how to request a review and how DTC brands prevent it.`,primaryKeyword:`meta ad account restricted`,secondaryKeywords:[`facebook ad account disabled`,`how to get facebook ad account back`,`advertising access restricted`,`business portfolio restricted`,`meta account quality`,`facebook ad account request review`],publishDate:`2026-11-11`,updatedDate:`2026-11-18`,readTime:`8 min read`,category:`Meta Ads`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`Account setup and compliant creative run by a team that manages Meta ads every day.`},{href:`/meta-ads-audit`,title:`Meta Ads Audit`,text:`A review of account structure, tracking and creative, including policy risk.`},{href:`/for/health-wellness`,title:`Wellness Brand Marketing`,text:`Meta ads for health and wellness brands, where policy risk is highest.`},{href:`/for/supplements`,title:`Supplement Brand Marketing`,text:`Compliant creative and offers for supplement brands on Meta.`},{href:`/blog/facebook-ads-for-shopify`,title:`Facebook Ads for Shopify`,text:`Set up your business portfolio, pixel and ad account the right way from the start.`}],author:u,directAnswer:`A restricted or disabled Meta ad account means Meta has stopped that account, your profile or your whole business portfolio from running ads, usually because its systems flagged a policy issue, a payment problem or suspicious activity. Check the status in Account Quality, read the reason Meta gives, fix what you can, and request a review there. Do not open new accounts to keep advertising, because Meta treats that as circumvention.`,definitionBox:{term:`Meta ad account restriction`,definition:`A limit Meta places on an ad account, a person's ability to advertise, or a business portfolio when it believes Meta's Advertising Standards, Community Standards or payment terms have been broken. Restricted assets cannot run ads until the issue is resolved or a review succeeds.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`four-types`,text:`Disabled, restricted, portfolio restricted or ad rejected?`},{id:`where-to-check`,text:`Where to check your status`},{id:`request-review`,text:`How to request a review`},{id:`common-causes`,text:`Common causes for DTC brands`},{id:`payment-restrictions`,text:`Payment failures and unpaid balances`},{id:`what-not-to-do`,text:`What not to do`},{id:`prevention-checklist`,text:`Prevention checklist`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`four-types`,h2:`Disabled, restricted, portfolio restricted or ad rejected?`,content:`There are four different problems that people describe as "my ad account got banned", and each one is fixed differently. Work out which one you have before you do anything else.`,table:{headers:[`What you see`,`What is affected`,`What it usually means`],rows:[[`Ad account disabled`,`One ad account. Its ads stop and you cannot create new ones in it.`,`Meta flagged activity in that account, such as policy violations, a payment problem or suspicious behaviour.`],[`Advertising access restricted on a profile`,`A person's Facebook profile. They cannot create or manage ads on any account, even ones they admin.`,`Meta has concerns about that person's activity, identity or links to other restricted assets.`],[`Business portfolio restricted`,`The whole business portfolio (formerly Business Manager) and often every ad account inside it.`,`Meta flagged the business itself, its admins or the assets connected to it.`],[`Ad rejected`,`One ad. Other ads keep running.`,`That ad, its landing page or its targeting did not meet Meta's Advertising Standards.`]]},list:[`An ad rejection is the mildest. You can edit the ad or request a review of that ad on its own.`,`Repeated rejections matter, though. A pattern of rejected ads can lead to wider restrictions on the account or the people running it.`,`A profile restriction follows the person, not the brand. If your media buyer's profile is restricted, adding a different admin may let the brand keep advertising while that person appeals.`]},{id:`where-to-check`,h2:`Where to check your status`,content:`Check your status in Account Quality, Meta's page for the restrictions on your profile, ad accounts, Pages and business portfolio. Meta's current help pages use two names for it: some send you to Account Quality, and the ads review policy page sends you to Business Support Home. Use whichever your account shows; both list the restriction, its reason and the request review option.

You can usually reach Account Quality from Meta Business Suite, from the menu in Ads Manager, or from the notice Meta shows when an asset is restricted. It lists each restricted asset, the reason Meta gives, and whether you can request a review. Meta also sends notices by email and in the Business Suite notifications, so check those too.

Read the stated reason closely. "Unacceptable business practices", "circumventing systems", "unpaid balance" and a specific ad policy all point to different fixes. If the reason is unclear, open the Meta Business Help Center for the latest guidance on that reason, because Meta updates the wording, menus and options regularly.`},{id:`request-review`,h2:`How to request a review`,content:`If you believe the restriction is a mistake, or you have fixed the cause, request a review from Account Quality. Select the restricted asset, choose the option to request a review, and follow the steps Meta shows. The steps vary by restriction type and region, and Meta may ask you to confirm your identity, for example with an ID document or a short video selfie, or to secure your account first.

Before you submit, do the groundwork:`,list:[`Fix what caused it. Edit or remove ads that break policy, clean up landing pages and settle any unpaid balance.`,`Secure every profile involved. Turn on two-factor authentication and remove any admins or partners you do not recognise.`,`Keep your explanation short and factual if Meta gives you a space to write one. Explain what the business sells and what you changed.`,`Request one review per asset and wait. The number of reviews you can request may be limited, and some decisions are final.`,`Keep a record of dates, screenshots and the reasons shown, in case you need to contact Meta support or your rep later.`],subsections:[{h3:`How long does a review take?`,content:`Meta does not commit to a fixed review time, and it varies by case. Some reviews come back quickly and others take much longer. Account Quality shows when a review is in progress and the outcome when it is done. If you have a Meta marketing rep through an agency or your own spend, they may be able to help you find the right support route, but they cannot overrule a policy decision.`}]},{id:`common-causes`,h2:`Common causes for DTC brands`,content:`Most restrictions on DTC brands come from ad copy and claims, payment problems, unusual account behaviour or a poor buyer experience. Meta's systems are largely automated, so it is the pattern of signals that matters, not intent. These are the causes we see most often.`,list:[`Health and before-and-after claims. Supplements, skincare and weight management ads that promise outcomes or show dramatic before-and-after images often break Meta's policies on health and personal health claims.`,`Personal attributes wording. Meta does not allow ads that assert or imply someone's personal attributes, such as a health condition or financial situation. "Struggling with acne?" or "Tired of your belly fat?" can be enough.`,`Payment failures. A declined card or an unpaid balance can disable an ad account until the balance is settled.`,`Sudden spend spikes on new accounts. A brand-new ad account that jumps straight to a large daily budget can look like a compromised or fraudulent account.`,`Circumvention signals. Links to previously disabled accounts or profiles, landing pages that show reviewers something different from users, or using someone else's account to keep advertising.`,`Low customer feedback. Meta collects feedback from people who bought after seeing your ads. A low feedback score, often from slow shipping or products that do not match the ad, can reduce delivery and in some cases lead to restrictions.`,`Compromised accounts. A hacked admin profile that runs unfamiliar ads, often in another currency or market, can get the whole portfolio restricted.`],subsections:[{h3:`Higher-risk categories`,content:`Supplements, health and wellness, skincare, weight management and anything that touches medical claims draw the most scrutiny. If you sell in these categories, write ads that describe the product and the experience of using it rather than promising a result, and make sure the landing page follows the same rules. Our health and wellness and supplement pages cover how we approach compliant creative for these brands.`}]},{id:`payment-restrictions`,h2:`Payment failures and unpaid balances`,content:`A payment restriction is usually the quickest to fix: pay the outstanding balance and the account can often return to normal. Go to Billing and payments for the ad account, check the failed charge, and either retry it or add a working payment method and pay the balance.

To stop it happening again, keep a backup payment method on the account, make sure your card limit can cover your daily spend, and tell your bank to expect regular charges from Meta. Give each ad account its own payment method where you can, so one failed card does not affect every account in the portfolio. If the account stays disabled after the balance is paid, request a review from Account Quality.`},{id:`what-not-to-do`,h2:`What not to do`,content:`Do not open new ad accounts, profiles or business portfolios to keep advertising while you are restricted. Meta treats this as circumvention, and it is one of the surest ways to turn a fixable restriction into a permanent one across every asset you own.

Avoid these as well:`,list:[`Buying, renting or borrowing ad accounts or profiles from other people.`,`Paying "unban" services that ask for your login details. They often cannot do what they promise, and sharing logins puts your assets at further risk.`,`Resubmitting the same rejected ad again and again without changing it.`,`Using landing pages or redirects that show Meta's reviewers something different from what customers see.`,`Deleting the assets or admins involved and starting over. Meta can link new assets to old ones.`]},{id:`prevention-checklist`,h2:`Prevention checklist`,content:`The best defence against a restricted Meta ad account is a well-set-up business portfolio, a gradual spend ramp and ads and pages that follow policy from the start. Work through this list once, then review it whenever you add a new ad account or team member. A Meta ads audit should include the same checks.`,list:[`Verify your business in the business portfolio settings, using your registered business details.`,`Turn on two-factor authentication for every person with access, and require it in the portfolio settings.`,`Have at least two trusted admins, so one restricted or locked-out profile does not lock the brand out.`,`Give agencies and freelancers partner access through their own business portfolio rather than sharing logins.`,`Ramp spend gradually on new ad accounts instead of starting at full budget.`,`Keep landing pages compliant: clear business details, contact information, shipping and returns policies, and claims that match the ads.`,`Use separate payment methods for each ad account, plus a backup card.`,`Check your ads against Meta's Advertising Standards before launch, especially health, personal attributes and before-and-after content.`,`Watch your customer feedback score and fix shipping or product issues that drive complaints.`,`Check Account Quality every week, so you catch warnings and rejected ads before they build up.`],subsections:[{h3:`If you are restricted right now`,content:`Confirm which of the four problems you have, read the reason in Account Quality, fix the cause, secure your accounts and request a review. Check the Meta Business Help Center for the current steps, since the process changes. Then use the waiting time to fix the setup issues on this list, so the next account you run is harder to restrict. If you want a second pair of eyes on the setup, our team can review it as part of a Brand Growth Audit.`}]}],faqs:[{question:`Why was my Meta ad account restricted?`,answer:`Meta restricts ad accounts when its systems flag policy violations, payment problems or suspicious activity. For DTC brands the common causes are health or before-and-after claims, personal attributes wording, failed payments, sudden spend spikes on new accounts and signs of circumvention. Account Quality shows the reason Meta gives.`},{question:`How do I get my Facebook ad account back?`,answer:`Open Account Quality, find the disabled account, read the reason, fix the cause and request a review. Meta may ask you to verify your identity or secure your account first. If the cause is an unpaid balance, paying it often restores the account.`},{question:`What is the difference between a disabled ad account and restricted advertising access?`,answer:`A disabled ad account affects one ad account. Restricted advertising access affects a person's profile, so they cannot run or manage ads on any account. A business portfolio restriction can affect every ad account inside the portfolio.`},{question:`How long does a Meta ad account review take?`,answer:`Meta does not publish a fixed time, and it varies by case. Account Quality shows when a review is in progress and the outcome when it is complete. Check the Meta Business Help Center for current guidance.`},{question:`Can I just create a new ad account if mine is disabled?`,answer:`No. Opening new ad accounts, profiles or business portfolios to keep advertising while restricted is treated by Meta as circumventing its systems, and it can lead to permanent restrictions on everything linked to you. Request a review instead.`}]}],f=[{slug:`tiktok-gmv-max`,title:`TikTok GMV Max: How It Works, ROI Targets and Why It Will Not Spend`,titleTag:`TikTok GMV Max Guide: ROI Target and Setup | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`TikTok GMV Max explained: Product vs LIVE GMV Max, setting a target ROI from your margins, Max delivery, attribution and what to do when it will not spend.`,primaryKeyword:`tiktok gmv max`,secondaryKeywords:[`gmv max`,`gmv max target roi`,`gmv max not spending`,`product gmv max vs live gmv max`,`tiktok shop ads`,`gmv max max delivery`],publishDate:`2026-11-12`,updatedDate:`2026-11-12`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Growth Channels`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/tiktok-shop-agency`,title:`TikTok Shop Agency`,text:`Have your GMV Max campaigns, affiliate creators and LIVE run on your real margins.`},{href:`/tools/tiktok-shop-break-even-roas-calculator`,title:`TikTok Shop Break-Even ROAS Calculator`,text:`Work out the lowest ROI target your margins can carry before you launch.`},{href:`/blog/tiktok-shop-strategy`,title:`TikTok Shop Strategy for DTC Brands`,text:`Listings, pricing, affiliate creators and LIVE, the inputs GMV Max depends on.`},{href:`/blog/tiktok-shop-vs-shopify`,title:`TikTok Shop vs Shopify`,text:`Fees, customer data and when a DTC brand should run both.`},{href:`/tiktok-ads-agency`,title:`TikTok Ads Agency`,text:`Creator ads, Spark Ads and in-feed campaigns for DTC brands.`}],author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`GMV Max is TikTok Shop's automated ad campaign type. You choose the products (Product GMV Max) or the LIVE stream (LIVE GMV Max), set a budget and a target ROI, and TikTok decides which videos, placements and audiences to use to drive the most gross merchandise value. According to TikTok's help center, it has been the default and only campaign type for new TikTok Shop ads since July 2025. Set the ROI target at or above your break-even, feed it plenty of creator videos, and lower the target slightly if it will not spend.`,definitionBox:{term:`GMV Max`,definition:`An automated TikTok Shop ad campaign that optimizes for gross merchandise value (GMV), the total value of orders before fees. It uses your shop's product videos, authorized affiliate creator posts and product cards, and aims to hit the return on ad spend you set as a target ROI.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-is-gmv-max`,text:`What GMV Max is and what it replaced`},{id:`product-vs-live`,text:`Product GMV Max vs LIVE GMV Max`},{id:`bidding-options`,text:`Target ROI vs Max delivery`},{id:`set-roi-target`,text:`How to set your ROI target from unit economics`},{id:`not-spending`,text:`Why GMV Max is not spending`},{id:`creative-affiliates`,text:`How creator and affiliate videos feed GMV Max`},{id:`attribution`,text:`How GMV Max counts organic and affiliate sales`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-is-gmv-max`,h2:`What GMV Max is and what it replaced`,content:`GMV Max is the campaign type TikTok now uses for almost all paid TikTok Shop advertising. Instead of building ad groups and audiences, you give it products or a LIVE room, a daily budget and a target ROI. TikTok then spends across its Shop ad placements using whatever content it can access for those products.

As of November 2026, TikTok's help center article on the GMV Max migration says that since July 2025 advertisers using the Sales objective with TikTok Shop as the destination can no longer create, edit or duplicate Product Shopping Ads, Video Shopping Ads or LIVE Shopping Ads. Existing campaigns could keep running, and upgraded campaigns started from their historical ROI. TikTok says Video Shopping Ads remain available with Showcase as the destination, and LIVE Shopping Ads can still be created with a TikTok account identity rather than a shop.

Two setup rules trip people up: the ad account, TikTok accounts and shop must sit in the same Business Center, and a product in GMV Max cannot also run in standard Shop ads. Availability also varies by market, so check the TikTok Ads help center for your country.`},{id:`product-vs-live`,h2:`Product GMV Max vs LIVE GMV Max`,content:`Product GMV Max promotes chosen products all day using short videos and product cards. LIVE GMV Max drives viewers into a LIVE stream while it is on air and optimizes for the revenue the stream makes. Most brands start with Product GMV Max and add LIVE GMV Max once they run LIVE on a steady schedule.`,table:{headers:[``,`Product GMV Max`,`LIVE GMV Max`],rows:[[`What it promotes`,`Selected products from your shop`,`A LIVE stream from a linked TikTok account`],[`When it runs`,`Always on, for as long as the campaign is active`,`Around LIVE sessions`],[`Creative it uses`,`Shop videos, authorized affiliate creator posts and product cards`,`The stream itself, plus short videos that send viewers into the LIVE (TikTok calls these Video-to-live and Live-to-live)`],[`What ROI measures`,`Gross revenue from the selected products divided by ad cost`,`Total LIVE room gross revenue divided by ad cost, according to TikTok`],[`Best for`,`Hero products with lots of video content and sales history`,`Brands with a regular LIVE schedule and a host who can sell`],[`Main risk`,`Reported ROI includes organic orders, so it can look better than paid performance really is`,`Spend during weak streams, and results tied to host quality`]]},subsections:[{h3:`Which one to start with`,content:`Start with Product GMV Max on a few hero products that already sell and already have creator videos. LIVE GMV Max only pays off when the stream itself converts, so prove that organically first.`}]},{id:`bidding-options`,h2:`Target ROI vs Max delivery`,content:`GMV Max has two main ways to spend. Target ROI tries to keep your return close to a number you set. Max delivery tries to spend the whole budget and treats ROI as secondary. TikTok's help center describes Max delivery as something you can turn on for individual products inside a Product GMV Max campaign, and TikTok keeps adding options, so the exact names in your account may differ.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`What it does`,`When to use it`,`What to watch`],rows:[[`Target ROI`,`Aims to keep return near your target. A lower target lets it spend more, a higher target restricts spend.`,`The default for most brands. Use it whenever profit per order matters.`,`A target set above your history often means very little spend.`],[`Max delivery`,`Spends the budget first and optimizes ROI second, so ROI can swing.`,`Product launches, sale events or pushing a proven product for more volume.`,`TikTok's own guidance suggests a budget of 1 to 5 times what the product spends on Target ROI, and warns that going above 5 times can cut ROI sharply.`],[`Growth autopilot`,`A newer option TikTok describes for Product GMV Max that works to a 7-day ROI target and 7-day budget.`,`Brands that want smoother weekly pacing.`,`TikTok advises against changing the target mid-cycle. Check the help center, as this is still changing.`]]},subsections:[{h3:`ROI protection`,content:`TikTok's help center describes ROI protection for some Target ROI campaigns: if ROI falls below 90% of the daily target, TikTok may issue ad credits. Eligibility rules apply and change, so check the current article. It softens a bad day but does not make an unprofitable target profitable.`}]},{id:`set-roi-target`,h2:`How to set your ROI target from unit economics`,content:`Set your GMV Max target ROI at or above your TikTok Shop break-even ROAS, never below it. GMV Max ROI is gross revenue divided by ad cost, which is the same maths as ROAS. Break-even ROAS is the price of the product divided by the profit you make on one order before ads. If you spend more than that profit to win the order, you lose money.

Here is an illustrative example with made-up numbers. A product sells for $40. Product cost is $10, shipping and packing is $6, the TikTok Shop referral fee comes to $3.20 and the affiliate commission is $6. Profit before ads is $40 minus $25.20, which is $14.80. Break-even ROAS is $40 divided by $14.80, which is about 2.7. Any ROI target below 2.7 loses money on every ad-driven order.

Our free calculator does this for you: enter your price, costs, referral fee and commission, and it shows profit per order and the break-even number.`,list:[`Start at break-even or a little above it. Below break-even you are buying revenue at a loss, unless you have a clear plan to earn it back through repeat orders.`,`Compare it with TikTok's recommendation. TikTok suggests a starting ROI based on your history, calculated as historical non-LIVE GMV divided by historical ad cost. If that number is well below your break-even, the product needs better margins, a higher price or better content before more spend.`,`Allow for organic orders. Reported GMV Max ROI includes organic orders for the same products, so the true paid return is lower than the dashboard shows. A target a little above break-even gives you a safety margin.`,`Use contribution margin, not gross margin: fees, shipping, returns and commission all come out before ads.`]},{id:`not-spending`,h2:`Why GMV Max is not spending`,content:`The most common reason GMV Max under-spends is a target ROI set higher than the product has achieved before. TikTok's own guidance treats low budget use as a sign that the target is too high, not as a delivery fault. The fix is usually to lower the target a little, then wait.`,list:[`ROI target too high. Lower it in small steps toward TikTok's recommended ROI, as long as it stays at or above your break-even. TikTok advises against changing it many times a day.`,`Too little content. GMV Max can only spend when it has videos to show. One or two videos per product gives it very little to work with.`,`Weak product page or price. A low conversion rate means each impression earns less, so the campaign cannot hit your target and holds back.`,`New shop or new product. With little sales history, TikTok has less data to predict who will buy. Expect slow, uneven spend for the first days.`,`Products locked elsewhere. A product already running in other Shop ads, or not set up correctly in the same Business Center, may not deliver in GMV Max.`,`Changes made too soon. Every edit gives the system less time to settle. TikTok's troubleshooting guidance suggests watching new or adjusted campaigns for 2 to 3 days.`],subsections:[{h3:`What not to do`,content:`Raising the budget will not fix under-spending: a campaign that cannot spend at your target will not spend more just because the budget is bigger. And do not drop the target far below break-even just to get spend moving.`}]},{id:`creative-affiliates`,h2:`How creator and affiliate videos feed GMV Max`,content:`GMV Max is only as good as the content it can choose from. According to TikTok's help center, Product GMV Max automatically includes authorized affiliate videos that feature your selected products, under the source TikTok labels "Affiliate post". Those videos receive a commercial content label. If a strong creator video is missing, you can ask the creator for authorization or add it with its video code.

So your affiliate program is also your ad creative pipeline. Every creator video gives GMV Max another option to test, which is why creator seeding and samples belong in the ad budget.`,list:[`Keep a steady flow of new videos for each hero product, from your own account and from creators.`,`Cover different angles: demonstrations, problem and solution, unboxing, comparisons and honest reviews.`,`Use exclusion filters to stop paid delivery on videos that are off-brand or make claims you would not approve.`,`Check which videos earn the most gross revenue each week and brief more creators on those angles.`]},{id:`attribution`,h2:`How GMV Max counts organic and affiliate sales`,content:`GMV Max gives itself credit for more than ad-driven sales, so read its numbers with care. TikTok's help center on GMV Max attribution says that while a Product GMV Max campaign runs, orders for its selected products are attributed to the campaign, including organic orders from people who did not view or click an ad. Orders from affiliate videos featuring those products also roll in. For ad-driven orders, TikTok counts purchases within one day of a view or click.

So reported ROI looks higher than the paid return really is, GMV Max can appear to "take over" sales that were already happening, and comparing its ROI with your Meta ROAS is not like for like.

TikTok itself recommends judging products and videos on gross revenue, because ROI and cost per order mix paid and organic orders. The better test is the whole shop: total TikTok Shop revenue and profit against total ad spend, before and after a change. If both go up, the campaign is adding sales. If revenue stays flat while ad spend rises, GMV Max is mostly claiming sales you would have had anyway.`,subsections:[{h3:`Where to go from here`,content:`GMV Max changes quickly, so check the TikTok Ads help center for current names and limits before big changes. Our TikTok Shop strategy guide covers the listings, pricing and LIVE selling that feed it, and our TikTok Shop agency team can run it for you, starting with a Brand Growth Audit.`}]}],faqs:[{question:`What is GMV Max on TikTok?`,answer:`GMV Max is TikTok Shop's automated ad campaign type. You choose products or a LIVE stream, set a budget and a target ROI, and TikTok picks the videos, placements and audiences to drive the most gross merchandise value. According to TikTok's help center, it has been the default campaign type for new TikTok Shop ads since July 2025.`},{question:`What is the difference between Product GMV Max and LIVE GMV Max?`,answer:`Product GMV Max promotes selected products all day using shop videos, affiliate creator posts and product cards. LIVE GMV Max sends viewers into a LIVE stream while it is on air and optimizes for the revenue the stream makes. Most brands start with Product GMV Max.`},{question:`What target ROI should I set for GMV Max?`,answer:`Set it at or above your break-even ROAS, which is your price divided by your profit per order before ads. Compare that with TikTok's recommended ROI, which is based on your history. If the recommendation is below break-even, fix margins, pricing or content before adding spend.`},{question:`Why is my GMV Max campaign not spending?`,answer:`Usually because the target ROI is higher than the product has achieved before. Other causes are too few videos, a weak product page or price, a new shop with little history, or frequent edits. Lower the target slightly, stay at or above break-even, and give it 2 to 3 days.`},{question:`Does GMV Max take credit for organic sales?`,answer:`Yes. TikTok's help center says orders for the selected products during a Product GMV Max campaign are attributed to it, including organic and affiliate orders. Reported ROI is therefore higher than the true paid return, so judge it on total shop revenue and profit against total ad spend.`}]}],p=[{slug:`tiktok-shop-affiliate-commission`,title:`TikTok Shop Affiliate Commission: How to Set Your Rate From Your Margins`,titleTag:`TikTok Shop Affiliate Commission Rate Guide | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`What commission to offer TikTok Shop affiliates: open vs targeted plans, Shop Ads rates, samples, and a method to set your rate from your real margins.`,primaryKeyword:`tiktok shop affiliate commission rate`,secondaryKeywords:[`what commission should I offer tiktok shop affiliates`,`tiktok shop open plan vs targeted plan`,`tiktok shop open collaboration`,`tiktok shop targeted collaboration`,`tiktok shop ads commission rate`,`tiktok shop affiliate samples`],publishDate:`2026-11-16`,updatedDate:`2026-11-16`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Growth Channels`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/tiktok-shop-agency`,title:`TikTok Shop Agency`,text:`Have your affiliate plans, samples, GMV Max and LIVE run on your real margins.`},{href:`/tools/tiktok-shop-break-even-roas-calculator`,title:`TikTok Shop Break-Even ROAS Calculator`,text:`Enter your price, costs, referral fee and commission to see profit per order.`},{href:`/blog/tiktok-gmv-max`,title:`TikTok GMV Max Guide`,text:`How affiliate videos feed GMV Max and how to set its ROI target.`},{href:`/blog/dtc-affiliate-program`,title:`How to Build a DTC Affiliate Program`,text:`Commission structures, recruiting and tracking beyond TikTok Shop.`},{href:`/dtc-affiliate-program-management`,title:`Affiliate Program Management`,text:`Done-for-you creator recruiting, commissions, payouts and fraud checks.`}],author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`There is no single right TikTok Shop affiliate commission rate. Set it from your unit economics: take your price, subtract product cost, shipping and the TikTok referral fee, and divide what is left by the price. That percentage minus the margin you want to keep is the most commission you can afford. Run a lower open plan rate for any creator, a higher targeted rate for proven creators, and a separate Shop Ads rate for affiliate videos TikTok uses in ads.`,definitionBox:{term:`TikTok Shop affiliate commission`,definition:`The percentage of an order's value that a seller pays a creator who sells the product through a TikTok video, LIVE or showcase. The seller sets the rate in Seller Center, TikTok tracks the sale, and the commission comes out of the seller's order settlement.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-rate`,text:`What commission should you offer TikTok Shop affiliates?`},{id:`how-calculated`,text:`How TikTok Shop affiliate commission is calculated and paid`},{id:`open-vs-targeted`,text:`Open plan vs targeted plan`},{id:`shop-ads-commission`,text:`Shop Ads commission for affiliate videos in ads`},{id:`set-from-margins`,text:`How to set your rate from unit economics`},{id:`samples`,text:`Sample and seeding costs`},{id:`tiering`,text:`Tiering commission by creator performance`},{id:`when-to-raise`,text:`When to raise your commission rate`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-rate`,h2:`What commission should you offer TikTok Shop affiliates?`,content:`Offer the highest rate your margins can carry after the TikTok referral fee and the profit you need to keep, and no higher. That number is different for every product, which is why published "typical rates" disagree so much.

As of November 2026, TikTok's Seller Center help says sellers can set an affiliate commission between 1% and 80% of each order. Third-party guides disagree on what is normal. Hamster Garage, a TikTok Shop agency, reports a US average of about 13% and a typical range of 5% to 30%, with top targeted deals reaching 50%. Other guides put open rates at 10% to 15% and targeted rates at 15% to 25%. None of these figures come from TikTok and they do not agree, so treat them as context, not a target. The useful question is what your product can afford.`},{id:`how-calculated`,h2:`How TikTok Shop affiliate commission is calculated and paid`,content:`Commission is a percentage of what the buyer actually paid for the product, minus refunds. TikTok Shop's US Seller University gives the formula as commission equals revenue minus refunds, multiplied by the commission rate. TikTok's settlement guidance describes the base as the product price excluding tax and minus seller discounts, so shipping and tax are not part of it.`,list:[`Cancelled and refunded orders: according to TikTok, a cancelled or fully refunded order pays no commission, and a partial refund reduces it.`,`When it is paid: TikTok's US help pages say commission is finalized after delivery and the after-sales period, and is typically released around 15 days after delivery. TikTok notes it can take up to 31 days when settlement, disputes or returns cause a delay.`,`Who pays: it comes out of your order settlement.`,`Rate changes: TikTok says creators already promoting a product keep their rate for 30 days after you cut it, while an increase applies straight away. Plan cuts a month ahead.`],subsections:[{h3:`How commission stacks with the referral fee`,content:`The referral fee is TikTok's own platform fee and the affiliate commission is what you pay the creator. They are separate, both are percentages of the sale, and both come out of the same order. So a creator order costs you both, on top of product cost and shipping. TikTok's US fee table listed a 6% referral fee for most categories from April 2024, and some 2026 seller guides report a rise to 8% that we could not confirm on an official page. Use the rate Seller Center shows for your category.`}]},{id:`open-vs-targeted`,h2:`Open plan vs targeted plan`,content:`An open plan (TikTok now calls it an open collaboration) is a public rate any eligible creator can pick up. A targeted plan (target collaboration) is a private offer you send to specific creators, usually at a higher rate. TikTok's help center says that when a creator qualifies for both on the same product, the target collaboration rate takes priority, which is why TikTok advises setting it higher.`,table:{headers:[``,`Open collaboration`,`Target collaboration`],rows:[[`Who sees it`,`Any eligible creator browsing the marketplace`,`Only the creators you invite`],[`Typical use`,`Your base rate for broad reach and discovery`,`Higher rate for proven or strategic creators`],[`Rate setting`,`One rate per product for everyone`,`Set per offer, can differ by creator`],[`Priority`,`Used when no targeted offer applies`,`Overrides the open rate for that creator`]]},subsections:[{h3:`A sensible starting split`,content:`Set the open rate at a level you are happy to pay on every creator sale and keep headroom above it. If your maximum affordable rate is 30%, an open rate of 15% leaves room to offer 20% to 25% to creators who prove they can sell.`}]},{id:`shop-ads-commission`,h2:`Shop Ads commission for affiliate videos in ads`,content:`TikTok lets you set a separate, usually lower, commission for orders that come from ads using a creator's video. TikTok's help center describes it as a Shop Ads commission rate you switch on inside an open collaboration. If you do not set one, the standard rate applies to ad orders too. Orders from ads that do not use affiliate posts pay no affiliate commission.

This matters because GMV Max automatically uses authorized affiliate videos. Without a Shop Ads rate, you pay full commission on sales you also paid ad spend to win.

TikTok's help center has said the Shop Ads rate in an open collaboration cannot go below 30% of the standard rate (so a 10% standard rate means at least 3%), in all regions except Mexico and Brazil. That article is more than a year old and some third-party guides quote a one-third minimum, so check the current floor in Seller Center.`},{id:`set-from-margins`,h2:`How to set your rate from unit economics`,content:`Use one formula: maximum commission % = (profit before ads and commission / price) minus your target margin. Profit before ads and commission is the price minus product cost, shipping and the referral fee. Target margin is the share of the price you want to keep before any ad spend.

Worked example, using the defaults in our TikTok Shop break-even ROAS calculator: a $35 price, $8 product cost, $6 shipping and an 8% example referral fee. The referral fee is $2.80, so profit before ads and commission is $35 minus $8, $6 and $2.80, which is $18.20, or 52% of the price.

If you want to keep at least 20% of the price as profit before ads, the most you can offer is 52% minus 20%, which is 32%. If you want to keep 25%, the ceiling is 27%. For simplicity the example assumes commission is paid on the full $35 with no discounts.

The table shows what each rate leaves you per creator order, and the break-even ROAS for any ads layered on top, which is the price divided by profit per order.`,table:{headers:[`Commission rate`,`Commission per order`,`Profit per order before ads`,`Margin before ads`,`Break-even ROAS for ads`],rows:[[`0% (no affiliate)`,`$0.00`,`$18.20`,`52.0%`,`1.92x`],[`10%`,`$3.50`,`$14.70`,`42.0%`,`2.38x`],[`15%`,`$5.25`,`$12.95`,`37.0%`,`2.70x`],[`20%`,`$7.00`,`$11.20`,`32.0%`,`3.13x`],[`25%`,`$8.75`,`$9.45`,`27.0%`,`3.70x`],[`30%`,`$10.50`,`$7.70`,`22.0%`,`4.55x`]]},subsections:[{h3:`Read the break-even column carefully`,content:`An organic creator order at 30% still makes $7.70. But when that same video runs in GMV Max at the full 30%, ads have to return at least 4.55x just to break even. That is the case for a lower Shop Ads rate: at 10% on ad orders, the break-even falls to 2.38x. Run your own numbers in the calculator with your real referral fee.`}]},{id:`samples`,h2:`Sample and seeding costs`,content:`Samples are part of your commission budget, not a separate marketing line. TikTok's Seller University says that for free samples the seller pays for the product, shipping and tax, and creators are expected to post within 14 days of receiving it. TikTok also offers refundable samples, where the creator buys the product and is refunded once their content drives a set number of sales.

In the example above, one free sample costs about $14 in product and shipping, roughly the profit from one creator order at a 15% rate. Illustrative maths: 20 samples cost $280, and the few creators whose videos sell have to cover all of it.`,list:[`Approve free samples for creators whose past sales and content fit your product, not every request.`,`Use refundable samples for unproven creators so the sample pays for itself only when it sells.`]},{id:`tiering`,h2:`Tiering commission by creator performance`,content:`Tie higher rates to results, not follower counts. Tiers keep costs predictable and give creators a reason to keep posting.`,list:[`Base tier: your open collaboration rate, available to everyone, set well below your maximum.`,`Proven tier: a targeted rate a few points higher for creators who have driven sales of your product, reviewed every month.`,`Top tier: your highest targeted rate, close to your ceiling, for the small group that drives most of your affiliate revenue.`,`Ad tier: a Shop Ads rate below the standard rate, so videos that GMV Max scales do not carry full commission on top of ad spend.`],subsections:[{h3:`Avoid creeping toward the ceiling`,content:`Every tier must sit under your maximum affordable rate. If top creators want more, offer early access, exclusive bundles or flat fees for specific videos instead.`}]},{id:`when-to-raise`,h2:`When to raise your commission rate`,content:`Raise your rate when the issue is creator interest and your margins have room, not when the product itself is not selling. A higher rate can attract creators but cannot fix a weak listing, price or content.`,list:[`Raise it when few creators request samples or add the product, and your rate is well below your ceiling.`,`Raise it for a launch, knowing a later cut takes 30 days to apply.`,`Do not raise it if creators are posting but videos do not convert. Fix the offer, the listing or the creator brief first.`,`Do not raise it if ads are already running on affiliate videos at the standard rate. Set a Shop Ads rate first.`],subsections:[{h3:`Honest caveats`,content:`No rate guarantees creator interest. TikTok's rules and fees change often and differ by country, so check Seller Center before each change. Our TikTok Shop agency team can set and run your affiliate plans, starting with a Brand Growth Audit.`}]}],faqs:[{question:`What commission should I offer TikTok Shop affiliates?`,answer:`Work it out from your margins. Take price minus product cost, shipping and the referral fee, divide by price, then subtract the margin you want to keep. On a $35 product with $8 cost, $6 shipping and an 8% referral fee, that is 52% minus your target margin, so 32% at a 20% target.`},{question:`What is the difference between an open plan and a targeted plan on TikTok Shop?`,answer:`An open plan, or open collaboration, is a public rate any eligible creator can use. A targeted plan, or target collaboration, is a private offer to chosen creators. TikTok says the target rate takes priority when a creator qualifies for both, so set it higher than your open rate.`},{question:`How is TikTok Shop affiliate commission calculated?`,answer:`According to TikTok's Seller University, commission equals revenue minus refunds, multiplied by the rate. The base is the price the buyer paid excluding tax and after seller discounts. Cancelled or fully refunded orders pay no commission.`},{question:`When are TikTok Shop affiliate commissions paid?`,answer:`TikTok's US help pages say commission is finalized after delivery and the after-sales period, typically released around 15 days after delivery, and can take up to 31 days if settlement, disputes or returns cause delays. It is deducted from the seller's order settlement.`},{question:`What is the Shop Ads commission rate on TikTok Shop?`,answer:`It is a separate commission for orders from ads that use a creator's video, including GMV Max. If you do not set one, the standard rate applies. TikTok's help center has said it cannot be below 30% of the standard rate in most regions, so check Seller Center for the current minimum.`}]}],m={name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, founder of Brand Scaling, published case studies with dashboard screenshots`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},h=[{slug:`facebook-ads-for-dropshipping`,title:`Facebook Ads for Dropshipping: How to Run Dropshipping Ads That Scale`,titleTag:`Facebook Ads for Dropshipping: 2026 Guide | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`How to run Facebook ads for dropshipping: break-even ROAS, account setup, creative, testing budgets, kill rules and scaling, with real store results.`,primaryKeyword:`facebook ads for dropshipping`,secondaryKeywords:[`dropshipping ads`,`dropshipping facebook ads`,`how to run ads for dropshipping`,`dropshipping ad budget`,`best ads for dropshipping`],publishDate:`2026-11-20`,updatedDate:`2026-11-20`,readTime:`11 min read`,category:`Meta Ads`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/how-to-find-winning-products`,title:`How to Find Winning Products`,text:`The research and testing process that comes before the ads.`},{href:`/tools/break-even-roas-calculator`,title:`Break-Even ROAS Calculator`,text:`Work out the lowest ROAS your store can run at before you spend.`},{href:`/case-studies/dropshipping-dtc`,title:`Dropshipping Case Studies`,text:`Dashboard proof from the dropshipping stores quoted in this guide.`},{href:`/ecommerce-advertising`,title:`Ecommerce Advertising Agency`,text:`Have Meta, TikTok and Google run for your store as one plan.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-library`,title:`Facebook Ad Library Guide`,text:`How to find long-running competitor ads to test.`}],author:m,directAnswer:`Facebook ads for dropshipping work when the product, the margin and the creative are right before the budget goes up. Work out your break-even ROAS first, launch with broad targeting and automatic bidding, test several new creatives per product, cut ads that spend past your break-even cost per purchase without converting, and scale winners in budget steps of 20% to 30% while ROAS holds above break-even.`,definitionBox:{term:`Dropshipping ads`,definition:`Paid ads, usually on Facebook, Instagram and TikTok, that send shoppers to a store which sells products shipped directly by a supplier. Because the store holds no stock, the ad account is where most of the money and risk sit.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-are-dropshipping-ads`,text:`What dropshipping ads are and why Facebook still leads`},{id:`before-you-spend`,text:`Before you spend: product, margin and tracking`},{id:`account-setup`,text:`How to set up the ad account`},{id:`creative`,text:`Creative that sells dropshipping products`},{id:`testing`,text:`Testing budgets and kill rules`},{id:`scaling`,text:`How to scale a winning product`},{id:`real-results`,text:`Real results from dropshipping stores`},{id:`mistakes`,text:`Common dropshipping ad mistakes`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-are-dropshipping-ads`,h2:`What dropshipping ads are and why Facebook still leads`,content:`Dropshipping ads are paid ads that send shoppers to a store selling products the store does not stock. Facebook and Instagram, run through Meta Ads Manager, remain the main channel for most dropshipping stores because they can find buyers for a product people were not searching for. TikTok works for the same reason and shares much of the same creative.

Google Shopping can work for products people already search for, but most dropshipping products are new to the buyer, so demand has to be created. That is Meta's strength, and it is where most of the published dropshipping results on this site come from.`},{id:`before-you-spend`,h2:`Before you spend: product, margin and tracking`,content:`Most failed dropshipping ad accounts lose money before the first ad runs, because the numbers could never work. Check three things first.

The product has to solve a real problem or show a clear before and after on video. The margin has to leave room for ads: subtract product cost, shipping, payment fees and an allowance for refunds from your price, and divide 1 by what is left as a share of price. That is your break-even ROAS. A store with 40% left needs 2.5x just to break even; one with 25% left needs 4x.

Tracking has to be right. Install the Meta pixel and the Conversions API through your Shopify app, check that purchases are deduplicated, and compare Meta-reported purchases with Shopify orders after the first days. Bad tracking makes good ads look bad.`,list:[`Break-even ROAS = 1 / (contribution margin as a share of price).`,`Check shipping times and refund risk before you calculate the margin, not after.`,`Pixel plus Conversions API, deduplicated, checked against Shopify orders.`,`A product page that loads fast on mobile and matches what the ad promises.`]},{id:`account-setup`,h2:`How to set up the ad account`,content:`Keep the structure simple. One testing campaign for new products and creatives, and one scaling campaign for proven winners, is enough for most stores. Too many campaigns and ad sets split a small budget so none of them gathers enough purchases to exit the learning phase.

In the $148K-in-a-day store, the team launched with automatic bidding and broad targeting from the start, and used manual bidding only once an offer showed consistent results. Broad targeting lets Meta find buyers from the creative itself, which is why the creative matters more than interest stacks.`,subsections:[{h3:`Testing campaign`,content:`New products and new creatives, a modest budget per ad set, broad targeting, purchase as the optimisation event.`},{h3:`Scaling campaign`,content:`Only proven winners move here, usually into a campaign budget (CBO or Advantage+ shopping) where Meta shifts spend to the best ads.`},{h3:`Retargeting`,content:`Small and optional at the start. Broad prospecting already reaches many past visitors; add dedicated retargeting once there is enough traffic to justify it.`}]},{id:`creative`,h2:`Creative that sells dropshipping products`,content:`With broad targeting, the ad creative decides who sees the ad and whether they buy. Short vertical video that shows the problem and the product solving it works best for most dropshipping products.

The $185,936-in-21-days store credits three basics: a product that solves a real problem, ad messaging that speaks to the customer's pain point, and a simple funnel, without over-engineered targeting. Build every creative around that: hook in the first two seconds, the problem, the product in use, proof such as reviews or a before and after, then the offer and one call to action.

Test several angles for each product, not one. An angle is the reason someone would buy: saving time, looking better, avoiding a specific problem. When a winner fatigues, a new hook on the same angle usually works faster than starting again.`,list:[`Hook in the first 1 to 3 seconds: the problem, a surprising visual or a strong claim.`,`Show the product working in real use, ideally filmed on a phone.`,`Use proof: reviews, before and after, or a short demonstration.`,`End with the offer and a single call to action.`,`Launch several angles per product and replace winners before frequency rises too far.`]},{id:`testing`,h2:`Testing budgets and kill rules`,content:`Testing is where dropshipping stores win or run out of money. Decide your rules before the ads run, so the numbers make the decision rather than hope.

Set a break-even cost per purchase from your margin (price multiplied by the margin share). An ad set that spends that amount without a sale is a warning; one that spends around twice that without a sale is usually cut. Ads that sell below break-even cost per purchase stay on and get more creative behind them.

Expect most products to fail. In the $148K-in-a-day store, 25 products were tested, six showed promise and one became the consistent winner. In the $100K-in-23-days store, almost 23 products were tested and 7 became winners. Budget for the losers; they are part of finding the winner.`,table:{headers:[`Signal after testing spend`,`What it usually means`,`Action`],rows:[[`No sale after about 1x break-even CPA`,`Too early to judge`,`Let it run if clicks and add to carts look healthy`],[`No sale after about 2x break-even CPA`,`The ad or the product is not working`,`Cut the ad set; test a new angle or product`],[`Sales above break-even CPA`,`Interest but weak economics`,`Test new creative, offer or bundle before scaling`],[`Sales below break-even CPA for several days`,`A potential winner`,`Move it to the scaling campaign and add new creatives`]]}},{id:`scaling`,h2:`How to scale a winning product`,content:`Scale in steps, not jumps. Raise budgets by about 20% to 30% at a time while ROAS stays above break-even, and keep adding new creatives on the winning angle so the ads do not fatigue as frequency rises.

When one product proves itself, build the store around it. The $148K-in-a-day store started as a general store and was niched around its winning product before it scaled. A focused store, product page and offer convert better than a general store selling the same item.

Then widen the offer. Bundles, quantity breaks and post-purchase upsells raise order value, which lets you pay more for each customer and keep scaling as costs rise.`},{id:`real-results`,h2:`Real results from dropshipping stores`,content:`These are published Brand Scaling case studies from dropshipping stores, each with dashboard screenshots. They show what is possible, not what every store will get.`,table:{headers:[`Store result`,`Detail`],rows:[[`$2.3M in sales at 11.6x ROAS`,`$200,000 of Meta ad spend in 2022, one consolidated structure`],[`$148K in one day`,`25 products tested, one winner, 37% net margin for the month`],[`$133,420 in 7 days at 4.06x ROAS`,`$32,857 of ad spend in Q4`],[`$185,936 in 21 days`,`56% profit margin`],[`$10,809 in 24 hours at 3.75x ROAS`,`One product, $2,882 of ad spend`]]}},{id:`mistakes`,h2:`Common dropshipping ad mistakes`,content:`The same mistakes appear in most dropshipping accounts we review.`,list:[`Launching before calculating break-even ROAS, so no one knows whether the ads make money.`,`Testing one creative per product and calling the product a loser.`,`Splitting a small budget across many interest ad sets that never exit learning.`,`Raising budgets too fast after one good day.`,`Leaving tracking unchecked, so Meta optimises on missing purchases.`,`Keeping a general store after a clear winner appears, instead of building around it.`]}],faqs:[{question:`How much should I spend on Facebook ads for dropshipping?`,answer:`Start from your break-even cost per purchase: price multiplied by your margin share. Give each product test enough budget to spend about two times that figure, and keep enough in reserve to test several products, because most will fail. In published cases, stores tested around 23 to 25 products to find their winners.`},{question:`What is a good ROAS for dropshipping?`,answer:`Any ROAS comfortably above your break-even ROAS, which is 1 divided by your margin share. Published dropshipping results on this site range from 3.75x to 11.6x, but a store with thin margins can lose money at 3x while a high-margin store can profit at 2x.`},{question:`Are Facebook ads still worth it for dropshipping?`,answer:`Yes, for products that solve a clear problem and can be shown on video, with enough margin to pay for ads. They do not work for commodity products with thin margins or products people can buy faster on a marketplace.`},{question:`Should I use TikTok or Facebook ads for dropshipping?`,answer:`Facebook and Instagram are usually the main channel because Meta finds buyers well with broad targeting. TikTok works well for visual products and younger audiences and can reuse the same short videos. Many stores start on Meta and add TikTok once a product is proven.`},{question:`How long should I test a dropshipping product?`,answer:`Until it has spent roughly one to two times your break-even cost per purchase, usually a few days. Judge it on cost per purchase against break-even, not on clicks or likes.`}]},{slug:`how-to-find-winning-products`,title:`How to Find Winning Products: The Research and Testing Process Behind Real Stores`,titleTag:`How to Find Winning Products in 2026 | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`How to find winning products for dropshipping and DTC: what makes a winner, where to research, how to test with ads, and what real stores found.`,primaryKeyword:`winning products`,secondaryKeywords:[`how to find winning products`,`winning products dropshipping`,`winning product criteria`,`product research dropshipping`,`how to test products with facebook ads`],publishDate:`2026-11-20`,updatedDate:`2026-11-20`,readTime:`9 min read`,category:`Strategy & Scaling`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/facebook-ads-for-dropshipping`,title:`Facebook Ads for Dropshipping`,text:`How to test and scale a product once you have found it.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-library`,title:`Facebook Ad Library Guide`,text:`Find long-running competitor ads, a strong sign of a proven product.`},{href:`/tools/profit-margin-calculator`,title:`Profit Margin Calculator`,text:`Check a product leaves enough margin for ads before you test it.`},{href:`/case-studies/dropshipping-dtc`,title:`Dropshipping Case Studies`,text:`Stores that found and scaled winning products, with dashboard proof.`}],author:m,directAnswer:`A winning product solves a clear problem, can be shown working in a short video, leaves enough margin to pay for ads, and is not easy to buy cheaper elsewhere. Find candidates by studying long-running ads in the Facebook Ad Library, organic TikTok content and customer reviews, then prove them with small ad tests judged against your break-even cost per purchase. Expect most products to fail: in published cases, stores tested around 23 to 25 products to find their winners.`,definitionBox:{term:`Winning product`,definition:`A product that sells profitably through paid ads at a cost per purchase below your break-even point, and keeps doing so as the budget increases.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-makes-a-winner`,text:`What makes a winning product`},{id:`where-to-research`,text:`Where to find product ideas`},{id:`spy-tools`,text:`Why spy tools alone are not enough`},{id:`testing`,text:`How to test products with ads`},{id:`hit-rate`,text:`How many products you will need to test`},{id:`after-the-winner`,text:`What to do once you find a winner`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-makes-a-winner`,h2:`What makes a winning product`,content:`A winning product is not just a product that sells. It sells profitably through ads and keeps selling as the budget grows. The products behind the results on this site tend to share the same traits.`,list:[`It solves a real problem or creates an obvious before and after.`,`It can be demonstrated in a few seconds of video, so the ad sells it.`,`Its margin leaves room for ad costs: check break-even ROAS before testing.`,`It is not a commodity buyers can get cheaper and faster on a marketplace.`,`It ships reliably, with low breakage and refund risk.`,`It has room for bundles or upsells, which raise order value.`]},{id:`where-to-research`,h2:`Where to find product ideas`,content:`The best product ideas come from evidence that real people are already buying. These are the sources we use.`,subsections:[{h3:`Facebook Ad Library`,content:`Search a product type and look for ads that have been running for two weeks or more. Advertisers rarely keep paying for ads that lose money, so long-running ads are a strong signal.`},{h3:`Organic TikTok and Instagram`,content:`Videos where people show a product solving a problem, with comments asking where to buy it, show demand before ads saturate it.`},{h3:`Customer reviews`,content:`Reviews on large marketplaces show what buyers love and what they complain about. Complaints about a popular product point to a better version you could sell.`},{h3:`Competitor stores`,content:`Stores that keep a product at the top of their catalog, run bundles on it and advertise it consistently are usually making money on it.`}]},{id:`spy-tools`,h2:`Why spy tools alone are not enough`,content:`Spy tools and product apps show the same trending products to thousands of stores at the same time. By the time a product appears at the top of those lists, many competitors are already advertising it, costs rise and margins fall.

Use tools to find ideas, then do the research yourself: check how long ads have run, read reviews, compare prices and calculate your margin. The $148K-in-a-day case makes the same point: the product research behind it was done manually rather than taken from spy tools and apps.`},{id:`testing`,h2:`How to test products with ads`,content:`Research only produces candidates. Ads prove which ones are winners. Before testing, calculate your break-even cost per purchase: your price multiplied by the share of it left after product cost, shipping, fees and refunds.

Launch each product with several creatives on different angles, broad targeting and purchase as the optimisation event. Judge it on cost per purchase, not on clicks or likes. A product that sells below break-even cost per purchase for several days is a candidate winner; one that spends about twice your break-even cost per purchase without a sale is usually cut.`},{id:`hit-rate`,h2:`How many products you will need to test`,content:`More than most people expect. These are the published examples on this site.`,table:{headers:[`Store`,`Products tested`,`Result`],rows:[[`General store, later niched`,`25 tested, 6 showed promise, 1 consistent winner`,`$148K in one day, $294K month at 37% net margin`],[`High-ticket store`,`Almost 23 tested, 7 winners`,`$100K in 23 days`],[`Dropshipping store`,`One product scaled`,`$10,809 in 24 hours on $2,882 ad spend, 3.75x ROAS`]]}},{id:`after-the-winner`,h2:`What to do once you find a winner`,content:`Finding a winner is the start, not the finish. The stores that went on to large results did three things next.

They built the store around the product: the $148K-in-a-day store was niched around its winner before it scaled. They kept the creative fresh, adding new hooks on the winning angle as frequency rose. And they raised order value with bundles and upsells, which lets the ads afford a higher cost per customer as the budget grows.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is a winning product in dropshipping?`,answer:`A product that sells profitably through paid ads, at a cost per purchase below your break-even point, and keeps doing so as the budget increases. Selling a few units is not enough; it has to stay profitable at scale.`},{question:`How do I find winning products for free?`,answer:`Use the free Facebook Ad Library to find ads that have run for two weeks or more, browse organic TikTok and Instagram for products people ask about, and read marketplace reviews. Then check the margin before spending anything on tests.`},{question:`How many products should I test before finding a winner?`,answer:`Plan for many. In published cases on this site, one store tested 25 products to find one consistent winner, and another tested about 23 to find 7.`},{question:`Are winning product lists worth buying?`,answer:`Usually not on their own. Lists and spy tools show the same products to many stores at once, which raises competition. Use them for ideas, then do your own research and testing.`},{question:`What margin does a winning product need?`,answer:`Enough that your break-even ROAS is realistic. If 40% of the price is left after product cost, shipping, fees and refunds, you break even at 2.5x ROAS; at 25% you need 4x, which is much harder to sustain.`}]}],g={title:`What Is ROAS? The ROAS Formula, Meaning and Examples`,updatedDate:`2026-11-10`,readTime:`11 min read`,secondaryKeywords:[`roas formula`,`roas meaning`,`how to calculate roas`,`break even roas`,`acos to roas`,`gross roas`,`net roas`,`roas tracking tools`],directAnswer:`ROAS (return on ad spend) is the revenue your ads generate for every dollar spent on them. The ROAS formula is revenue attributed to ads divided by ad spend: $20,000 in sales from $5,000 of ads is a 4x ROAS. Whether 4x is profitable depends on your margin, which sets your break-even ROAS.`,definitionBox:{term:`ROAS (Return On Ad Spend)`,definition:`Revenue attributed to advertising divided by the cost of that advertising, written as a ratio (4x or 4:1) or a percentage (400%). It measures revenue efficiency, not profit.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`roas-formula`,text:`The ROAS formula: how to calculate ROAS`},{id:`roas-meaning`,text:`ROAS meaning: what the number tells you`},{id:`break-even-formula`,text:`Break-even ROAS: the number that matters`},{id:`roas-vs-roi`,text:`ROAS vs ROI`},{id:`roas-vs-mer`,text:`ROAS vs MER`},{id:`gross-vs-net-roas`,text:`Gross ROAS vs net ROAS`},{id:`target-roas`,text:`How to set a target ROAS`},{id:`how-to-improve-roas`,text:`How to improve ecommerce ROAS`},{id:`roas-in-practice`,text:`What ROAS looks like in real accounts`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`roas-formula`,h2:`The ROAS formula: how to calculate ROAS`,content:`ROAS = revenue attributed to ads / ad spend.

Take the revenue your ad platform (or your analytics) credits to a campaign, and divide it by what you spent on that campaign over the same dates. If a Meta campaign spends $5,000 in a month and is credited with $20,000 of Shopify orders, ROAS = $20,000 / $5,000 = 4.0, usually written 4x, 4:1 or 400%.

Three rules keep the number honest: use the same date range for spend and revenue, use the same attribution setting every time you compare (for example 7-day click, 1-day view on Meta), and use revenue after discounts but before tax and shipping charged to the customer.`,table:{headers:[`Ad spend`,`Attributed revenue`,`ROAS`,`As a percentage`],rows:[[`$1,000`,`$2,000`,`2.0x`,`200%`],[`$5,000`,`$20,000`,`4.0x`,`400%`],[`$40,124`,`$146,184`,`3.64x`,`364% (real skincare account, 27 days)`],[`$28,391`,`$112,866`,`3.98x`,`398% (real early-BFCM result, 13 days)`]]},subsections:[{h3:`ROAS as a percentage, and converting ACoS to ROAS`,content:`ROAS as a percentage is the same number times 100: 3.5x is 350%. Amazon reports the inverse, ACoS (advertising cost of sale = ad spend / ad revenue). To convert, divide 1 by ACoS: an ACoS of 25% is a ROAS of 1 / 0.25 = 4x, and a 5x ROAS is an ACoS of 20%.`},{h3:`Mistakes that make ROAS look better than it is`,list:[`Comparing a 7-day-click ROAS this month with a 1-day-click ROAS last month.`,`Counting revenue that includes tax and shipping fees.`,`Adding up ROAS from Meta, TikTok and Google: each platform credits itself, so the same order is often counted more than once.`,`Judging retargeting and prospecting together: retargeting looks high because many of those buyers would have purchased anyway.`]}]},{id:`roas-meaning`,h2:`ROAS meaning: what the number tells you`,content:`ROAS stands for return on ad spend. As a marketing term it answers one question: for every dollar spent on ads, how many dollars of revenue came back?

It measures revenue, not profit. A 4x ROAS means $4 of sales for every $1 of ads, before product cost, shipping, payment fees and returns are taken out. That is why the same 4x can be very profitable for a store with 70 percent margins and lose money for a store with 25 percent margins. ROAS is the right number for comparing ads and campaigns quickly; it is the wrong number for deciding whether the business is making money.`},{id:`break-even-formula`,h2:`Break-even ROAS: the number that matters`,content:`Break-even ROAS is the ROAS at which an order pays for its product, fulfilment and ad cost with nothing left over.

Break-even ROAS = 1 / contribution margin %, where contribution margin is what is left of the order value after product cost (COGS), shipping and fulfilment, and payment fees.

Example: an $80 order with $24 product cost, $9 shipping and a 3 percent payment fee leaves $44.60, a 55.75 percent contribution margin. Break-even ROAS = 1 / 0.5575 = 1.79x. Below 1.79x that order loses money on ads; above it, the difference is profit. Work out your own with the break-even ROAS calculator before setting any target.`,table:{headers:[`Contribution margin`,`Break-even ROAS`],rows:[[`70%`,`1.43x`],[`60%`,`1.67x`],[`50%`,`2.0x`],[`40%`,`2.5x`],[`30%`,`3.33x`]]}},{id:`roas-vs-roi`,h2:`ROAS vs ROI: what is the difference?`,content:`ROAS compares revenue to ad spend only. ROI (return on investment) compares profit to every cost. A campaign can have a 3x ROAS and a negative ROI if product cost, shipping and fees take more than two thirds of the revenue.

Use ROAS to compare ads and campaigns day to day, because it is fast and every platform reports it. Use profit after ad spend (or ROI) to decide whether to keep scaling. The free ROAS calculator shows both: break-even ROAS and profit per order.`},{id:`roas-vs-mer`,h2:`ROAS vs MER: which should you scale on?`,content:`Since iOS 14.5 and browser privacy changes, platform-reported ROAS misses some sales and double counts others. MER (marketing efficiency ratio) avoids attribution entirely: total store revenue divided by total marketing spend across every channel.

Run campaigns on platform ROAS, because it is the only number available at ad level. Judge the business on MER and new-customer acquisition cost, because they match what actually lands in Shopify. If Meta ROAS dips but MER holds, the ads are usually still working and the purchases are being credited elsewhere.`},{id:`gross-vs-net-roas`,h2:`Gross ROAS vs net ROAS`,content:`Gross ROAS is the number ad platforms report: total attributed revenue divided by ad spend, before anything is taken off. Net ROAS uses the revenue you actually keep, after refunds, returns, discounts, shipping charged and sales tax are removed.

The gap matters most for brands with high return rates or heavy discounting. If ads drive $40,000 in attributed sales on $10,000 of spend, gross ROAS is 4x. If $4,000 of those orders are refunded and $2,000 was tax and shipping, net revenue is $34,000 and net ROAS is 3.4x. Compare net ROAS, not gross, with your break-even ROAS, because break-even is calculated from margin on the money you keep.`,list:[`ROAS tracking tools: Meta Ads Manager, TikTok Ads Manager and Google Ads report platform (gross) ROAS per campaign and ad.`,`Shopify analytics shows net sales after returns and discounts, which you need for net ROAS and MER.`,`Server-side tracking (Meta Conversions API, TikTok Events API) recovers purchases that browser pixels miss.`,`Attribution tools such as Triple Whale or Northbeam combine store and ad data in one view; a spreadsheet of daily Shopify net sales against total ad spend gives you MER for free.`]},{id:`target-roas`,h2:`How to set a target ROAS`,content:`Start from margin, not from someone else's benchmark. Work out break-even ROAS first, then decide how much profit each order must leave after ads.`,list:[`Contribution margin 60%: break-even 1.67x; a target around 2.5x leaves room for profit.`,`Contribution margin 50%: break-even 2.0x; target around 3x.`,`Contribution margin 40%: break-even 2.5x; target 3.5x to 4x, or raise order value with bundles first.`,`Subscription or high-repeat brands can accept a lower first-order target, because repeat purchases pay back the acquisition cost.`]},{id:`how-to-improve-roas`,h2:`How to improve ecommerce ROAS`,content:`ROAS rises when either more revenue comes from the same spend or the same revenue costs less to buy. In practice the biggest levers are, in order:`,list:[`Creative volume: test new hooks and angles every week so the best ad is always fresh. Our partner accounts launch 30 to 50+ new creatives a month.`,`Order value: bundles, quantity breaks and post-purchase upsells lift revenue per order without extra ad spend.`,`Conversion rate: a fast mobile product page that answers the main buying objection converts more of the traffic you already pay for.`,`Account structure: fewer, broader campaigns (Advantage+ or broad targeting) exit the learning phase faster than many small, overlapping ad sets.`,`Tracking: a working Conversions API means Meta optimises on real purchases, not on the share it can still see.`]},{id:`roas-in-practice`,h2:`What ROAS looks like in real accounts`,content:`Across the 25 Brand Scaling case studies that publish a ROAS, the median is 3.97x and the middle half sits between 3.61x and 4.39x. By niche: supplements 3.89x to 4.51x, fashion 3.56x to 5.49x, fitness 3.61x to 4.37x, skincare 3.64x to 4.39x. These are accounts that scaled well enough to publish, so treat them as what good looks like, not as an average.

For the full breakdown by niche, see the ecommerce ROAS benchmarks; for how to judge your own number against your margin, see what a good ROAS is for ecommerce.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is the ROAS formula?`,answer:`ROAS equals revenue attributed to ads divided by ad spend over the same period. If a campaign spends $5,000 and is credited with $20,000 in sales, ROAS is $20,000 / $5,000 = 4x, also written 4:1 or 400%. Use the same date range and the same attribution setting whenever you compare two ROAS numbers.`},{question:`What does ROAS mean in marketing?`,answer:`ROAS means return on ad spend: the revenue your advertising brings in for every dollar it costs. A 3x ROAS means $3 of sales per $1 of ads. It is a revenue measure, not a profit measure, so it has to be read against your margins and break-even ROAS before you can tell whether ads are making money.`},{question:`How do I calculate break-even ROAS?`,answer:`Divide 1 by your contribution margin, which is what remains of the order value after product cost, shipping and payment fees. With a 55.75 percent contribution margin, break-even ROAS is 1 / 0.5575 = 1.79x. Any ROAS below that loses money on each ad-driven order; anything above it is profit before overheads.`},{question:`How do you convert ACoS to ROAS?`,answer:`ROAS is the inverse of ACoS. Divide 1 by ACoS written as a decimal: an ACoS of 25 percent is 1 / 0.25 = 4x ROAS, and 20 percent ACoS is 5x ROAS. To go the other way, divide 1 by ROAS: a 2.5x ROAS equals a 40 percent ACoS, the metric Amazon advertising reports by default.`},{question:`Is a higher ROAS always better?`,answer:`No. Very high ROAS often means spend is limited to retargeting and people who would have bought anyway. Growing brands deliberately accept a lower ROAS on prospecting to reach new customers, as long as it stays above break-even and blended MER holds. Total profit dollars matter more than the highest possible ratio.`},{question:`What is the difference between ROAS and ROI?`,answer:`ROAS compares revenue to ad spend only. ROI compares profit to all costs, including product, shipping, fees and overheads. A campaign can show a healthy 3x ROAS and still have a negative ROI if those costs take more than two thirds of revenue, which is why margins have to be checked first.`}],relatedLinks:[{href:`/tools/break-even-roas-calculator`,title:`Break-Even ROAS Calculator`,text:`Enter price, product cost, shipping and fees to get your break-even ROAS.`},{href:`/blog/good-roas-for-ecommerce`,title:`What Is a Good ROAS for Ecommerce?`,text:`How to judge your ROAS against your own margin.`},{href:`/benchmarks/ecommerce-roas`,title:`Ecommerce ROAS Benchmarks`,text:`ROAS by niche from 25 published case studies, every figure sourced.`},{href:`/roas-calculator`,title:`ROAS Calculator with Profit Model`,text:`Break-even ROAS, profit per order and monthly profit in one model.`}]},_={id:`what-is-dtc-performance-marketing`,h2:`What is DTC performance marketing?`,content:`DTC performance marketing is paid advertising for direct-to-consumer brands that is judged only on what it sells. An ecommerce performance marketing agency is the team that runs it for you, paid to grow orders and profit rather than reach. Every campaign on Meta, TikTok or Google is measured on orders, revenue, cost per new customer and profit after ad spend, not on reach or awareness.

It differs from performance marketing for lead generation or apps in three ways. The product is bought on the spot, often from a single video, so creative carries most of the work. The store is part of the funnel, so product page speed, the offer and checkout decide how much of the paid traffic converts. And margins are thin enough that the same ROAS can be profitable for one brand and a loss for another, so targets start from unit economics rather than benchmarks.`},v={id:`roas-improvement-agency`,h2:`What an ecommerce ROAS improvement agency actually changes`,content:`When an agency promises to improve ROAS, ask what it will change, because ROAS only moves for a few reasons: more revenue from the same spend, or the same revenue for less spend. In practice the work falls into four areas.`,list:[`Creative: new hooks and angles every week, so spend moves to fresh winners instead of fatigued ads.`,`Account structure: fewer, broader campaigns with clear budget and bid rules, so the platform optimises on enough purchases.`,`Store and offer: product page, bundles and post-purchase upsells, so each click is worth more.`,`Measurement: Conversions API and blended MER, so you can see whether a higher platform ROAS is real or only a shift in attribution.`]},y={id:`choose-dtc-growth-agency`,h2:`How to find an ecommerce growth agency that specializes in scaling DTC brands`,content:`An ecommerce growth agency that specializes in DTC brands works only, or mostly, with brands that sell their own products online, and it can show published results from stores like yours. Generalist agencies that also serve SaaS, local businesses or lead generation tend to optimize for leads and clicks, not for orders and contribution margin.

Use five checks before you sign. They apply whether you are hiring for Meta, TikTok or a Google Ads agency.`,list:[`Proof in your range: case studies with spend, revenue and ROAS from brands at your monthly revenue, not only logos.`,`Creative in-house: DTC scaling depends on new ads every week, so ask who writes, films and edits them.`,`Profit-based targets: they should ask for your margins and set a break-even ROAS before setting any target.`,`Store work included: product page, offer and checkout changes, because paid traffic converts on your Shopify store.`,`You own everything: ad accounts, pixel, data and creative stay in your name, and reporting shows blended MER, not platform ROAS alone.`]},b=[{question:`Which Google Ads agencies are best for scaling DTC ecommerce brands?`,answer:`The best Google Ads agencies for DTC brands run Shopping and Performance Max from a clean product feed, split branded from non-branded search, and judge results on new-customer revenue and blended MER rather than Google-reported ROAS. Ask for DTC case studies with spend and revenue at your size before choosing one.`},{question:`What does an ecommerce growth agency that specializes in DTC do differently?`,answer:`It plans around orders and margin instead of leads: creative testing every week, account structures that give the algorithm enough purchases, store and offer changes that raise conversion rate and order value, and reporting on contribution margin and new-customer CAC.`}],x=e=>{let t=[...e.sections],n=t.findIndex(e=>e.id===`what-is-performance-marketing`)+1;t.splice(n,0,_,v,y);let r=[...e.tableOfContents],i=r.findIndex(e=>e.id===`what-is-performance-marketing`)+1;return r.splice(i,0,{id:_.id,text:`What is DTC performance marketing?`},{id:v.id,text:`What a ROAS improvement agency changes`},{id:y.id,text:`Finding an agency that specializes in DTC`}),{sections:t,tableOfContents:r,faqs:[...e.faqs||[],...b],metaDescription:`What a DTC performance marketing agency does: Meta, TikTok and Google ads, creative, CRO and margin-based reporting, plus how to choose one.`,secondaryKeywords:[...e.secondaryKeywords||[],`ecommerce performance marketing agency`,`dtc performance marketing agency`],updatedDate:`2026-11-12`}},S={id:`affiliate-roas`,h2:`How to measure affiliate ROAS`,content:`Affiliate ROAS is the revenue affiliates drive divided by everything the program costs: commissions, the discount on affiliate codes, free product sent to creators, platform fees and any paid ads that boost affiliate content. Commission alone sets a ceiling, not a benchmark. At a 15% commission, commission-only ROAS is always 6.67x (100 / 15), and at 20% it is 5x, so a number like that says nothing about whether the program works.

The useful benchmark is your own break-even ROAS. Add every program cost, divide affiliate revenue by it, and compare the result with the break-even ROAS you use for paid ads. Then check the share of affiliate orders from new customers. A program that mostly pays commission on orders from existing customers can show a high ROAS while adding very little revenue the brand would not have made anyway.`,table:{headers:[`Commission rate`,`Commission-only ROAS`,`What to add before judging it`],rows:[[`10%`,`10x`,`Discount on the code, product seeding, platform fees`],[`15%`,`6.67x`,`Same, plus any Spark Ads or partnership ads spend`],[`20%`,`5x`,`Same, and check the new-customer share closely`],[`30%`,`3.33x`,`Usually only works with high gross margins`]]}},C={id:`scaling-challenges`,h2:`Affiliate marketing scaling challenges for ecommerce brands`,content:`Most ecommerce brands hit the same problems when they try to grow an affiliate program past its first few dozen partners.`,list:[`Coupon sites take the credit: code-sharing and deal sites claim sales from shoppers who were already at checkout. Pay those partners a lower rate or exclude them.`,`Few active affiliates: a large share of sign-ups never post. Judge recruiting on active affiliates per month, not total sign-ups.`,`Content that does not sell: creators make lifestyle posts instead of product demos. Send a short brief with hooks and the main product benefit.`,`Attribution conflicts: the same order is claimed by an affiliate link, a Meta ad and an email. Decide which source gets paid and use blended MER for the overall picture.`,`Margin leaks: a high commission stacked on a large discount code can turn a sale into a loss. Model the full cost per order before you raise rates.`,`Slow payouts: late or confusing payments make the best creators stop posting. Pay on a fixed schedule after the refund window.`]},w=e=>{let t=[...e.sections],n=t.findIndex(e=>e.id===`tracking-and-metrics`)+1;t.splice(n,0,S,C);let r=[...e.tableOfContents],i=r.findIndex(e=>e.id===`tracking-and-metrics`)+1;return r.splice(i,0,{id:S.id,text:`How to measure affiliate ROAS`},{id:C.id,text:`Affiliate scaling challenges`}),{sections:t,tableOfContents:r,updatedDate:`2026-11-10`,secondaryKeywords:[...e.secondaryKeywords||[],`affiliate roas benchmark`,`affiliate marketing scaling challenges`]}},T=[{slug:`bfcm-meta-ads-playbook`,title:`BFCM 2026: The Meta Ads Playbook for Shopify Brands`,titleTag:`BFCM Ads 2026: Black Friday Meta Playbook | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`BFCM ads for 26 to 30 Nov 2026: a last-minute Meta checklist, Black Friday and Cyber Monday budget plan, offers and creative from real client accounts.`,primaryKeyword:`bfcm`,secondaryKeywords:[`bfcm ad strategy`,`bfcm ads`,`black friday ads`,`what is bfcm in marketing`,`bfcm dates 2026`,`best bfcm ads`],publishDate:`2026-10-22`,updatedDate:`2026-11-22`,readTime:`16 min read`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/bfcm-ads-management`,title:`BFCM Ads Management`,text:`Hand the Black Friday and Cyber Monday account to us: structure, creative and daily budget checks.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`Get the BFCM creative bank and campaign structure built for you.`},{href:`/case-studies/apparel-fashion`,title:`Apparel & Fashion Case Studies`,text:`The 4-day BFCM peak result: $59,241 from $16,630 at 3.56x ROAS.`},{href:`/roas-calculator`,title:`Free ROAS Calculator`,text:`Set your break-even ROAS before the peak weekend.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-library`,title:`Meta Ads Library Guide`,text:`Find the Black Friday ads competitors kept running last year.`}],category:`Strategy & Scaling`,author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`BFCM stands for Black Friday Cyber Monday, the biggest sales weekend in ecommerce. In 2026 it runs from Thanksgiving on Thursday 26 November through Black Friday on 27 November to Cyber Monday on 30 November. For Meta ads, the brands that win build creative and audiences in early November, open early access offers mid month and scale with cost controls during the peak.`,definitionBox:{term:`BFCM (Black Friday Cyber Monday)`,definition:`The critical 5 day holiday shopping weekend extending from Thanksgiving to Cyber Monday. For high growth Shopify DTC brands, it often accounts for 20% to 35% of annual net revenue.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-is-bfcm`,text:`What is BFCM in marketing?`},{id:`bfcm-timeline`,text:`BFCM 2026 dates and the Meta ads timeline`},{id:`bfcm-countdown`,text:`Three weeks out: the BFCM countdown checklist`},{id:`bfcm-last-two-weeks`,text:`BFCM ads in the last two weeks (14 to 26 Nov)`},{id:`bfcm-last-minute`,text:`Last-minute BFCM ads checklist (22 to 26 Nov)`},{id:`cyber-monday-ads`,text:`Cyber Monday ads (30 Nov): the final push`},{id:`phase-1-creative`,text:`Phase 1 (3 to 13 Nov): Build the creative bank`},{id:`phase-2-early-access`,text:`Phase 2 (16 to 25 Nov): Early access & warm audiences`},{id:`phase-3-scale`,text:`Phase 3 (26 to 30 Nov): Scale without burning margin`},{id:`winning-offers`,text:`BFCM offers that work on Meta`},{id:`ad-patterns`,text:`What the best BFCM ads have in common`},{id:`bfcm-ad-strategy`,text:`BFCM ad strategy: the campaign structure that holds CPA`},{id:`bfcm-budget-plan`,text:`BFCM budget plan by week`},{id:`bfcm-retargeting`,text:`Retargeting during Black Friday`},{id:`bfcm-creative-checklist`,text:`BFCM creative checklist`},{id:`bfcm-results`,text:`BFCM and Q4 results from client accounts`},{id:`after-bfcm`,text:`After Cyber Monday: December momentum`},{id:`bfcm-mistakes`,text:`BFCM mistakes that cost the most`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-is-bfcm`,h2:`What is BFCM in marketing?`,content:`BFCM stands for Black Friday Cyber Monday. In modern direct to consumer ecommerce marketing, BFCM has expanded beyond a single weekend into a 3 to 4 week promotional cycle where customer acquisition volume and ad spend surge across Meta, TikTok, and Google.

Top performing Shopify brands do not wait until Thanksgiving morning to launch campaigns. They utilize October and early November to stress test high converting video hooks, consolidate audience signals into broad Advantage+ setups, and build high intent VIP waitlists before ad auction CPMs double.`},{id:`bfcm-timeline`,h2:`BFCM 2026 dates and the Meta ads timeline`,content:`Timing ad budget deployment is the single biggest factor separating profitable 4x+ ROAS scale from wasted margin burn. Below is the exact operational calendar we deploy across our partner ad accounts:`,table:{headers:[`Date Window`,`Day`,`Meta Ads Action Plan`,`Target Metric Focus`],rows:[[`Tue 3 Nov to Fri 13 Nov`,`Pre Season Phase 1`,`Produce 20+ creative variations; test angles & hooks at low budget`,`Thumbstop Rate (>35%), Hold Rate`],[`Mon 16 Nov to Wed 25 Nov`,`Early Access Phase 2`,`Launch VIP early access; list building via Lead/Conversion ads`,`Early ROAS (>3.5x), CPA stability`],[`Thu 26 Nov`,`Thanksgiving`,`Scale top 3 creative winners; turn on cost caps for midnight surge`,`Conversion Rate (>4%)`],[`Fri 27 Nov`,`Black Friday`,`Full budget release on Advantage+ & bid capped scaling campaigns`,`Daily Revenue Milestone`],[`Sat 28 to Sun 29 Nov`,`Cyber Weekend`,`Consolidate budget; push quantity breaks & bundles to replenish AOV`,`Blended MER (>3.2x)`],[`Mon 30 Nov`,`Cyber Monday`,`Final push with countdown urgency creative; extended flash deals`,`Checkout completion rate`],[`Tue 1 Dec to Fri 4 Dec`,`Post Cyber Week`,`Last chance extended offers; transition into December gift flows`,`First party customer LTV`]]}},{id:`bfcm-countdown`,h2:`Three weeks out: the BFCM countdown checklist`,content:`Black Friday 2026 falls on Friday 27 November and Cyber Monday on 30 November, so the second week of November is the last point where changes can still be tested before spend peaks. Anything you launch after about 20 November is running on guesswork, because the auction gets more expensive every day and there is no time left to read results.

Work through this list in order this week. Each item is something we check on partner accounts before Thanksgiving week.`,list:[`Confirm tracking: the Meta pixel and Conversions API both fire on purchase, and Events Manager shows a healthy event match quality score. Fix this first; nothing else can be judged without it.`,`Lock the offer: one headline discount or bundle, the same on ads, product pages, email and SMS. Changing the offer mid-week resets everything you have learned.`,`Work out your break-even ROAS at the discounted price, not the full price. A 30 percent discount can push the break-even point up sharply, so set CPA and ROAS floors from the new margin.`,`Finish the creative bank: at least three angles with several hook variations each, plus a static and a video version of the offer, so fatigue never forces you to launch untested ads in Cyber Week.`,`Build the early access list now: email and SMS sign-ups collected in November are the cheapest buyers you will reach on Black Friday.`,`Check the store on a phone: page speed, the offer above the fold, and a checkout that works with the discount applied. Paid traffic exposes every friction point.`,`Agree the budget steps in advance: how much you raise spend each day and the CPA level at which you pull back, so decisions on the day are rules, not panic.`]},{id:`bfcm-last-two-weeks`,h2:`BFCM ads in the last two weeks (14 to 26 Nov)`,content:`In the last two weeks before Black Friday, stop building and start loading: the job is to have every BFCM ad approved, every audience ready and every budget rule written down before Thanksgiving week, because changes made during the peak reset learning when it costs the most.

Use this order, with the dates for 2026.`,list:[`14 to 18 Nov: pick the winners. Keep the offer ads and hooks that already hold CPA in testing, pause the rest, and duplicate the winners into the campaigns you will scale so they collect delivery history now.`,`18 to 22 Nov: submit everything for review. Upload every BFCM ad, including Cyber Monday and last-chance versions, so Meta reviews them before the rush; ad review can take longer as Black Friday gets closer.`,`20 to 24 Nov: warm the early access list. Run the early access offer to past buyers, email and SMS lists and engaged audiences, and send the cold traffic campaigns to the sign-up page rather than straight to a discount.`,`24 to 26 Nov: set the guardrails. Confirm the daily budget steps, the CPA or ROAS level that triggers a pull-back and who checks the account each morning, then leave the structure alone.`,`Every day: watch blended MER from Shopify alongside Meta ROAS, because attribution gets noisier when every channel is pushing the same offer at once.`]},{id:`bfcm-last-minute`,h2:`Last-minute BFCM ads checklist (22 to 26 Nov)`,content:`If Black Friday is less than a week away and the account is not ready, do not try to build everything. With days left, the only changes worth making are the ones that protect margin and keep proven ads delivering. Anything new you launch now enters learning when the auction is most expensive.

Do these in order, and stop when you run out of time.`,list:[`Check purchase tracking today: place a test order and confirm the purchase shows in Events Manager from both the pixel and the Conversions API. If tracking is broken, fix that before touching anything else.`,`Recalculate break-even ROAS at the BFCM price. Put your discounted price, product cost, shipping and fees into a break-even ROAS calculator and write down the ROAS and CPA you must not go below.`,`Use the ads you already have. Add the offer as a text overlay or first frame on your 3 to 5 best evergreen ads instead of filming new ones; proven hooks with a clear offer beat untested BFCM creative.`,`Upload every BFCM, Cyber Monday and last-chance ad now, so it clears review before the weekend.`,`Put the new ads into your existing best campaign or a duplicate of it, not into brand-new campaigns, so they inherit delivery history.`,`Add one cost-capped campaign at your break-even CPA as a safety valve for the days when CPMs spike.`,`Send the offer to email and SMS before ads on Thanksgiving and Black Friday morning: your own list is the cheapest revenue of the weekend.`,`Write the budget rules down: raise budgets about 20% to 30% at a time, and pull back when CPA passes the limit for half a day.`]},{id:`cyber-monday-ads`,h2:`Cyber Monday ads (30 Nov): the final push`,content:`Cyber Monday is a separate buying day, not a quieter Black Friday. Many shoppers who browsed on Friday come back to buy, so the people who visited, added to cart or opened your emails over the weekend are the most valuable audience of the day.

Swap every ad to a Cyber Monday or "ends tonight" version on Sunday evening so nothing still says Black Friday. Lead with the deadline and the exact saving, keep the bundle or quantity offer that held AOV over the weekend, and keep the budget rules unchanged. If you extend the sale, say so clearly from Tuesday 1 December with a "last chance" message, then move to gifting creative with shipping deadlines.`},{id:`phase-1-creative`,h2:`Phase 1 (3 to 13 Nov): Build the creative bank`,content:`During the first two weeks of November, your priority is creative volume over aggressive ad spend. You need 20 to 30 distinct direct response video variations ready before Thanksgiving week.

Key angles to test immediately:

1. Urgency & Limited Supply: Emphasize inventory caps on best selling SKUs.
2. Gift Giving & Bundles: Multi item kits that instantly push your Average Order Value (AOV) above $120.
3. Founder & Behind The Scenes Hooks: Raw, high trust direct to camera explanations of why this is the only major discount of the year.
4. Offer in the First Second: Clear text overlay stating the exact percentage or tiered savings before 0:02.`},{id:`phase-2-early-access`,h2:`Phase 2 (16 to 25 Nov): Early access & warm audiences`,content:`Opening early access to email and SMS subscribers 7 to 10 days before Black Friday unlocks two immense advantages: it captures sales before Meta ad CPMs peak, and it feeds fresh purchase pixel signals to the algorithm.

In our client case studies, scaling early access offers generated $112,866 in 13 days at 3.98x ROAS before Cyber Week officially kicked off. Run teaser ads with direct early access unlock hooks to build high intent retargeting pools.`},{id:`phase-3-scale`,h2:`Phase 3 (26 to 30 Nov): Scale without burning margin`,content:`During peak Cyber Week, manual micro adjustments will kill ad delivery. Structure your ad account with two core engines:

Advantage+ Shopping Campaign (ASC): Allow Meta to dynamically allocate budget to your top 5 to 10 validated creative assets across broad demographics.

Cost Controlled Scaling Campaign: Duplicate top performing ad sets with bid caps and cost caps set at your target break even CPA to absorb high intent volume without overspending.

In our client accounts, this combination allowed a women's fashion brand to scale $16,630 in spend into $59,241 in revenue (3.56x ROAS) during the 4 day peak.`},{id:`winning-offers`,h2:`BFCM offers that work on Meta`,content:`Flat sitewide discounts often hurt net profit margins. Structuring tiered offers protects your contribution margin while increasing cart sizes:`,table:{headers:[`Offer Architecture`,`Best Suited For`,`Margin Impact`,`Real-World Example`],rows:[[`Tiered Spend & Save`,`Stores with wide catalog & variable prices`,`Low to Moderate`,`Spend $100 get $20 off, spend $200 get $50 off`],[`Curated Bundles`,`Skincare, supplements, apparel essentials`,`Lowest (Preserves Margin)`,`Buy 3 Step Routine, get free full size toner ($135 value)`],[`Quantity Breaks`,`Single hero SKU stores, consumables`,`Low`,`Buy 2 get 10% off, Buy 3 get 25% off + free shipping`],[`Gift With Purchase (GWP)`,`Luxury goods, jewelry, high AOV brands`,`Very Low`,`Free travel leather pouch on orders over $150`]]}},{id:`ad-patterns`,h2:`What the best BFCM ads have in common`,content:`Analyzing hundreds of winning Black Friday campaigns from the Meta Ads Library reveals 6 non negotiable patterns:

Offer in the first 2 seconds: State the savings immediately in heavy contrast text overlay.

Clear expiration date: Specify Sunday midnight or Cyber Monday end date to enforce genuine scarcity.

Product demonstrated in use: High clarity UGC showing unboxing, application, or fit within 5 seconds.

Social proof overlay: Trust badges (10,000+ 5 Star Reviews, As Seen in Vogue).

Clear price anchoring: Show the regular price crossed out next to the bundle price.

Single unambiguous CTA: Direct users to Shop the Black Friday Sale with no confusing multi links.`},{id:`bfcm-ad-strategy`,h2:`BFCM ad strategy: the campaign structure that holds CPA`,content:`Most BFCM accounts break in the same way: ten new campaigns launched on Thanksgiving, all in learning, all competing for the same people at the most expensive CPMs of the year. The structure below is the one we run for Shopify clients, and it is built before the peak so nothing important is learning on Black Friday.`,subsections:[{h3:`1. One Advantage+ sales campaign for scale`,content:`Your proven evergreen ads plus the 5 to 10 best BFCM creatives from Phase 1, broad targeting, one budget. This campaign takes most of the peak budget because it has the most purchase data behind it.`},{h3:`2. One cost-capped or bid-capped campaign for margin`,content:`Duplicate your best ad sets with a cost cap at your break even CPA. When CPMs spike, Meta simply spends less here instead of buying expensive, unprofitable sales. It is the safety valve that protects contribution margin.`},{h3:`3. Catalog retargeting, kept small`,content:`Dynamic product ads for site visitors and cart abandoners from the last 30 days, at roughly 10% to 15% of spend. Show the BFCM offer, not the product at full price.`},{h3:`4. Email and SMS in sync with ads`,content:`Your early access list from Phase 2 is the cheapest revenue of the weekend. Send the offer to email and SMS first, then let Meta reach the people who did not open. Exclude recent purchasers from prospecting so you do not pay to reach your own customers.`}]},{id:`bfcm-budget-plan`,h2:`BFCM budget plan: how to split spend across November`,content:`Budget follows the calendar, not the other way round. This is how we usually split a brand's November Meta budget; adjust the shares to your margins and inventory. Never raise a campaign's budget by more than about 20% to 30% in one step, even on Black Friday, or it re-enters learning at the worst moment.`,table:{headers:[`Window`,`Share of November budget`,`What the money buys`],rows:[[`3 to 13 Nov`,`10% to 15%`,`Creative testing at low budgets, finding the 5 to 10 winners`],[`16 to 25 Nov`,`20% to 25%`,`Early access offers, list building, warming audiences`],[`26 to 30 Nov (Thanksgiving to Cyber Monday)`,`45% to 55%`,`Scaling proven ads in the Advantage+ and cost-capped campaigns`],[`1 to 4 Dec`,`10% to 15%`,`Extended offers, last chance creative, December gifting`]]}},{id:`bfcm-retargeting`,h2:`Retargeting during Black Friday without burning budget`,content:`Retargeting looks brilliant in Ads Manager during BFCM because it gets credit for people who were going to buy anyway. Keep it honest:`,list:[`Cap retargeting at about 10% to 15% of total spend and judge it on blended MER, not platform ROAS.`,`Use catalog ads for visitors and cart abandoners from the last 7 to 30 days; skip ad sets for 1 to 3 day windows that are too small to deliver.`,`Change the message, not just the audience: lead with the deadline and the bundle, because these people already know the product.`,`Exclude buyers from the last 30 days, and anyone who purchased during early access.`]},{id:`bfcm-creative-checklist`,h2:`BFCM creative checklist`,content:`Before Thanksgiving, every ad in the scale campaign should pass this list.`,list:[`The offer is readable in the first 2 seconds, with sound off.`,`The deadline is explicit: "ends Cyber Monday" or a date.`,`The product is shown in use, not just on a white background.`,`Social proof is on screen: review count, rating or a customer clip.`,`The regular price is shown next to the BFCM price.`,`One call to action, pointing to a landing page that repeats the same offer.`,`At least 5 genuinely different concepts are live, so Meta has variety to choose from when CPMs rise.`]},{id:`bfcm-results`,h2:`BFCM and Q4 results from our client accounts`,content:`These are published results from Brand Scaling client accounts during Q4 and BFCM. Each has its own case study with the dashboard proof.`,table:{headers:[`Brand`,`Result`,`Window`,`Detail`],rows:[[`Dropshipping brand, early BFCM offers`,`$112,866`,`13 days`,`$28,391 Meta ad spend, about 3.98x ROAS`],[`Women's fashion brand`,`$59,241`,`BFCM peak`,`$16,630 spend, 3.56x ROAS`],[`Dropshipping brand, Q4`,`$133,420`,`7 days`,`$32,857 spend, 4.06x ROAS`],[`Skincare brand, Q4`,`$146,184`,`27 days`,`3.64x ROAS, 957 orders`]]}},{id:`after-bfcm`,h2:`After Cyber Monday: keep the momentum into December`,content:`The week after Cyber Monday is usually cheaper than the peak and full of warm buyers. Run a short last chance or extended offer from 1 to 4 December, then switch creative to gifting with shipping deadlines. Move the best BFCM ads back to full price with the gifting angle; the concept still works, only the offer changes. Finally, segment everyone who bought during BFCM for a post-purchase flow: a second order in December or January is what turns a BFCM sale into profitable lifetime value.`},{id:`bfcm-mistakes`,h2:`BFCM mistakes that cost the most`,content:`The expensive mistakes are almost always structural, not creative.`,list:[`Launching new campaigns on Thanksgiving, so they are still learning when sales peak.`,`Doubling or tripling budgets overnight instead of stepping them up 20% to 30%.`,`Sitewide discounts on low margin products that make every order unprofitable after fees.`,`Running out of the hero product mid-weekend because inventory was not planned against ad spend.`,`Reading platform ROAS instead of blended MER, and over-crediting retargeting.`,`No cost controls, so the account buys expensive sales when CPMs spike.`]}],faqs:[{question:`Is it too late to run Black Friday ads?`,answer:`Not if you use what already works. In the last week, add the BFCM offer to your best existing ads, upload Black Friday and Cyber Monday versions so they clear review, add a cost-capped campaign at your break-even CPA, and send the offer to email and SMS first. Avoid launching brand-new campaigns on Thanksgiving.`},{question:`What should Cyber Monday ads say?`,answer:`Lead with the deadline and the exact saving, for example "ends tonight" and the discount or bundle price, and focus on people who visited or added to cart over the weekend. Swap all Black Friday wording to Cyber Monday on Sunday evening.`},{question:`When is BFCM 2026?`,answer:`Black Friday is Friday 27 November 2026 and Cyber Monday is Monday 30 November 2026. Most high performing Shopify brands run offers across the whole Cyber Week beginning Thursday 26 November (Thanksgiving).`},{question:`How long should a Shopify brand run BFCM offers?`,answer:`The core peak event spans 5 days from Thanksgiving through Cyber Monday. However, top scaling brands typically launch VIP early access 7 to 10 days earlier and extend last chance offers through the first week of December.`},{question:`When should I start Black Friday ads on Meta?`,answer:`Start producing and testing creatives in the first half of November. Launch early access campaigns mid month, and allocate 60% or more of your monthly budget between Thanksgiving and Cyber Monday.`},{question:`What is the best campaign structure for Meta BFCM ads?`,answer:`A simplified structure consisting of one consolidated Advantage+ Shopping Campaign for broad scale combined with one cost capped or bid capped manual sales campaign to protect net profit margins during peak auction volatility.`},{question:`What is a good ROAS during Black Friday on Meta?`,answer:`While blended ROAS varies by margin, healthy DTC accounts aim for 3.2x to 4.5x blended ROAS during Cyber Week, supported by higher AOV bundles that absorb seasonal CPM surges.`},{question:`What is the best BFCM ad strategy for Meta?`,answer:`Build creative and audiences in early November, open early access offers 7 to 10 days before Thanksgiving, and during the peak scale one Advantage+ sales campaign plus one cost-capped campaign, with catalog retargeting at about 10% to 15% of spend.`},{question:`How much should I increase my ad budget for Black Friday?`,answer:`Plan to spend about 45% to 55% of your November budget between Thanksgiving and Cyber Monday, but raise each campaign's budget in steps of about 20% to 30% so it does not re-enter the learning phase.`},{question:`Should I run retargeting ads during BFCM?`,answer:`Yes, but keep it to about 10% to 15% of spend with catalog ads, exclude recent buyers, and judge it on blended MER, because retargeting takes credit for many sales that would have happened anyway.`}]},{slug:`what-is-roas`,title:`What Is ROAS? Formula, Good ROAS Benchmarks & Break Even Calculator for Ecommerce`,titleTag:`What Is ROAS? Formula, Meaning & Examples | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`What is ROAS? The ROAS formula, worked examples, how to find your break-even ROAS and how ROAS differs from ROI and MER for ecommerce brands.`,primaryKeyword:`what is roas`,secondaryKeywords:[`good roas ecommerce`,`roas formula`,`break even roas`,`roas vs mer`],publishDate:`2026-10-24`,updatedDate:`2026-11-03`,readTime:`13 min read`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/roas-calculator`,title:`Free ROAS Calculator`,text:`Enter price, COGS and CAC to get break-even ROAS and profit per order.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`Accounts run between 3x and 6x ROAS at scale, with $50M+ managed.`},{href:`/case-studies`,title:`150+ Verified Case Studies`,text:`Real ROAS figures from client ad accounts.`},{href:`/blog/meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda Explained`,text:`Why platform ROAS and blended MER now diverge.`}],category:`Metrics & Unit Economics`,author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`ROAS stands for Return On Ad Spend. It is calculated by dividing total attributed revenue from an advertising campaign by the total ad spend (ROAS = Revenue divided by Ad Spend). A 4.0x ROAS means your business generates $4 in revenue for every $1 spent on ads. A good ROAS depends entirely on your product gross margin: stores with 60% margins break even at 1.67x ROAS, while stores with 40% margins need 2.5x ROAS just to break even.`,definitionBox:{term:`ROAS (Return On Ad Spend)`,definition:`A primary ecommerce marketing efficiency metric showing gross revenue generated per dollar invested into advertising channels like Meta, TikTok, or Google.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`roas-definition`,text:`What is ROAS and how is it calculated?`},{id:`break-even-formula`,text:`How to calculate your break even ROAS`},{id:`industry-benchmarks`,text:`What is a good ROAS? Benchmarks by industry`},{id:`roas-vs-mer`,text:`ROAS vs. MER: Which metric should you scale on?`},{id:`how-to-improve-roas`,text:`How to improve ecommerce ROAS`},{id:`roas-meaning`,text:`ROAS meaning: what does ROAS stand for?`},{id:`roas-formula-examples`,text:`ROAS formula with worked examples`},{id:`average-roas`,text:`What is an average ROAS?`},{id:`roas-vs-roi`,text:`ROAS vs ROI`},{id:`target-roas`,text:`How to set a target ROAS`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`roas-definition`,h2:`What is ROAS and how is it calculated?`,content:`Return on Ad Spend (ROAS) is the baseline metric direct to consumer (DTC) brands and media buyers use to determine the immediate revenue performance of paid advertising campaigns.

The ROAS Formula:
ROAS = Attributed Revenue / Total Ad Spend

For instance, if your Meta Ads campaign spends $5,000 in a month and generates $20,000 in tracked Shopify orders:
ROAS = $20,000 / $5,000 = 4.00x (or 400%)

While platform ROAS is straightforward to monitor inside Meta Ads Manager or Google Ads, scaling a profitable brand requires looking beyond platform metrics to calculate your net contribution margin.`},{id:`break-even-formula`,h2:`How to calculate your break even ROAS`,content:`Many brand founders make the fatal mistake of celebrating a 2.5x ROAS on Meta when their unit economics actually mean they are losing money on every order.

Your Break Even ROAS is the minimum return required so that your revenue covers the cost of goods sold (COGS), payment processing, picking & packing, and shipping:

Break Even ROAS = 1 / Gross Margin %

Use our Free Interactive ROAS Calculator to test your exact margins, COGS, and shipping costs.`},{id:`industry-benchmarks`,h2:`What is a good ROAS? Benchmarks by industry`,content:`There is no universal good ROAS that applies equally to every brand. A luxury jewelry brand with an 85% gross margin can comfortably scale at a 2.2x blended ROAS, while a custom mechanical keyboard store with a 45% margin would run out of cash at the same return.

Based on managing over $50M in ad spend across 150+ DTC brands, here are real world baseline benchmarks for paid Meta and TikTok ads:

Beauty & Skincare: 3.4x to 5.2x ROAS
Fashion & Apparel: 2.8x to 4.2x ROAS
Health & Supplements: 3.2x to 4.8x ROAS (backed by subscription recurring LTV)
Home & Kitchen: 2.9x to 4.1x ROAS
B2B & High Ticket Services: 4.5x to 7.0x+ ROAS`},{id:`roas-vs-mer`,h2:`ROAS vs. MER: Which metric should you scale on?`,content:`Post iOS 14.5 and modern browser cookie restrictions, platform reported ROAS inside Meta and TikTok suffers from signal loss, attribution delays, and cross device drop off.

Top tier direct response growth teams scale based on MER (Marketing Efficiency Ratio):
MER = Total Shopify Net Sales / Blended Ad Spend Across All Channels

If platform ROAS drops slightly on Meta because customers view the ad and later purchase via Google search or direct type in, your MER reveals the true unmodeled health of your acquisition engine.`},{id:`how-to-improve-roas`,h2:`How to improve ecommerce ROAS: 5 proven ways`,content:`1. Engineered Multi Angle Creative Testing: Continually deploy 10 to 20 net new hook iterations weekly. Explore our Viral Creatives Vault for 58+ live direct response examples.

2. Post Purchase One Click Upsells: Adding a seamless post purchase upsell typically lifts Average Order Value by 18% to 28% without increasing ad spend by a single dollar.

3. Sub 1s Mobile Landing Page Optimization: Every second of mobile load delay reduces conversion rates by 7%. Optimize your Shopify theme to achieve sub 1.2s mobile speed index.

4. Broad Audience Advantage+ Consolidation: Eliminate micro targeted ad sets that trigger auction bidding wars and audience overlap.

5. Tiered Quantity Breaks: Encourage shoppers to purchase 2 or 3 items with clear value incentives such as Buy 2 Save 15%.`},{id:`roas-meaning`,h2:`ROAS meaning: what does ROAS stand for?`,content:`ROAS stands for Return On Ad Spend. It answers one question: for every dollar spent on ads, how many dollars of revenue came back? It is written as a ratio (4x, or 4:1) or sometimes as a percentage (400%).

ROAS measures revenue, not profit. A 4x ROAS means $4 of sales for every $1 of ads, before product cost, shipping, payment fees and returns are taken out. That is why the same ROAS can be very profitable for one store and loss-making for another.`},{id:`roas-formula-examples`,h2:`ROAS formula with worked examples`,content:`ROAS = revenue attributed to ads / ad spend. Use the same date range and the same attribution setting for both numbers.`,table:{headers:[`Ad spend`,`Attributed revenue`,`ROAS`,`What it means`],rows:[[`$1,000`,`$2,000`,`2.0x`,`$2 back per $1; break even only at 50% gross margin`],[`$5,000`,`$20,000`,`4.0x`,`$4 back per $1; healthy for most DTC brands`],[`$40,124`,`$146,184`,`3.64x`,`A real skincare client account over 27 days`],[`$28,391`,`$112,866`,`3.98x`,`A real early BFCM result over 13 days`]]}},{id:`average-roas`,h2:`What is an average ROAS?`,content:`There is no single average, because ROAS depends on margin, price point, channel and how attribution is counted. As a working reference, healthy ecommerce accounts we see usually land in these ranges once they are past the testing phase. Treat them as orientation, not targets: your own break even ROAS is the only number that decides whether an account is profitable.`,table:{headers:[`Channel`,`Typical ROAS range we see`,`Why it differs`],rows:[[`Meta prospecting (cold traffic)`,`2x to 4x`,`Finds new customers; most of the growth`],[`Meta retargeting`,`5x to 10x+`,`Over-credited; many buyers would have purchased anyway`],[`Google brand search`,`8x to 20x+`,`Captures people already searching for you`],[`Google Shopping and Performance Max`,`3x to 6x`,`Mix of new and returning demand`],[`Blended MER (all channels)`,`3x to 5x`,`Total revenue / total ad spend; the honest number`]]}},{id:`roas-vs-roi`,h2:`ROAS vs ROI: what is the difference?`,content:`ROAS looks at ad spend and revenue only. ROI (return on investment) looks at profit after all costs. A campaign can have a 3x ROAS and a negative ROI if product cost, shipping and fees take more than two thirds of the revenue.

Use ROAS to compare ads and campaigns day to day, because it is fast and every ad platform reports it. Use ROI, or contribution margin after ad spend, to decide whether to keep scaling. Our free ROAS calculator shows both: break even ROAS and profit per order.`},{id:`target-roas`,h2:`How to set a target ROAS`,content:`Start from margin, not from a benchmark. Break even ROAS = 1 / gross margin. Then decide how much profit each order must leave after ads.`,list:[`Gross margin 60%: break even ROAS 1.67x; a target of about 2.5x leaves room for profit.`,`Gross margin 50%: break even ROAS 2.0x; target about 3x.`,`Gross margin 40%: break even ROAS 2.5x; target about 3.5x to 4x, or raise order value with bundles first.`,`Subscription or high repeat purchase brands can accept a lower first-order target because lifetime value pays it back.`]}],faqs:[{question:`What is ROAS in digital marketing?`,answer:`ROAS stands for Return On Ad Spend. It measures the revenue generated for every dollar spent on paid advertising campaigns.`},{question:`What is a good ROAS for ecommerce brands?`,answer:`For most DTC ecommerce stores with 60% to 75% gross margins, an attributed ROAS between 3.0x and 4.5x represents healthy, sustainable scaling profit.`},{question:`How do I calculate break even ROAS?`,answer:`Break even ROAS is calculated as 1 divided by your Gross Margin percentage (1 / Gross Margin %). If your margin is 65% (0.65), your break even ROAS is 1 / 0.65 = 1.54x.`},{question:`Is higher ROAS always better?`,answer:`Not necessarily. Maximizing ROAS often restricts ad spend to small retargeting audiences. High growth DTC brands intentionally scale ad spend at a stable, profitable target ROAS to maximize total net contribution dollars.`},{question:`What does ROAS mean?`,answer:`ROAS means Return On Ad Spend: the revenue your ads generate for every dollar spent on them. A 4x ROAS means $4 of revenue for each $1 of ad spend.`},{question:`What is the ROAS formula?`,answer:`ROAS = revenue attributed to ads divided by ad spend. For example, $20,000 in revenue from $5,000 of ads is a 4x ROAS.`},{question:`What is an average ROAS for ecommerce?`,answer:`Healthy ecommerce accounts usually run a blended 3x to 5x, but there is no universal average. What matters is staying above your own break even ROAS, which is 1 divided by your gross margin.`},{question:`What is the difference between ROAS and ROI?`,answer:`ROAS compares revenue to ad spend only. ROI compares profit to all costs. A campaign can have a good ROAS and still lose money if product, shipping and fees are high.`}]},{slug:`performance-marketing-agency`,title:`DTC Performance Marketing Agency: What It Does and How to Choose One`,titleTag:`DTC Performance Marketing Agency Explained | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`What a DTC performance marketing agency does: Meta, TikTok and Google ads, creative, CRO and margin-based reporting, plus how to choose one.`,primaryKeyword:`performance marketing agency`,secondaryKeywords:[`ecommerce marketing agency usa`,`dtc growth agency`,`direct response marketing agency`,`meta ads agency`],publishDate:`2026-10-26`,updatedDate:`2026-11-07`,readTime:`14 min read`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/`,title:`DTC Marketing and Ecommerce Growth Agency`,text:`What the Brand Scaling engagement includes and who it is for.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency`,text:`The Facebook and Instagram arm of the engine.`},{href:`/growth`,title:`Ecommerce Growth Engine`,text:`Media buying, creative, CRO and retention in one system.`},{href:`/case-studies`,title:`150+ Verified Case Studies`,text:`Proof from real ad accounts across 30+ niches.`},{href:`/meta-ads-audit`,title:`Meta Ads Audit`,text:`Find out why ROAS is slipping before you hire anyone.`},{href:`/cro-agency`,title:`Ecommerce CRO Agency`,text:`Raise revenue per visitor so paid traffic pays back faster.`},{href:`/tiktok-shop-agency`,title:`TikTok Shop Agency`,text:`Affiliates, GMV Max and Shop ads for DTC brands.`},{href:`/google-shopping-ads-agency`,title:`Google Shopping Ads Agency`,text:`Capture the search demand your Meta ads create.`}],category:`Strategy & Scaling`,author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`A DTC performance marketing agency runs paid acquisition for brands that sell direct to consumers and is paid to grow revenue, not impressions. More broadly, a performance marketing agency is a growth firm that drives direct, trackable commercial outcomes, such as verified revenue, ROAS, customer acquisition, and qualified leads, rather than abstract vanity metrics like impressions or follower counts. In modern DTC ecommerce, high tier performance agencies combine algorithmic media buying across Meta and TikTok with in house direct response creative production and Shopify conversion rate optimization.`,definitionBox:{term:`Performance Marketing Agency`,definition:`A specialized digital agency where client compensation, strategy, and daily campaign execution are directly anchored to measurable financial results (ROAS, CPA, MER, Net Margin).`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-is-performance-marketing`,text:`What does a performance marketing agency actually do?`},{id:`traditional-vs-performance`,text:`Traditional marketing agencies vs. performance agencies`},{id:`core-pillars`,text:`The 4 pillars of modern DTC performance marketing`},{id:`key-metrics`,text:`The metrics that matter: Beyond vanity ROAS`},{id:`how-to-choose`,text:`How to vet and choose the right agency for your brand`},{id:`choose-ecommerce-growth-agency`,text:`How to choose an ecommerce growth agency`},{id:`dtc-performance-marketing-channels`,text:`DTC performance marketing channels`},{id:`google-ads-agency-capabilities`,text:`What a Google Ads agency for DTC should do`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-is-performance-marketing`,h2:`What does a performance marketing agency actually do?`,content:`Unlike traditional branding agencies that focus on awareness campaigns, billboards, and social media aesthetic grids, a performance marketing agency is engineered around one singular objective: predictable, profitable customer acquisition.

Every dollar invested into paid ad channels (Meta, TikTok, Google Search, and YouTube) is mapped directly to conversions, customer acquisition cost (CPA), and net contribution margin. 

At Brand Scaling, managing over $50M in paid advertising across 150+ Shopify stores has proven that media buying alone no longer scales brands. Modern performance marketing is a tightly integrated triad:

1. Direct Response Creative Engineering: Scripting, shooting, and editing 15 to 30 vertical video variations monthly.
2. Algorithmic Media Buying: Deploying broad targeting Advantage+ architectures with strict bid controls.
3. Full Funnel Conversion Optimization: Engineering landing pages, bundle upsells, and sub 1s load times.`},{id:`traditional-vs-performance`,h2:`Traditional marketing agencies vs. performance agencies`,content:`Understanding the distinction between traditional creative agencies and performance driven growth partners is critical for brand founders looking to scale beyond $100K/month:`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Traditional Creative Agency`,`Performance Marketing Agency`],rows:[[`Primary KPI`,`Brand awareness, impressions, reach, social engagement`,`Net revenue, blended ROAS, CPA, and customer LTV`],[`Creative Approach`,`Polished, cinema style brand films produced over months`,`Rapid UGC, native TikTok hooks, and weekly iterative testing`],[`Channel Mastery`,`Broad media placements, PR, and billboard takeovers`,`Algorithmic Meta Ads, TikTok Shop, Google PMax, and CAPI`],[`Reporting Cadence`,`Monthly slide decks with vanity metrics`,`Daily live dashboards, blended MER tracking, and Slack comms`],[`Accountability`,`Paid regardless of whether the campaign drives sales`,`Retainers and scale incentives tied to performance targets`]]}},{id:`core-pillars`,h2:`The 4 pillars of modern DTC performance marketing`,content:`High Converting Direct Response Video Creatives:
In modern Meta and TikTok advertising, creative is your targeting. Winning agencies do not rely on generic stock video; they analyze second by second drop off rates and engineer distinct hooks (problem agitation, founder origin, side by side comparison, social proof UGC). Explore our 58+ Vertical Video Ad Winners to see this in practice.

Full Funnel Shopify Conversion Rate Optimization (CRO):
Scaling ad spend without optimizing on page conversion is like pouring water into a leaky bucket. Performance agencies optimize product display pages (PDPs), introduce 1 click bundle upsells, and streamline mobile checkout flows to maximize Average Order Value (AOV).

Server Side Data Tracking & Conversions API (CAPI):
With browser tracking restrictions, resilient server to server tracking via Meta Conversions API and first party pixels is mandatory to feed high fidelity purchase signals back to advertising algorithms.

Mathematical Unit Economics & Profit Protection:
Scaling spend without understanding break even metrics leads to bankruptcy. Top performance teams model contribution margin, blended MER, and return on capital before scaling daily budgets.`},{id:`key-metrics`,h2:`The metrics that matter: Beyond vanity ROAS`,content:`A true performance partner monitors full funnel unit economics:

Blended MER (Marketing Efficiency Ratio): Total revenue divided by total marketing expenditure across all platforms.

Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC): The exact cost to acquire a first time purchasing customer.

Contribution Margin (CM): Net profit remaining after subtracting COGS, ad spend, shipping, and merchant processing fees.

First Time vs. Returning Customer Split: Ensuring that paid acquisition fuels sustainable long term business equity.`},{id:`how-to-choose`,h2:`How to vet and choose the right agency for your brand`,content:`When hiring a performance marketing agency, ask these critical vetting questions:

1. Can I see live screen recordings of ad dashboards, not just screenshots? (Inspect our 150+ Verified Case Studies for real client dashboard recordings).

2. Do you produce the video creative in house, or do I have to provide the videos?

3. What is your strategy for creative fatigue once a winning ad stops performing?

4. How do you protect net profit margins when ad auction CPMs fluctuate?`},{id:`choose-ecommerce-growth-agency`,h2:`How to choose an ecommerce growth agency that specializes in scaling DTC brands`,content:`Ecommerce growth agencies that specialize in scaling DTC brands look similar on their websites: the same platform logos, the same ROAS screenshots. The difference shows up in how the work is set up. A specialist runs creative, media buying, the store and retention as one system, because a DTC brand rarely stalls for a single reason. Spend stops scaling when creative fatigues, when the product page cannot convert colder traffic, or when first-order margin cannot cover the cost of a new customer.

So judge an agency on three things: who makes the ads and how many new ones launch each month, whether they will change the store and the offer rather than only the campaigns, and which number they report first. An agency that leads with blended MER and new-customer CAC is managing your business; one that leads with platform ROAS is managing a dashboard. Ask the questions below on the first call.`,subsections:[{h3:`Questions to ask before you sign`,list:[`Can you show live ad account or Shopify dashboards, not just logos and testimonials?`,`Who produces the creative, and how many new ads a month will we launch?`,`Will you touch the store (product pages, offers, checkout), or only the ads?`,`Which number do you report first: platform ROAS or blended MER and new-customer CAC?`,`Do we own the ad account, pixel, data and creative if we leave?`,`What happens in the first 14 days?`]},{h3:`Red flags`,list:[`Guaranteed ROAS or revenue numbers before they have seen your account.`,`Reporting that only shows platform ROAS and clicks.`,`Ten ad sets of interest targeting and three creatives a month.`,`Long contracts with no performance review points.`]}]},{id:`dtc-performance-marketing-channels`,h2:`DTC performance marketing: which channels do what`,content:`Performance marketing for a DTC brand is not one channel. Each one has a different job, and a good agency budgets for the job, not for the channel.`,table:{headers:[`Channel`,`Main job`,`When it leads`],rows:[[`Meta ads (Facebook, Instagram)`,`Finding new customers at scale with creative`,`Most DTC brands from $20K to $2M+ a month`],[`TikTok ads and TikTok Shop`,`Reach and creator-led discovery, in-app checkout`,`Visual, demo-friendly and impulse products`],[`Google Search and Shopping`,`Capturing people already searching`,`Brands with search demand or a known category`],[`Email and SMS (Klaviyo)`,`Repeat purchases and lifetime value`,`Every brand once traffic is flowing`],[`Shopify CRO`,`Turning more paid traffic into orders`,`Before scaling any paid channel`]]}},{id:`google-ads-agency-capabilities`,h2:`What capabilities should a Google Ads agency have for DTC ecommerce?`,content:`If Google is part of the mix, the agency should be able to: run a clean Merchant Center feed with optimised titles and images; separate brand search from non-brand so brand traffic does not inflate results; run Performance Max with audience signals from your customer lists; set target ROAS from your margins rather than a default; and report Google alongside Meta on blended MER so the two channels are not both claiming the same sale. For most DTC brands Google captures demand that Meta and TikTok create, so the agency should plan them together.`}],faqs:[{question:`What does a DTC performance marketing agency do?`,answer:`A DTC performance marketing agency plans and runs paid campaigns on Meta, TikTok and Google for brands that sell direct to consumers, and is judged on revenue, new-customer CAC and contribution margin. The strongest ones also produce the ad creative and improve the store, because ads alone cannot fix a weak offer or a slow product page.`},{question:`How do I choose a DTC performance marketing agency?`,answer:`Ask for results from brands at your spend level and in your niche, who produces the creative and how many new ads you get each month, how they report (blended MER and contribution margin, not platform ROAS alone), and who owns the ad accounts. Avoid agencies that cannot show verified results from real ad accounts.`},{question:`What is a performance marketing agency?`,answer:`A performance marketing agency is a specialized digital marketing partner that designs, executes, and scales paid ad campaigns where success is measured by trackable financial returns such as revenue, ROAS, and customer acquisition costs.`},{question:`How do performance marketing agencies charge?`,answer:`Performance agencies typically operate on a base monthly management retainer plus a percentage of ad spend or a performance incentive based on net revenue scaled past an agreed baseline.`},{question:`What is the difference between digital marketing and performance marketing?`,answer:`Digital marketing is an umbrella term covering all online activities, including SEO, organic social, and brand PR. Performance marketing is specifically focused on paid advertising and direct response conversions where every dollar is tracked against revenue generated.`},{question:`When should an ecommerce brand hire a performance marketing agency?`,answer:`Brands should consider a performance agency when they have validated product market fit (typically $20k+/month in revenue) and need a repeatable, scalable creative and media buying engine to break through to $100k to $500k per month.`},{question:`Which agencies are best for scaling DTC ecommerce brands?`,answer:`The best fit is an agency that runs creative, media buying and Shopify conversion work together, shows live dashboards as proof, and reports on blended MER and new-customer CAC rather than platform ROAS alone.`},{question:`What should I look for in an ecommerce growth agency?`,answer:`Live account proof, in-house creative volume of 30 or more new ads a month, willingness to fix the store as well as the ads, full ownership of your accounts and data, and a clear plan for the first 14 days.`},{question:`What capabilities should a Google Ads agency have for DTC ecommerce?`,answer:`A clean Merchant Center feed, brand and non-brand search separated, Performance Max with first-party audience signals, target ROAS set from your margins, and reporting on blended MER alongside Meta.`}]},{slug:`meta-ads-library`,title:`Facebook Ad Library (Meta Ads Library): How We Find Winning Ads and Scaled a DTC Brand to $376K in 25 Days`,titleTag:`Facebook Ad Library (Meta Ads Library): Find Winning Ads`,metaDescription:`How to use the free Facebook Ad Library (Meta Ads Library): find long-running competitor ads, turn them into tests, and how one brand hit $376K in 25 days.`,primaryKeyword:`facebook ad library`,secondaryKeywords:[`meta ads library`,`facebook ads library`,`how to find winning ads`,`meta ads case study`,`scale meta ads`,`cbo campaign`,`how to use meta ads library`,`is meta ads library free`,`meta ad library search by id`],publishDate:`2026-10-30`,updatedDate:`2026-10-31`,readTime:`16 min read`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`Have us run this research, testing and CBO scaling for your brand.`},{href:`/case-studies/skincare-beauty`,title:`Skincare Case Studies`,text:`More skincare and beauty accounts scaled on Meta ads.`},{href:`/roas-calculator`,title:`Free ROAS Calculator`,text:`Set your break-even ROAS and target CPA before you test.`}],category:`Creative Research`,author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`The Meta Ads Library is Meta's free, public database of every active ad on Facebook and Instagram. We use it to find competitor ads that have run for 2 weeks or more, because brands only keep paying for ads that make money. We study why those ads work, test our own versions at $20 per ad set, and move the winners into a CBO campaign. That process took a DTC skincare brand to $376,000 in revenue in 25 days.`,video:{youtubeId:`UjUTcbDwe24`,title:`How We Scaled a DTC Brand to $376K in 25 Days With Meta Ads (Live Breakdown)`,description:`Live breakdown: finding winning ads in the Meta Ads Library, testing audiences and creatives at $20 per ad set, and scaling winners into a CBO campaign for a DTC skincare brand.`,duration:`PT31M35S`,durationSeconds:1895,uploadDate:`2026-10-30`,chapters:[{t:0,label:`$376K in 25 days for a DTC skincare brand`},{t:65,label:`Finding winning ads in the Meta Ads Library`},{t:158,label:`The 5 to 8 second rule for your hook`},{t:248,label:`How long an ad must run to count as a winner`},{t:388,label:`Using Amazon reviews inside your ads`},{t:448,label:`Carousel retargeting: selling routines and bundles`},{t:697,label:`Our DTC Meta ads testing playbook`},{t:780,label:`The 8 audience types we test`},{t:1237,label:`What to test in every ad`},{t:1517,label:`When to kill an ad set`},{t:1615,label:`Scaling winners into a CBO campaign`},{t:1664,label:`When to kill during scaling`}],takeaways:[`An ad that has run for 2 weeks or more is a winner signal. Brands do not keep paying for losing ads.`,`Your ad must make its point in 5 to 8 seconds. Longer explainer ads should stay under 30 to 40 seconds.`,`Test at $20 per ad set, 7 to 10 ad sets, 1 to 2 creatives each, for 3 days.`,`Kill an ad set if CTR is under 1% after a day, CPC is over $1 with no sales, or CPA is 2 to 3 times your target.`,`Move ad sets with CTR above 1.5% and a profitable CPA into one CBO campaign with a budget of 3 to 5 times your target CPA.`]},definitionBox:{term:`Meta Ads Library (Facebook Ads Library)`,definition:`A free transparency tool at facebook.com/ads/library that lists every ad currently running on Facebook, Instagram, Messenger and Audience Network, with the start date, platforms, creative versions and advertiser page. It does not show spend, reach or results for normal commercial ads outside the EU, and you do not need an ad account or a paid spy tool to use it.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-is-meta-ads-library`,text:`What is the Meta Ads Library?`},{id:`find-winning-ads`,text:`How to find winning ads in the Meta Ads Library`},{id:`winner-scorecard`,text:`The winning ad scorecard`},{id:`five-patterns`,text:`Why those ads work: 5 patterns we copied`},{id:`testing-playbook`,text:`Our DTC Meta ads testing playbook`},{id:`audience-types`,text:`The 8 audience types we test`},{id:`what-to-test`,text:`What to test in every ad`},{id:`kpis-and-kill-rules`,text:`KPIs, test setup and when to kill an ad set`},{id:`scaling-cbo`,text:`Scaling winners into a CBO campaign`},{id:`the-result`,text:`The result: $376K in 25 days`},{id:`search-like-a-media-buyer`,text:`Searching the library like a media buyer`},{id:`library-limits`,text:`Downloads, the API and when the library is not working`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-is-meta-ads-library`,h2:`What is the Meta Ads Library?`,content:`The Meta Ads Library (many people still call it the Facebook Ads Library) is a free tool from Meta that shows every ad currently running on Facebook, Instagram, Messenger and Audience Network. You can search by keyword or brand name, filter by country, and see the date each ad started running. You do not need an ad account, a Business Manager or even a Facebook login to use it.

For each ad you see the advertiser's page, the start date, the platforms it runs on and every creative version under the same ad. What you do not see for normal ecommerce ads in the US, UK, Canada or Australia is spend, impressions, CTR or ROAS. That is why media buyers read how long an ad has run, and how many versions a brand keeps live, as the signal for what is making money.

For a DTC brand it is the cheapest market research you can do: it shows you exactly which ads your biggest competitors keep paying for. The official tool lives at facebook.com/ads/library.`},{id:`find-winning-ads`,h2:`How to find winning ads in the Meta Ads Library`,content:`This is the exact process from the video, run for a DTC skincare client selling a vitamin C serum.`,subsections:[{h3:`1. Search your product, not just your competitors`,content:`Open the Meta Ads Library, set the country to where your customers are (for our client, the United States), choose All ads and search for your product category. For this client we searched "vitamin C serum". This shows every brand advertising that product, including ones you did not know about.

If the category is stuck on Issues, elections or politics you will see no ecommerce ads at all. Switching it to All ads is the most common fix when the library looks empty.`},{h3:`2. Keep only ads that have run for 2 weeks or more`,content:`Every ad shows a Started running on date. Two weeks is enough to prove an ad, whether the brand spends $20 or $100 a day on it. If a big brand has kept an ad live for 2 to 3 months, it is profitable. An ad that started 12 days ago is not proven yet, so skip it.

Also check how many ads use the same creative. When a brand runs the same creative in 3 or more ads, it is scaling a winner.`},{h3:`3. Check the brand's own Facebook page too`,content:`One search does not always show everything. Open the brand's Facebook page and its Ad library link to see all of its active ads in one place, then open See ad details to count the versions of each concept and click through to the landing page behind it.`}],table:{headers:[`Filter`,`Best setting for ecommerce research`,`Why`],rows:[[`Country`,`Your main market (US, UK, CA, AU)`,`Ads are localised; the US shows the most volume`],[`Ad category`,`All ads`,`The political category hides every commerce ad`],[`Active status`,`Active`,`Inactive ads include failed tests and old offers`],[`Media type`,`Video first, then image`,`Video carries most DTC spend and shows the hook`],[`Platform`,`Instagram, then Facebook`,`Shows which placements the brand invests in`],[`Date range`,`Last 90 days`,`Separates proven evergreen ads from fresh tests`]]}},{id:`winner-scorecard`,h2:`The winning ad scorecard`,content:`Because the library hides performance data, you infer it. Score every ad you save on these six signals; anything that scores 9 or more out of 12 goes into your next creative brief.`,table:{headers:[`Signal`,`What to check`,`Score 0 to 2`],rows:[[`Run time`,`Started running on date`,`0: under 14 days, 1: 14 to 30 days, 2: 30+ days`],[`Versions`,`See ad details, version count`,`0: one, 1: two to three, 2: four or more`],[`Duplication`,`Same creative under several ads`,`0: none, 1: two, 2: three or more`],[`Brand activity`,`New ads from the page this week`,`0: none, 1: one to two, 2: three or more`],[`Landing page`,`Dedicated page, bundle or offer`,`0: generic product page, 1: offer on page, 2: dedicated page`],[`Comments`,`Engagement on the ad post`,`0: under 20, 1: 20 to 100, 2: 100+`]]}},{id:`five-patterns`,h2:`Why those ads work: 5 patterns we copied`,content:`Finding a winner is half the job. The other half is understanding why it wins, so you copy the structure, not the ad.`,subsections:[{h3:`The message lands in 5 to 8 seconds`,content:`One winning vitamin C serum ad made its whole point in 6 seconds. Your hook has 5 to 8 seconds to stop the scroll and state the benefit. If you need to explain more, keep the full ad under 30 to 40 seconds.`},{h3:`Trust comes first in skincare`,content:`The same ad opened with "recommended by US dermatologists". People are skeptical about putting a product they bought online on their skin. Proof (dermatologist recommended, clinically tested, verified reviews) answers that objection before it forms.`},{h3:`Results without "before and after"`,content:`One winning ad showed hands at day 1 and day 30 without ever saying "before and after". Meta restricts before and after claims for personal health and beauty products, and ads that use them often get flagged. Show the change; do not label it.`},{h3:`Amazon reviews inside the creative`,content:`Many DTC brands also sell on Amazon. One brand put a screenshot of a real Amazon review straight into the ad. Viewers who check Amazon find the same reviews, which removes the last doubt.`},{h3:`Sell the routine, not the single product`,content:`A carousel retargeting ad showed a full skincare routine: cleanser, vitamin C serum, retinol, soothing cream. Selling a routine lifts order value and works well with subscriptions. On our client's landing page we added "clinically recommended: 3 months for best results", so buyers choose the 3 month plan and stay long enough to see results.`}]},{id:`testing-playbook`,h2:`Our DTC Meta ads testing playbook`,content:`Research only pays off when it turns into tests. We test at $20 per ad set. You can go down to $10 or up to $50 depending on your budget; this example assumes a brand that can spend $150 to $200 a day on testing.`,list:[`Objective: conversions, optimised for purchase.`,`$20 per ad set, 7 to 10 ad sets, 1 to 2 creatives per ad set.`,`Run for 3 days. This is the validation phase.`,`Checkpoints over 14 days: day 1, 4, 5, 7, 10 and 14.`]},{id:`audience-types`,h2:`The 8 audience types we test`,subsectionsAfterTable:!0,content:`Each ad set in the test targets one audience type. Combining interests this way keeps each ad set distinct, so Meta is not bidding against itself.`,table:{headers:[`Ad set`,`Who it targets`,`Skincare example`],rows:[[`Magazine`,`Readers of trade, lifestyle and category magazines`,`Beauty and skincare publications`],[`Informational`,`People learning about your category and its problems`,`Skincare blogs and education sites`],[`Category`,`Interest in the product type itself`,`Anti aging cream, cleanser, moisturizer`],[`Passion`,`People passionate about the lifestyle around your product`,`Beauty content consumers`],[`Popular group`,`Followers of well known creators, experts and communities`,`Skincare creators and communities`],[`Seed`,`Built around your use case and ideal customer`,`Sunscreen, serum, premium vs budget buyers`],[`Buyers`,`Pages liked by people who praise competitor ads in the comments`,`Brands found on commenters' profiles`],[`Brand`,`Competing brands in your market`,`Large beauty retailers and cosmetic brands`]]},subsections:[{h3:`How we find proven buyers from comments`,content:`Find a competitor ad with thousands of shares and positive comments. Open the profiles of people who say "I have used this brand for years" and look at the pages they like. Add those brands as interests in Ads Manager. These are people who already buy in your category.`},{h3:`Audience size rules`,list:[`Keep each ad set close to, but under, 5 million people.`,`Combine 2 to 3 interests from one group with 2 to 3 from another, then narrow with your main keyword to hit that size.`,`Do not use the same keyword in more than 3 ad sets.`]}]},{id:`what-to-test`,h2:`What to test in every ad`,content:`Change one element at a time across the ad sets, so you know which change moved the numbers.`,table:{headers:[`Element`,`What to test`],rows:[[`Creative concept`,`Product demo, how to use, customer testimonial, founder story, benefit focus`],[`Hook (first 3 seconds)`,`Bold claim, question, problem statement, visual result`],[`Visuals`,`Close up, product in use, UGC video, lifestyle shot, unboxing`],[`Ad copy`,`Convenience, quality, value and identity angles`],[`Headline`,`Short, and name the offer`],[`Offer`,`15% off first order, bundles, free shipping, buy more save more, 25% off subscription`],[`Call to action`,`Shop now, discover the collection, explore the range`],[`Social proof`,`Verified reviews, Amazon review screenshots`],[`Format`,`UGC video, static image, carousel, collection ad`]]}},{id:`kpis-and-kill-rules`,h2:`KPIs, test setup and when to kill an ad set`,content:`Track the whole funnel, not just ROAS, so you can see where a losing ad set breaks.`,subsections:[{h3:`The KPIs we track`,list:[`Delivery: link clicks, CTR, CPC, CPM, landing page views, amount spent, frequency.`,`Funnel: view content, add to cart and cost per add to cart, initiate checkout, cost per purchase.`,`Revenue: purchase conversion value, ROAS, average order value.`,`Video: 25%, 50%, 75% and 95% watch rates.`]},{h3:`When to kill an ad set`,list:[`CTR under 1% after a full day and $20 spent: people are not responding to the offer.`,`CPC above $1 with no conversions.`,`CPA at 2 to 3 times your target with no improvement.`,`ROAS under your minimum (for this client, 2).`,`Clicks but no view content events: people are not reaching the landing page.`,`No meaningful action by day 3.`]}]},{id:`scaling-cbo`,h2:`Scaling winners into a CBO campaign`,content:`Over the 14 day test you kill losers and keep 2 to 3 performers. Ad sets with CTR above 1.5% and a profitable CPA or positive ROAS move into one CBO (campaign budget optimization) campaign.`,list:[`Put 3 to 5 proven ad sets in the CBO.`,`Set the CBO budget at 3 to 5 times your target CPA. Example: target CPA $20, CBO budget about $100 a day.`,`Keep scaling while the CBO stays profitable.`],subsections:[{h3:`When to pause or adjust the CBO`,list:[`CPA rises above 2 times your target.`,`CTR drops by 1 point: a sign of creative fatigue.`,`ROAS falls below your minimum.`,`Conversions drop on creatives that used to work: launch new iterations of the winning angle.`]}]},{id:`the-result`,h2:`The result: $376K in 25 days`,content:`We used this exact process for a DTC skincare brand selling a vitamin C serum. In 25 days the brand did $376,000 in revenue and sold 2,508 units, around $150 per order. Results are from one client account, shared with permission. They are not typical and not a guarantee.

The method is repeatable even if your numbers are smaller: find ads that have survived 2 weeks, copy why they work rather than what they look like, test at $20 per ad set with clear kill rules, and only scale what has proven itself into a CBO. Need ad creatives to test? Our creative studio, SevenFigureStudio, makes UGC video ads for DTC brands (link below).`,table:{headers:[`Metric`,`Result`],rows:[[`Revenue`,`$376,000`],[`Timeframe`,`25 days`],[`Units sold`,`2,508`],[`Average order value`,`About $150`],[`Product`,`Vitamin C serum (DTC skincare)`],[`Channel`,`Meta ads (Facebook and Instagram)`]]}},{id:`search-like-a-media-buyer`,h2:`Searching the library like a media buyer`,content:`Keyword searches that work for ecommerce. Search for offer language, not just product names: free shipping, bundle, limited drop, buy 2 get 1, 30 day guarantee. Combine with one niche word, for example bundle skincare or free shipping serum. Offer words surface ads that are built to convert.

Meta ad library search by ID. Every ad has a numeric Library ID shown on its card and in its URL. Set the search type to Ad ID and paste it to see that ad with all of its versions. Use this when a teammate or creator sends you a link.

Instagram only. Set the platform filter to Instagram. Vertical 9:16 Reels placements usually mean the brand has a creator led UGC pipeline, which is the fastest production model to copy.

Can you sort by impressions? Not for commercial ads. Sorting by impressions and spend ranges are only available for ads about social issues, elections or politics. Run time plus version count is the closest free proxy.`},{id:`library-limits`,h2:`Downloads, the API and when the library is not working`,content:`Downloading videos. There is no download button. For internal research teams screen record the ad or save it with a browser tool. Keep it in a private swipe file and recreate the structure with your own product and footage; never re-upload a competitor's ad, footage or creators.

The Meta Ad Library API. It is free but limited: it covers ads about social issues, elections and politics plus EU transparency data, and needs a Meta developer account with confirmed identity. For ecommerce competitor research the web tool shows the same commercial ad fields, so most brands do not need the API.

When the library is not working, check these in order: the country is set to your market; the category is All ads; ad blockers and privacy extensions are off (try a private window); the search type matches what you pasted (Keyword vs Ad ID); and if nothing else works, Meta's tool may be down for a short time.`}],faqs:[{question:`Is the Meta Ads Library free?`,answer:`Yes. The Meta Ads Library is free and public. You can search all active Facebook and Instagram ads without logging in or having an ad account.`},{question:`How long should an ad run before it counts as a winner?`,answer:`At least 2 weeks. Brands do not keep paying for ads that lose money, so an ad running for 2 weeks or more, especially one used in several ads, is a strong winner signal.`},{question:`How much should I spend to test Meta ads?`,answer:`We test at $20 per ad set across 7 to 10 ad sets for 3 days. You can start at $10 per ad set on a smaller budget, but you need enough spend for Meta to show results.`},{question:`When should I kill a Meta ad set?`,answer:`Kill it if CTR is under 1% after a day, CPC is above $1 with no sales, or CPA stays at 2 to 3 times your target. If nothing meaningful happens by day 3, turn it off.`},{question:`Can I use before and after images in skincare ads?`,answer:`Meta restricts before and after claims for personal health and beauty products, and these ads often get rejected. Show day 1 and day 30 visually instead of labelling it before and after.`},{question:`Can you see ad spend in the Meta Ads Library?`,answer:`Not for normal commercial ads in the US, UK, Canada or Australia. Spend ranges and impressions are shown only for ads about social issues, elections or politics, and ads delivered in the EU show reach data.`},{question:`Can I search the Meta Ads Library by ad ID?`,answer:`Yes. Set the search type to Ad ID and paste the numeric Library ID from the ad card or its URL to see that ad with all of its versions.`},{question:`What is a CBO campaign in Meta ads?`,answer:`CBO (campaign budget optimization, now called Advantage campaign budget) sets one budget at campaign level and lets Meta move it to the best ad sets. We move 3 to 5 proven ad sets into a CBO with a budget of 3 to 5 times target CPA.`}]},{slug:`meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda Explained: What Changed for Ecommerce Ads`,titleTag:`Meta Andromeda Explained for Ecommerce Ads | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`Meta Andromeda changed how Meta picks which ads to show. What it means for ecommerce: creative diversity, simpler campaign structure and new testing rules.`,primaryKeyword:`meta andromeda`,secondaryKeywords:[`what is meta andromeda`,`how meta andromeda works`,`meta andromeda creative diversity`,`meta andromeda campaign structure`,`meta andromeda vs gem`,`meta andromeda best practices`,`is meta andromeda live`],publishDate:`2026-10-30`,updatedDate:`2026-10-30`,readTime:`10 min read`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/blog/how-to-scale-facebook-ads`,title:`How to scale Meta ads for an 8-figure DTC brand`,text:`Consolidate, step budgets and feed creative so spend grows without CPA blowing up.`},{href:`/meta-ads-agency`,title:`Meta Ads Agency for Ecommerce`,text:`Account structure and creative volume built for Andromeda.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-library`,title:`Meta Ads Library Guide`,text:`Where the new angles for a diverse creative matrix come from.`},{href:`/roas-calculator`,title:`Free ROAS Calculator`,text:`Know your break-even ROAS before adding creative volume.`},{href:`/shopify-agency`,title:`Shopify Agency`,text:`Landing page match and offer work that creative scales into.`}],category:`Meta Ads`,author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`Meta Andromeda is the AI retrieval system Meta uses to pick which ads, out of millions of candidates, could be shown to each person before the final ranking step in the auction. Meta described it on its engineering blog in December 2024. For ecommerce brands it means creative variety now decides reach: fewer, broader campaigns with many genuinely different ads beat narrow targeting and near duplicate creatives.`,definitionBox:{term:`Meta Andromeda`,definition:`Meta's next generation personalized ads retrieval engine, built on custom AI hardware, that narrows the full pool of eligible ads to a short list for each impression. Ranking models (including the GEM model) then choose the final ad and price. Andromeda powers Advantage+ automation and affects every advertiser; there is no opt in or opt out.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`what-is-andromeda`,text:`What is Meta Andromeda?`},{id:`how-it-works`,text:`How Meta Andromeda works (retrieval, then ranking)`},{id:`andromeda-vs-gem`,text:`Meta Andromeda vs GEM`},{id:`creative-diversity`,text:`Meta Andromeda and creative diversity`},{id:`campaign-structure`,text:`Meta Andromeda campaign structure`},{id:`best-practices`,text:`Meta Andromeda best practices for ecommerce`},{id:`what-did-not-change`,text:`What did not change`},{id:`is-it-live`,text:`Is Meta Andromeda live? What it means in 2026`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`what-is-andromeda`,h2:`What is Meta Andromeda?`,content:`Every time someone opens Instagram or Facebook, Meta has a few milliseconds to choose one ad from tens of millions of eligible ads. It cannot score all of them with its heaviest models, so the job is split in two: a retrieval step that quickly finds a few thousand plausible candidates for that specific person, and a ranking step that scores those candidates precisely and runs the auction.

Andromeda is the new retrieval step. Meta announced it in the engineering post Meta Andromeda: Supercharging Advantage+ automation with the next-gen personalized ads retrieval engine on 2 December 2024. The key change is that retrieval itself became a large, personalised AI model running on Meta's own accelerator hardware, instead of a lighter heuristic filter. Meta reported higher ad quality and recall, and tied the system directly to Advantage+ campaign automation.

In plain words: the first cut used to be rough and mostly driven by your targeting settings. Now the first cut is a model that already understands the person well, and it picks the ads whose creative is most likely to interest that person. Your creative is doing the targeting.`},{id:`how-it-works`,h2:`How Meta Andromeda works (retrieval, then ranking)`,content:`Step 1: Retrieval narrows millions of ads to a short list per person. Andromeda looks at the person's signals and at embeddings of every eligible ad (what the creative shows and says, what people who engaged with it look like) and returns the candidates most likely to be relevant. If two of your ads are near duplicates, they land in the same place in that embedding space and effectively compete for one slot in the short list.

Step 2: Ranking models pick the final ad and the price. The retrieved candidates are scored by Meta's ranking models for predicted action rate, combined with the advertiser's bid and the ad quality signals, and the auction decides the winner and the price. This is where cost caps, bid caps and your optimisation event matter.

The practical consequence: an ad that never makes it through retrieval never gets the chance to prove itself in ranking, no matter how good the offer. Getting through retrieval depends on being different enough from your own other ads and relevant enough to a real slice of people.`,table:{headers:[`Stage`,`What happens`,`What you control`],rows:[[`All eligible ads`,`Tens of millions of active ads could be shown`,`Being active, within policy and with the right placements`],[`Retrieval (Andromeda)`,`A personalised model shortlists a few thousand candidates`,`Creative diversity, concept variety, clear hooks`],[`Ranking`,`Heavy models predict the action rate of each candidate`,`Optimisation event, Conversions API signal quality, landing page`],[`Auction`,`Bid times predicted value plus quality picks the winner`,`Budget, bid strategy, cost caps`]]}},{id:`andromeda-vs-gem`,h2:`Meta Andromeda vs GEM`,content:`People mix these up because Meta published both as parts of its ads AI stack. Andromeda is retrieval: it chooses which ads are candidates for a given person. GEM (Generative Ads Recommendation Model) is Meta's large ranking foundation model, described separately by Meta as a transformer based system trained across its ads data that improves how candidates are scored.

A simple way to hold it: Andromeda decides who gets to audition, GEM decides who gets the part. Both reward the same two things from an advertiser: creatives that are genuinely different from each other, and clean conversion signals so the models learn from real purchases rather than noise.`},{id:`creative-diversity`,h2:`Meta Andromeda and creative diversity`,content:`The most visible change media buyers reported after the rollout: accounts with 12 ad sets each holding the same three creatives stopped scaling, while accounts with one or two campaigns and 20 clearly different ads kept growing. That is retrieval at work. Near duplicate ads (same video, new headline; same hook, new background music) are treated as one concept, so adding more of them does not add reach.

What different actually means. A new concept changes at least one of: the hook (the first 3 seconds and the claim), the angle (problem, mechanism, objection, identity, offer), the format (UGC testimonial, founder explainer, demo, split screen, static, carousel), or the person on camera. Changing the headline, the CTA button or the colour grade is an iteration, useful for squeezing a proven concept, but it does not open new reach.

The 45+ hook and angle matrix. For partner brands we brief creatives from a matrix of 5 angles by 3 hook styles by 3 formats. Every cell is a different concept to the retrieval model. A full matrix gives 45 distinct starting points; the weekly job is to fill the empty cells, kill the losers and iterate the winners. This is how accounts receive 30 to 50 new creatives a month without becoming 50 versions of one ad.`},{id:`campaign-structure`,h2:`Meta Andromeda campaign structure`,content:`Consolidate. The structure that works under Andromeda is simpler than what most accounts still run: one Advantage+ sales campaign for the core catalogue with broad targeting, one testing campaign where new concepts launch, and optionally one retargeting campaign at 10% to 15% of spend using catalog ads. Interest stacks, lookalike ladders and geographic splits mostly fragment learning now. In our case library the pattern repeats: consolidating ad sets into simplified broad targeting Advantage+ campaigns is the step that unlocked scale.

When cost caps and bid caps still help. Cost controls do not fight Andromeda; they protect margin once a concept is proven. For a DTC partner we scaled $13,195 in spend into $54,552 in 7 days at 4.43x ROAS by duplicating the winning ad sets with bid caps set 20% higher than the original, which let the winners buy more volume without reopening the test budget.

Before and after. Before: 12 ad sets, 3 creatives each, interest targeting per ad set, 36 ads of which 30 were near duplicates. After: 2 campaigns, broad targeting, 20 distinct concepts plus 10 iterations of the top 3, a weekly refresh. Same spend, and the account exits learning faster because conversions are pooled.`,table:{headers:[`Element`,`Pre-Andromeda habit`,`Andromeda structure`],rows:[[`Campaigns`,`6 to 12 split by audience`,`2 to 3: scale, test, retargeting`],[`Targeting`,`Interests, lookalikes, stacked exclusions`,`Broad with Advantage+ audience`],[`Creatives per campaign`,`3 to 5, mostly iterations`,`15 to 25 distinct concepts`],[`Testing`,`Separate CBO per idea`,`New concepts inside one testing campaign`],[`Bidding`,`Lowest cost everywhere`,`Lowest cost to learn, cost caps to protect margin at scale`],[`Refresh`,`When fatigue shows`,`Weekly, from a hook and angle matrix`]]}},{id:`best-practices`,h2:`Meta Andromeda best practices for ecommerce`,content:`1. Ship creative volume weekly. Aim for 8 to 12 new ads a week at $10K+ monthly spend, 30 to 50 a month at scale. Fatigue is now a reach problem, not just a frequency problem.

2. Split concepts from iterations. Each week, two thirds new concepts (new hook, angle, format or person), one third iterations of last week's winners.

3. Test inside one campaign. Launch new concepts in a single testing campaign with broad targeting so Meta compares them against each other on equal footing, then move winners into the scale campaign.

4. Match the landing page to the ad. The hook on the video must continue above the fold on the page; a dedicated landing page per angle lifts conversion rate and tells the ranking models the click was a good one.

5. Fix signal quality first. Conversions API with event match quality of 8 or higher, deduplicated with the pixel, so purchases feed the models rather than add to cart noise.

6. Give it patience windows. Judge a concept after 3 to 5 days or 2 to 3 times target CPA in spend, whichever comes first; do not edit live ad sets daily.

7. Read results on MER, not platform ROAS. Blended marketing efficiency ratio and new customer CAC catch the double counting that broad campaigns and retargeting produce in platform reports.

8. Refresh on a calendar. Put the Monday swipe file review and the Tuesday brief in the calendar; accounts that refresh by schedule rather than by panic have the flattest CPA curves.`},{id:`what-did-not-change`,h2:`What did not change`,content:`Andromeda changed delivery, not economics. The offer, the product, the landing page and your margins still decide whether scale is profitable. A 4x ROAS on a 40% margin product still loses money after returns and fees; a 2.5x ROAS on a 75% margin bundle can fund aggressive growth.

Before you add creative volume, know your break even ROAS (1 divided by gross margin) and your target CAC. Our free ROAS calculator gives you both in under a minute, and the Shopify agency page covers the product page, bundle and checkout fixes that raise the ceiling creative can scale into.`},{id:`is-it-live`,h2:`Is Meta Andromeda live? What it means in 2026`,content:`Yes. Meta stated in its December 2024 engineering post that Andromeda powers ads retrieval for Advantage+ automation, and performance patterns across our partner accounts changed in the way the post predicts: consolidated, creatively diverse accounts scale; fragmented, duplicate heavy accounts stall. Every advertiser is affected; there is no setting to turn it on or off.

What we can say with confidence as of 30 October 2026: creative diversity is the primary scaling lever, broad consolidated structures outperform segmented ones for most DTC brands, and cost controls remain useful for margin protection. What we avoid claiming: specific thresholds Meta has not published (an exact number of creatives, a precise similarity cut off). When Meta publishes more detail we update this page and change the date above.

If your account was built in 2022 and has not been restructured since, the fastest check is a Brand Growth Audit: we map your current structure against the Andromeda layout in the table above and show the creative gaps in your matrix before any spend changes.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is the best Meta Andromeda targeting for premium ecommerce brands?`,answer:`Broad targeting with a wide range of genuinely different creatives. For premium products such as furniture, the creative does the targeting: show the product in real homes, at its real price point, with angles for different buyers, and let Andromeda match each ad to the people most likely to buy.`},{question:`How do I find Meta Andromeda ad experts in the United States?`,answer:`Look for an agency that can show recent results from accounts restructured for Andromeda: fewer, broader campaigns, high creative volume and reporting on blended MER. Brand Scaling is a New York DTC agency that runs Meta accounts this way and publishes verified case studies from client ad accounts.`},{question:`What is Meta Andromeda?`,answer:`Meta Andromeda is Meta's ads retrieval system. It chooses which ads could be shown to each person before the ranking models pick the final ad. Meta introduced it on its engineering blog in December 2024 as the retrieval engine behind Advantage+ automation.`},{question:`Is Meta Andromeda live?`,answer:`Yes. Meta has said Andromeda powers ads retrieval for Advantage+ automation. Every advertiser is affected, and there is no way to opt in or out.`},{question:`What is the best campaign structure for Meta Andromeda?`,answer:`For most ecommerce brands: two or three campaigns (scale, test and a small retargeting campaign), broad targeting, and many clearly different creatives inside each campaign, with cost caps only where margins need protecting.`},{question:`Does Meta Andromeda mean targeting no longer matters?`,answer:`Interest targeting matters far less than before. Your creative now does most of the targeting, because different hooks, angles and formats are retrieved for different people.`},{question:`How many creatives do I need for Meta Andromeda?`,answer:`Enough to test new concepts every week: roughly 8 to 12 new ads a week at $10K+ in monthly spend. Brand Scaling ships 30 to 50+ angle iterations a month for partner brands so accounts do not fatigue.`},{question:`What is the difference between Meta Andromeda and GEM?`,answer:`Andromeda is the retrieval stage that shortlists candidate ads for a person. GEM is Meta's large ranking model that scores those candidates. Both reward creative diversity and clean conversion signals.`}]},{slug:`facebook-ads-for-shopify`,title:`Facebook Ads for Shopify: The 2026 Setup`,titleTag:`Facebook Ads for Shopify: The 2026 Setup | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`Facebook ads for Shopify, set up right: connect the Facebook and Instagram app, pixel and Conversions API, first campaign, budget and creative plan.`,primaryKeyword:`facebook ads for shopify`,secondaryKeywords:[`how to run facebook ads for shopify store`,`how to set up facebook ads for shopify`,`how to connect facebook ads to shopify`,`facebook ads pixel shopify`,`how much are facebook ads for shopify`,`facebook ads library shopify`],publishDate:`2026-11-02`,updatedDate:`2026-11-02`,readTime:`12 min read`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/shopify-agency`,title:`Shopify Agency & CRO`,text:`Product pages, bundles and checkout built for paid Meta traffic.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-library`,title:`Meta Ads Library: $376K Case Study`,text:`How to find winning ads to copy before you brief creative.`},{href:`/blog/meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda Explained`,text:`Why 5 to 10 genuinely different creatives beat narrow targeting.`},{href:`/roas-calculator`,title:`Free ROAS Calculator`,text:`Work out break-even ROAS and target CPA before you set a budget.`}],category:`Meta Ads`,author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`To run Facebook ads for a Shopify store in 2026, connect Shopify's Facebook and Instagram app to your Meta Business account, turn on the pixel and Conversions API with maximum data sharing, verify your domain, then launch one Advantage+ sales campaign with 5 to 10 different creatives. Start with a daily budget you can hold for 7 days without touching it, and judge results on cost per purchase, not clicks.`,definitionBox:{term:`Facebook ads for Shopify`,definition:`Paid campaigns on Facebook and Instagram, bought through Meta Ads Manager, that send shoppers to a Shopify store and are optimised for purchases. Shopify's Facebook and Instagram app connects the store's product catalog, pixel and Conversions API so Meta can see who bought and find more people like them.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`before-you-start`,text:`Before you start: what you need`},{id:`connect-facebook-ads-to-shopify`,text:`How to connect Facebook ads to Shopify`},{id:`pixel-tracking`,text:`Facebook ads pixel for Shopify: check your tracking`},{id:`first-campaign`,text:`Your first campaign`},{id:`how-much`,text:`How much are Facebook ads for Shopify?`},{id:`creative`,text:`Creative that sells on Shopify`},{id:`reading-results`,text:`Reading results and scaling`},{id:`common-mistakes`,text:`Common mistakes`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`before-you-start`,h2:`Before you start: what you need`,content:`Most broken Shopify ad accounts were broken on day one: a pixel installed twice, a personal ad account, a catalog that never synced. Fifteen minutes on this checklist saves weeks of bad data.`,list:[`A Meta Business account (business.facebook.com) that you own, not one owned by a freelancer.`,`An ad account inside that Business account, with a payment method and the correct currency and time zone. Currency cannot be changed later.`,`A Facebook page and an Instagram professional account for the brand, both added to the Business account.`,`Admin access to the Shopify store.`,`A clean product catalog in Shopify: titles, prices, images and stock that you would be happy to show in an ad.`,`Your store domain ready to verify (you will need access to DNS or the ability to upload a file or meta tag).`]},{id:`connect-facebook-ads-to-shopify`,h2:`How to connect Facebook ads to Shopify`,content:`The connection runs through one Shopify app. It replaces the old method of pasting pixel code into your theme, and it is the only setup we recommend for Shopify stores.`,subsections:[{h3:`1. Install the Facebook and Instagram app`,content:`In Shopify admin, open the Shopify App Store and install the Facebook and Instagram app published by Meta. If an old pixel snippet is still pasted in your theme code, remove it first, or every event will be counted twice.`},{h3:`2. Connect your business assets`,content:`The app walks you through choosing the Business account, ad account, Facebook page, Instagram account and pixel (dataset). Pick the assets owned by your Business account. If you see assets you do not recognise, stop and clean up access in Business Settings first.`},{h3:`3. Set data sharing to maximum`,content:`The app offers several data sharing levels. Choose the maximum level. This turns on the Conversions API, which sends purchase events from Shopify's servers to Meta as well as from the browser. Browser-only tracking misses a share of purchases because of ad blockers and privacy settings; server events fill that gap, and Meta deduplicates the two.`},{h3:`4. Verify your domain and sync the catalog`,content:`In Meta Business Settings, add and verify your store domain. Then confirm that the product catalog has synced from Shopify and that products show as approved. A synced catalog unlocks Advantage+ catalog ads and dynamic retargeting later.`}]},{id:`pixel-tracking`,h2:`Facebook ads pixel for Shopify: check your tracking`,content:`Do not spend a dollar until these five events fire correctly. Open Events Manager, choose your dataset and use Test events while you browse your store in another tab and place a test order (or use a 100% discount code).

If Purchase fires twice per order, you still have an old pixel in the theme or a second app sending events. If Purchase does not fire at all, the checkout is not connected: re-run the app setup. Then check Event Match Quality in Events Manager; aim for 8 or higher on Purchase, which usually means email and phone are being passed from checkout.`,table:{headers:[`Event`,`When it fires`,`What to check`],rows:[[`PageView`,`Every page load`,`Fires once per page, from both browser and server`],[`ViewContent`,`Product page view`,`Includes product ID and price`],[`AddToCart`,`Add to cart click`,`Value and currency match the product`],[`InitiateCheckout`,`Checkout starts`,`Fires on Shopify checkout, not only the cart page`],[`Purchase`,`Order confirmed`,`Exactly one per order, correct value, deduplicated`]]}},{id:`first-campaign`,h2:`How to run Facebook ads for a Shopify store: your first campaign`,content:`Keep the first campaign simple. Complexity is the most common reason new Shopify accounts never leave the learning phase.`,subsections:[{h3:`Campaign: Advantage+ sales, broad targeting`,content:`Create a sales campaign optimised for Purchase. For most Shopify stores with working tracking, let Advantage+ handle audiences: broad targeting in your selling countries, no stacked interests. Run one campaign, not five. If you want a controlled test, run a single manual sales campaign with broad targeting next to it.`},{h3:`Creative: 5 to 10 genuinely different concepts`,content:`Meta now uses your creative to decide who sees the ad, so variety matters more than targeting. Launch 5 to 10 different concepts: a customer testimonial, a product demo, a founder video, a problem and solution ad, a comparison, a static offer ad. Five versions of the same video with new headlines count as one concept. Our guide to Meta Andromeda explains why.`},{h3:`Landing page: match the ad`,content:`Send each ad to the page that continues its promise. A single hero product goes to its product page; a bundle offer goes to a bundle page; a problem-led ad goes to a landing page that opens with the same problem. If the product page loads slowly on mobile or hides the price and reviews below the fold, fix that before scaling; it is the work our Shopify agency does first on every account.`}]},{id:`how-much`,h2:`How much are Facebook ads for Shopify?`,content:`There is no fixed price; you set the budget and Meta's auction sets the cost per result. What matters is whether the budget buys enough purchases for Meta to learn. The aim is about 50 purchases a week per ad set or campaign, so the formula is: daily budget = target CPA x 50 / 7.

That is the aim, not the minimum. The honest minimum for testing is a daily budget of 2 to 3 times your target cost per purchase, held for 7 days. Below that, results swing too much to read. Work out your target CPA from your margins first with the free ROAS calculator: break-even ROAS is 1 divided by your gross margin.`,table:{headers:[`Target cost per purchase`,`Testing minimum (2 to 3x CPA per day)`,`Learning aim (CPA x 50 / 7 per day)`],rows:[[`$20`,`$40 to $60`,`about $143`],[`$30`,`$60 to $90`,`about $214`],[`$40`,`$80 to $120`,`about $286`],[`$60`,`$120 to $180`,`about $429`]]}},{id:`creative`,h2:`Creative that sells on Shopify`,content:`Creative does most of the work on Meta now. The patterns that keep winning for our Shopify clients:`,list:[`A hook that makes the point in the first 5 to 8 seconds; the whole ad under 30 to 40 seconds.`,`UGC: real customers or creators using the product, filmed on a phone, not a studio.`,`Founder videos: why the product exists, said by the person who made it.`,`Proof on screen: reviews, ratings, "dermatologist recommended" style trust signals, and Amazon review screenshots if you sell there too.`,`An offer the viewer understands instantly: bundle, free shipping threshold, or first order discount.`],subsections:[{h3:`Research competitors in the Facebook ads library`,content:`Before briefing creative, search your product category and your competitors in the Meta Ads Library (often called the Facebook ads library). Ads that have run for 2 weeks or more are paying for themselves; study why they work and brief your own versions. Our Meta Ads Library case study shows the full process that took a skincare brand to $376K in 25 days.`}]},{id:`reading-results`,h2:`Reading results and scaling`,content:`Give a new campaign 7 days before big changes. Then read it on cost per purchase and blended MER (total revenue divided by total ad spend), not on clicks or platform ROAS alone.`,subsections:[{h3:`When to kill, iterate or scale`,list:[`Kill an ad that has spent 2 to 3 times your target CPA with no purchase, or has CTR under 1% after a full day.`,`Iterate on ads with good CTR but weak conversion: test a new offer or landing page, not a new audience.`,`Scale a campaign that holds your target CPA for 3 to 4 days by raising budget about 20% at a time, or duplicate the winners into a CBO campaign with a budget of 3 to 5 times target CPA.`]},{h3:`What this looks like on a real account`,content:`For a fashion apparel brand, $2,210 in ad spend turned into $12,496 in sales in a single day once the winning creative, a clear offer and cost controls were in place. The full breakdown is in our fashion and jewelry case studies.`}]},{id:`common-mistakes`,h2:`Common mistakes`,content:`These come straight from the accounts we audit most weeks.`,list:[`Audience overlap: several ad sets targeting similar interests bid against each other and push your costs up. Check overlap in Ads Manager and consolidate.`,`Too many ad sets for the budget: each one needs purchases to learn. One campaign with 5 to 10 creatives beats ten starved ad sets.`,`Retargeting overload: separate retargeting campaigns at high frequency burn budget on people who would have bought anyway. Keep retargeting to about 10% to 15% of spend.`,`Manual bids on small budgets: bid caps and cost caps need volume. With a daily budget under 5 times your CPA, Meta often will not spend at all.`,`Judging ads after one day: daily results swing. Decide on 3 to 7 days of data unless an ad is clearly failing the kill rules.`,`Duplicate pixels: a theme snippet plus the app doubles every purchase and makes ROAS look twice as good as it is.`]}],faqs:[{question:`How do I connect Facebook ads to Shopify?`,answer:`Install the Facebook and Instagram app in Shopify, connect your Meta Business account, ad account, page and pixel, and set data sharing to maximum so the Conversions API sends purchase events from Shopify's servers.`},{question:`How much should I spend on Facebook ads for my Shopify store?`,answer:`Spend enough to get steady purchase data every week. Start testing with a daily budget of about 2 to 3 times your target cost per purchase for 7 days, and aim for roughly 50 purchases a week per campaign as you scale.`},{question:`Do Facebook ads still work for Shopify stores?`,answer:`Yes. Brand Scaling clients still scale on Meta, for example a fashion brand that turned $2,210 in ad spend into $12,496 in sales in one day, but creative volume and tracking quality matter more than before.`},{question:`Should I use Advantage+ sales campaigns for Shopify?`,answer:`For most Shopify brands with working tracking and enough creative, yes. Keep a manual campaign only for controlled tests or cost-capped scaling.`},{question:`Do I still need to add the Facebook pixel code to my Shopify theme?`,answer:`No. The Facebook and Instagram app installs the pixel and Conversions API for you. Pasting the pixel into the theme as well will double count your events.`},{question:`Can I see my competitors' Shopify ads?`,answer:`Yes. Search the brand or product in the Meta Ads Library to see all of its active Facebook and Instagram ads and the date each one started running.`}]},{slug:`dtc-affiliate-program`,title:`How to Scale a DTC Affiliate Program in 2026: Creators, Commissions and TikTok Shop`,titleTag:`How to Scale a DTC Affiliate Program in 2026 | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`How to scale a DTC affiliate program: commission structures, recruiting creators, TikTok Shop affiliates, partnership ads, tracking and a 90-day plan.`,primaryKeyword:`scale affiliate program dtc`,secondaryKeywords:[`dtc affiliate program scaling`,`ecommerce affiliate marketing scaling challenges`,`shopify affiliate program`,`tiktok shop affiliate program`,`creator affiliate program`,`affiliate commission rates ecommerce`],publishDate:`2026-11-03`,updatedDate:`2026-11-03`,readTime:`12 min read`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/tiktok-shop-agency`,title:`TikTok Shop Agency`,text:`We run TikTok Shop listings, affiliate creators, Spark Ads and GMV Max for DTC brands.`},{href:`/viral-creatives`,title:`Direct-Response Creatives & UGC`,text:`The creative engine that turns affiliate videos into paid ads.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-library`,title:`Meta Ads Library: $376K Case Study`,text:`Find the creator ads your competitors keep paying for.`},{href:`/roas-calculator`,title:`Free ROAS Calculator`,text:`Check what commission you can afford at your margins.`}],category:`Growth Channels`,author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`To scale a DTC affiliate program, treat affiliates as a creator content engine rather than a coupon channel. Set a commission your margins can carry (usually 10% to 20% of order value), recruit creators who already make content in your niche, give them samples and a clear brief, track every sale with unique links or codes, and put paid budget behind the best affiliate videos as partnership ads or Spark Ads. Programs stall when they recruit coupon sites, pay commissions they cannot afford, or never amplify the content that works.`,definitionBox:{term:`DTC affiliate program`,definition:`A partnership where creators, publishers or customers promote a direct-to-consumer brand and earn a commission on the sales they drive, tracked through unique links or discount codes. On TikTok Shop the same model runs inside the app, with commissions set per product.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`why-programs-stall`,text:`Why DTC affiliate programs stop scaling`},{id:`commission-structure`,text:`Commission structures that scale`},{id:`recruit-creators`,text:`How to recruit affiliates who actually sell`},{id:`tiktok-shop-affiliates`,text:`TikTok Shop affiliates`},{id:`amplify-with-ads`,text:`Amplify affiliate content with paid ads`},{id:`tracking-and-metrics`,text:`Tracking and the metrics that matter`},{id:`protect-margin`,text:`Protecting margin and stopping fraud`},{id:`ninety-day-plan`,text:`A 90-day plan to scale your program`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`why-programs-stall`,h2:`Why DTC affiliate programs stop scaling`,content:`Most ecommerce affiliate programs grow fast for a few months and then flatten. The scaling challenges are almost always the same:`,list:[`Coupon and deal sites take the commission on sales that would have happened anyway, so the program grows on paper but adds no new customers.`,`A handful of affiliates drive most of the revenue, and nobody is recruiting the next wave.`,`Commissions were set to attract affiliates, not to fit the margin, so scaling the program loses money.`,`Affiliates get a link and nothing else: no samples, no brief, no examples of what converts.`,`The best affiliate content is posted once and disappears, instead of being turned into ads.`,`Tracking breaks across devices and apps, so the brand cannot tell which partners actually drive sales.`]},{id:`commission-structure`,h2:`Commission structures that scale`,content:`Start from what one new customer is worth to you, not from what competitors pay. Use the free ROAS calculator to see your margin per order; the commission plus any discount must leave profit after product cost, shipping and fees. Then pick a structure that rewards the behaviour you want.`,table:{headers:[`Structure`,`How it works`,`Best for`],rows:[[`Flat percentage`,`10% to 20% of order value on every sale`,`Simple programs, most DTC brands starting out`],[`Tiered`,`Rate rises at sales thresholds (for example 12%, then 15%, then 20%)`,`Motivating mid-tier creators to keep posting`],[`New customer bonus`,`Higher rate or a fixed bonus for first orders only`,`Brands that want growth, not repeat-buyer cannibalisation`],[`Flat fee plus commission`,`A fee per video plus a lower commission`,`Recruiting proven creators who will not work on commission alone`],[`Product seeding only`,`Free product, no fee, commission on sales`,`Building a large base of smaller creators`]]}},{id:`recruit-creators`,h2:`How to recruit affiliates who actually sell`,content:`The affiliates who scale a DTC brand are creators who already talk to your buyer, not coupon sites. Recruit where your customers already watch.`,subsections:[{h3:`Start with your own customers`,content:`Customers who post about you unprompted convert best, because their content is already genuine. Invite reviewers, repeat buyers and people who tag you on Instagram or TikTok to join first.`},{h3:`Find creators through ads that already work`,content:`Search your category in the Meta Ads Library and on TikTok to see which creators competitors keep paying to promote. Creators whose videos keep running as ads for weeks are proven sellers in your niche.`},{h3:`Give every affiliate a kit`,list:[`Free product, shipped fast.`,`A one-page brief: the problem the product solves, three hooks that work, what not to claim.`,`Two or three example videos from your best affiliates or UGC ads.`,`Their unique link and code, and how the commission is paid.`]}]},{id:`tiktok-shop-affiliates`,h2:`TikTok Shop affiliates`,content:`If your product sells on impulse and demos well on video, TikTok Shop is the fastest way to scale affiliates today. Creators add your product to their showcase, post videos and lives with a shoppable link, and earn commission inside the app, so there is no link tracking to break.

Set an open commission that any creator can join plus targeted collaborations with higher rates for creators you choose. Seed samples generously; most TikTok Shop affiliates will not post without trying the product. Then run the best affiliate videos as ads with GMV Max or Spark Ads. This is the core of the TikTok Shop scaling services we run for DTC brands.`},{id:`amplify-with-ads`,h2:`Amplify affiliate content with paid ads`,content:`The biggest unlock in most programs is paid amplification. An affiliate video that sells organically will usually sell harder with budget behind it, and it looks native because it runs from the creator's own handle.`,list:[`Meta partnership ads: run the creator's post as an ad from their account and yours, with their name and social proof attached.`,`TikTok Spark Ads: boost the creator's original video, keeping its likes and comments.`,`Recut winners: take the hook of a winning affiliate video and brief new UGC variations around it.`,`Pay creators a usage fee for ad rights so you can run their content for 30 to 90 days.`]},{id:`tracking-and-metrics`,h2:`Tracking and the metrics that matter`,content:`Track every affiliate with a unique link and a unique discount code, because many sales start on a phone app and finish on a laptop. Shopify apps such as Shopify Collabs or a dedicated affiliate platform handle links, codes and payouts. Then judge the program on new customers, not total sales.`,table:{headers:[`Metric`,`Why it matters`],rows:[[`New customer share`,`Sales from first-time buyers; coupon traffic is mostly existing customers`],[`Revenue per active affiliate`,`Shows whether you are recruiting the right creators`],[`Active affiliate rate`,`Share of affiliates who posted in the last 30 days`],[`Cost per acquisition`,`Commission plus discount plus product seeding, per new customer`],[`Blended MER`,`Total revenue / total marketing spend, so affiliates and ads are judged together`]]}},{id:`protect-margin`,h2:`Protecting margin and stopping fraud`,content:`Scaling an affiliate program should not scale your losses.`,list:[`Pay commission on net revenue, after refunds and returns, with a 30 day hold.`,`Do not stack a large discount code on top of a high commission.`,`Block brand-name bidding by affiliates in your terms, so they do not buy your own search traffic.`,`Exclude existing customers or pay a lower rate on repeat orders.`,`Review the top 10 affiliates every month for unusual refund rates or traffic sources.`]},{id:`ninety-day-plan`,h2:`A 90-day plan to scale your program`,content:`This is the order we work in when we take over a DTC affiliate or creator program.`,table:{headers:[`Days`,`Focus`,`Output`],rows:[[`1 to 14`,`Economics and setup`,`Commission set from margin, tracking live, affiliate kit ready`],[`15 to 45`,`Recruiting and seeding`,`30 to 50 creators seeded, first wave of content posted`],[`45 to 75`,`Amplification`,`Best 5 to 10 affiliate videos running as partnership ads or Spark Ads`],[`75 to 90`,`Scale and prune`,`Tiered rates for top creators, inactive affiliates replaced, monthly recruiting cadence`]]}}],faqs:[{question:`How do you scale an affiliate program for a DTC brand?`,answer:`Set a commission your margins can carry, recruit creators who already reach your buyers, give them samples and a brief, track every sale with unique links and codes, and run the best affiliate videos as partnership ads or Spark Ads.`},{question:`What commission should a DTC affiliate program pay?`,answer:`Most DTC brands pay 10% to 20% of order value. Work back from your margin per order so the commission plus any discount still leaves profit, and consider a higher rate only for new customers.`},{question:`What are the biggest challenges in scaling ecommerce affiliate marketing?`,answer:`Coupon sites taking credit for existing customers, reliance on a few top affiliates, commissions that do not fit the margin, affiliates without samples or briefs, and broken tracking across devices.`},{question:`Is TikTok Shop good for affiliate marketing?`,answer:`Yes, for products that demo well and sell on impulse. Creators earn commission inside the app with no link tracking to break, and the best affiliate videos can be scaled as ads.`},{question:`Should I run affiliate content as ads?`,answer:`Yes. Paying for usage rights and running winning affiliate videos as Meta partnership ads or TikTok Spark Ads is usually the fastest way to scale a program, because the content is already proven.`}]},{slug:`scale-ugc-production`,title:`How DTC Brands Scale UGC Production: 30 to 50 Ads a Month Without Losing Quality`,titleTag:`How to Scale UGC Production for DTC Brands | Brand Scaling`,metaDescription:`How DTC brands scale UGC production to 30 to 50 ads a month: creator sourcing, briefs, a hook and angle matrix, editing, testing, usage rights and costs.`,primaryKeyword:`how can dtc brands scale ugc production`,secondaryKeywords:[`scale ugc production`,`ugc ads for dtc brands`,`ugc creator brief`,`how many ad creatives per month`,`ugc video ads cost`,`creative testing meta ads`],publishDate:`2026-11-05`,updatedDate:`2026-11-05`,readTime:`13 min read`,relatedLinks:[{href:`/viral-creatives`,title:`Direct-Response Creatives & UGC`,text:`58+ live hooks from the creative engine described in this guide.`},{href:`/blog/meta-ads-library`,title:`Meta Ads Library: $376K Case Study`,text:`Where the angles in your UGC briefs should come from.`},{href:`/blog/meta-andromeda`,title:`Meta Andromeda Explained`,text:`Why creative variety now decides how far an account scales.`},{href:`/blog/dtc-affiliate-program`,title:`Scale a DTC Affiliate Program`,text:`Turn affiliate creators into a second source of UGC.`}],category:`Creative Production`,author:{name:`Gaurav Kapoor`,title:`Founder, Brand Scaling`,avatar:`/team/gaurav.webp`,credentials:`12+ years in ecommerce, $50M+ in Meta ad spend managed, 150+ published case studies`,profileUrl:`/about`,youtubeUrl:`https://www.youtube.com/@gauravecom`,instagramUrl:`https://www.instagram.com/gauravecomm/`,linkedinUrl:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gauravecom/`},directAnswer:`DTC brands scale UGC production by turning it into a weekly system instead of one-off creator orders. Build a hook and angle matrix from ads that already work, brief a bench of 10 to 20 creators against it, film raw footage in batches, and let an editor cut each shoot into several hooks and formats. Test new concepts every week inside one campaign, iterate the winners and retire losers fast. Done this way, a brand can ship 30 to 50 genuinely different ads a month at a steady cost per asset.`,definitionBox:{term:`UGC (user-generated content) ads`,definition:`Ads built from videos filmed by customers or paid creators on a phone, in a natural, non-studio style: testimonials, unboxings, demos and reactions. In DTC advertising "UGC" usually means paid creator content made to look and feel like a real customer post.`},tableOfContents:[{id:`why-ugc-volume`,text:`Why UGC volume decides how far you scale`},{id:`how-many-ads`,text:`How many UGC ads a month do you need?`},{id:`angle-matrix`,text:`Start with a hook and angle matrix, not creators`},{id:`creator-bench`,text:`Build a creator bench`},{id:`ugc-brief`,text:`The UGC brief that gets usable footage`},{id:`batch-and-edit`,text:`Film in batches, multiply in the edit`},{id:`test-and-iterate`,text:`Testing and iterating UGC ads`},{id:`costs-and-rights`,text:`UGC costs and usage rights`},{id:`weekly-system`,text:`The weekly UGC production system`},{id:`faq-section`,text:`Frequently asked questions`}],sections:[{id:`why-ugc-volume`,h2:`Why UGC volume decides how far you scale`,content:`On Meta and TikTok the creative now does most of the targeting. Since Meta's Andromeda retrieval update, near-duplicate ads compete for the same people, while genuinely different ads each open a new slice of the audience. That makes creative volume, and creative variety, the main ceiling on how far an account can scale.

UGC is the format that scales best for most DTC brands: it is cheaper per asset than studio production, it looks native in the feed, and the same raw footage can be cut many ways. The brands that win are not the ones with the best single video; they are the ones that can produce a steady stream of new concepts every week.`},{id:`how-many-ads`,h2:`How many UGC ads a month do you need?`,content:`Volume should follow spend. Too few new ads and the account fatigues; too many and the budget is spread across ads that never get enough data. These are the ranges we plan around for partner brands.`,table:{headers:[`Monthly Meta spend`,`New ads per month`,`New concepts vs iterations`],rows:[[`$5K to $15K`,`8 to 15`,`About half new concepts`],[`$15K to $50K`,`15 to 30`,`Two thirds new concepts`],[`$50K to $150K`,`30 to 50`,`Two thirds new concepts, the rest iterations of winners`],[`$150K+`,`50+`,`Dedicated weekly concept sprints plus winner iterations`]]}},{id:`angle-matrix`,h2:`Start with a hook and angle matrix, not creators`,content:`The most common UGC mistake is hiring creators first and deciding what they should say later. Decide the angles first. We brief UGC from a 45+ cell matrix: 5 angles x 3 hook styles x 3 formats. Every cell is one distinct concept, so a full matrix gives 45 starting points that Meta treats as genuinely different ads.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Options`,`Example for a skincare serum`],rows:[[`Angle`,`Problem, mechanism, objection, identity, offer`,`"Why your vitamin C turns orange" (mechanism)`],[`Hook style`,`Question, bold claim, visual pattern break`,`Close-up of day 1 vs day 30 skin, no words`],[`Format`,`Testimonial, demo, founder, split screen, static`,`Creator applying the serum on camera`]]},subsections:[{h3:`Where the angles come from`,content:`Search your category in the Meta Ads Library and keep the ads competitors have run for two weeks or more; those angles are already proven. Add the questions and objections from your own reviews and customer support inbox. That gives you a matrix grounded in what your market already responds to.`}]},{id:`creator-bench`,h2:`Build a creator bench`,content:`Scaling means never depending on one creator. Keep a bench of 10 to 20 creators who match your buyers by age, look and voice, so you can brief several shoots a week.`,list:[`Your own customers first: reviewers and repeat buyers make the most believable UGC.`,`Creator marketplaces and platforms for paid UGC creators, filtered by niche and past brand work.`,`Affiliate creators: people already posting about you can be paid for ad-ready footage.`,`A mix of faces and settings, so the account does not show the same person in every ad.`,`A short paid test shoot before adding anyone to the bench, judged on energy, clarity and delivery.`]},{id:`ugc-brief`,h2:`The UGC brief that gets usable footage`,content:`A good brief gets you footage you can cut ten ways. A vague brief gets you one long video you cannot use. Every brief we send fits on one page:`,list:[`The angle and the single belief the ad must change.`,`Three to five hook lines to film separately, each delivered in under 3 seconds.`,`The proof shots needed: product in hand, application, result, packaging, price on screen.`,`What not to say: claims that break ad policy, such as medical promises or labelled "before and after".`,`Technical rules: vertical 9:16, natural light, phone camera, no music, several takes of each line.`,`Two example ads that show the tone, ideally your own past winners.`]},{id:`batch-and-edit`,h2:`Film in batches, multiply in the edit`,content:`The cheapest way to scale UGC is to get more ads from every shoot. Ask each creator for raw clips, not a finished edit: several hooks, the body of the message, proof shots and a call to action. An editor then combines them.

One shoot with 5 hooks, 2 bodies and 2 calls to action can produce 10 or more ads, and the hooks alone can be tested against each other. Add on-screen text, captions and a clear offer in the first 2 seconds; most people watch with the sound off. Keep the editing light: UGC that looks over-produced loses the trust that makes it work.`},{id:`test-and-iterate`,h2:`Testing and iterating UGC ads`,content:`Production only pays off when testing is disciplined. We launch new concepts every week inside one testing campaign with broad targeting, so Meta compares them fairly, and judge them on cost per purchase, not views or likes.`,list:[`Give each new concept 3 to 5 days, or 2 to 3 times your target CPA in spend, before deciding.`,`Kill ads with CTR under 1% after a full day, or CPA at 2 to 3 times target.`,`For winners, film new hooks on the same body first: the hook is usually what fatigues.`,`Move proven ads into the scale campaign and keep a weekly refresh so the account never relies on one video.`,`Log every test in a sheet (angle, hook, format, creator, result) so the matrix learns what works.`]},{id:`costs-and-rights`,h2:`UGC costs and usage rights`,content:`Costs vary widely by creator experience, deliverables and usage rights, so budget per usable ad rather than per video. Raw footage plus in-house editing is usually far cheaper per finished ad than paying creators for polished edits.

Always agree usage rights in writing before the shoot: how long you can run the content as paid ads (commonly 30, 60 or 90 days, or perpetual), on which platforms, and whether you can run it from the creator's own handle as Meta partnership ads or TikTok Spark Ads. Running from the creator's handle often performs better because the ad keeps their name and social proof. Use our free ROAS calculator to check how much creative cost per order your margins can carry.`},{id:`weekly-system`,h2:`The weekly UGC production system`,content:`This is the cadence that lets partner brands ship 30 to 50 new ads a month without the team guessing.`,table:{headers:[`Day`,`Step`,`Output`],rows:[[`Monday`,`Research and results review`,`Last week's winners and losers logged; new angles from the Ads Library and reviews`],[`Tuesday`,`Briefs`,`4 to 8 one-page briefs sent to the creator bench`],[`Wednesday to Thursday`,`Filming and editing`,`Raw clips back; editor cuts hooks, bodies and formats`],[`Friday`,`Launch`,`8 to 12 new ads live in the testing campaign`],[`Following week`,`Decide`,`Winners to the scale campaign, iterations briefed, losers off`]]}}],faqs:[{question:`How can DTC brands scale UGC production?`,answer:`Turn UGC into a weekly system: build a hook and angle matrix from proven ads, brief a bench of 10 to 20 creators against it, film raw clips in batches, cut each shoot into several hooks and formats, and test new concepts every week.`},{question:`How many UGC ads does a DTC brand need per month?`,answer:`It depends on spend. Brands spending $15K to $50K a month on Meta usually need 15 to 30 new ads a month; at $50K to $150K, 30 to 50, with about two thirds being genuinely new concepts.`},{question:`What should a UGC creator brief include?`,answer:`The angle and the belief the ad must change, three to five hook lines to film separately, the proof shots needed, what not to claim, technical rules such as vertical 9:16 and natural light, and two example ads.`},{question:`Should I buy finished UGC edits or raw footage?`,answer:`Raw footage plus in-house or agency editing usually gives more ads per shoot and a lower cost per finished ad, because each set of clips can be combined into several hooks and formats.`},{question:`Do I need usage rights for UGC ads?`,answer:`Yes. Agree in writing how long and where you can run the content as paid ads, and whether you can run it from the creator's handle as Meta partnership ads or TikTok Spark Ads.`}]}],E={"what-is-roas":g,"performance-marketing-agency":x,"dtc-affiliate-program":w},D=[...T.map(e=>{let t=E[e.slug];return t?{...e,...typeof t==`function`?t(e):t}:e}),...t,...r,...a,...s,...l,...d,...f,...p,...h];export{D as t};